      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>Dr. Pin Pills.</head>
        <p>Dr. Pin Pills.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Mr. E. T. Wilson, acting Under Sheriff, and a fury I sat at Preston, on Wednesday, to assess damages in an extraordinary action for libel by telegrams brought ; against John M`Nally, Dyche-street, Manchester, by Mary Toole. trading as James Toole, hide and skin I dealer, Allen-street, Warrington and Winwick. Defendant did not appear. Mr. Madden, barrister, for the plaintiff, explained that inJanuary plaintiff's son purchased a consignment of rabbit skins from M'Nally, who was a marine store dealer. They arrived at plaintiff's warehouse on January 22nd, whereupon a cheque was sent to M'Nally for £l7 us. 64. The cheque was crossed, and was on the Warrington branch of the Union Bank On the 24th M`Nally sent the following telegram : "Cheque no use." Of course, said council, if defendant lsad any brains at all he would have known he could not get a crossed cheque paid at a Manchester bank, and ought to have paid it away or into the bank. So defendant next day went to Wari:ngton, being very abusive saying he "would expose the swindle all over the plac Edward Toole, one of plaintiff's sons who carry on the business, thereupon exchanged the crosied cheque for an open cheque. Defendant went away satisfied and presented this at Manchester, although he knew he should have presented it at Warrington. About eleven next morning another telegram was received from defendant saying " There is a warrant for your arrest." Toole was very much annoyed and upset, and in the afternoon another telegram came, "Cheque dishonoured three times, reply." Asa matter of fact, the next day M'Sally went to Warrington and the cheque was immediately met at the branch of the bank there. Defendant did a large busi- ness and had to deal with cheques, so he must know something about them. He did not ask for vindictive damages, but such as would teach M`Nally that they were not at liberty to 1198 the post office for the purpose of libel. Defendant did not appear and the jury assessed the damages at £3. , •</p>
        <p>"Weary and ill at Ease." THE LADY ORGANIST SWOONED. Life a Martyrdom from Anminia. So Weak, Bloodless and Miserable. With.Dizzinnss and Acute Indigestion. Is Now Strong, Cheerlul and Well, Health Fully Restored by</p>
        <p>A pathetic incident arose on one occasion at a church is Longton, Staffs, when the lady organist suddenly collapred at service, and fell in a swoon. That event was recalled at an interview which a press representa- tive recently had with the lady, Mrs. J. Madeley, who resides at 45, Dunrobin-street, Longton. • "The incident," she stated, "was the result of ill- health, and though it occurred some years a-go I still have vivid recollections of my sufferings. I suffered from acute indigestion with great pain after meals, and sense of oppression like choking. "1 dieted myself and ate less and less, yet every morsel of food punished me severely. I sought medical advice, but to me it seemed only a waste of money. Then came shooting pains between my shoulders and down my back. My body never seemed free from pain. If I went upstairs or walked up a hill my heart Would beat furiously. For months I was never without throbbing pains of neuralgia in my head. I seemed to be in a maze all day long, I had continual noises in the ears, with attacks of dizziness and faintness. Through deficiency of blood I lost energy. Tired and languid by day, and without rest at night, I became painfally weak</p>
        <p>"One day while at the church organ, I was overcome with an attack of dizziness and fainted away. I was taken out into the fresh air, and there recovered from the swoon, but for a long time I 'felt the effects of this. l*ventu‘lly my life became a daily martyrdom; weak and bloodless from anemia and racked by pains, I was a picture of misery and helplessness.</p>
        <p>" LUckily a friend earnestly advised me to try Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, so I purchased a box and before long was surprised to find myself more Cheerful and taking an interest in life. As I continued the pills my food nourished me. I could feel the glow of new life in mr body. I slept soundly at night ; the pains dis- appeared, my appetite increased, and health came back. Soon I was well again. My eyes were bright, my com- plexion clear, and I WAS strong." Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People make Good, Rich, Red Blood, and fortify the Nerves ; they have repeatedly cured Anwmia, Indigestion' Palpitations, Rheumatism, Sciatica, Neuralgia, St. Vitus' Dance, Eczema, Nervous Disorders, Paralysis, Locornotor Ataxy, and Effects of Overwork or Worry, also Ladies' Ailments. But only the genuine pills cure, those always hearing the full name in seven words as above. Of all dealers or direct from Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., Holborn-viaduct, London,post free 2s. 9d. for one box, or 13s. 9d. for six. Substitutes are worse than uselese.</p>
