      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>McDOUGALL'S Patent SELF—RAISING FLOUR I ISTNEESINI N .</head>
        <p>McDOUGALL'S Patent SELF—RAISING FLOUR I ISTNEESINI N .</p>
        <p>LADDEB LOA , ABSOLUTELY PURE A STRANGE LIBEL ACTION- I WOMEN'S GHAT.</p>
        <p>A Great London Hostess.—lt was at Londonderry House about a year ago that the King of Spain first made the intimate acquaintance of Princess Ea, and one of the last visits of the Princess before leaving for Spain was to Lord and Lady Londonderty. The Marchioness is a woman of great beauty, charm of manner, and high intelligence, and may be said to be a typical representative of all that is best in the aristoc- racy of England, Scotland, and Ireland. Not only has she entertained.the King and Queen, and the Prince and Princess of Wales, but Lady Londondonderry is invari- ably one of the private hostesses to entertain the foreign Royalties who visit this country. The hospitality offered to the German Emperor and Empress in 1891 at Londonderry House was one of the most notable of these occasions, and another was the visit of the King of Spain last year, wl4ch has had such a happy conclu- sion in the wedding festivities at Madrid.</p>
        <p>The Spanish Royal Wedding.—Few monarchs can boast the possession of more palaces than the King of Spain, but many of the great Spanish nobles have placed their mansions in Madrid at the King's disposal for guests invited to the wedding. The palace of El Pardo, which has been pi:teed at the disposal of Princess Henry of Battenburg and Princess Rea is about seven miles from Madrid, and is not so vast as the Royal Palace in the city where a suite of apartments has been allotted to the Prince and Princess of Wales, who will remain as King Alfonso's guests until June 5. Lady William Cecil is to be in attendance upon Princess Ens, and among the invited guests are the Duke and Duchess of Norfolk, and the Duke and Duchess of Marlborough. London Society will also be represented by the Duke and Duchess of Wellington, the Duke being a Grandee of Spain, and by Lord arid Lady Dudley, Lady Granby land Lady Marjorie Manners, Lord and Lady Castlereagh and Lord and Lady Londonderry. Women and the Suffrage.—The women suffragists were bitterly disappointed with the result of their interview with Sir H. Campbell-Bannerman last Satur- day. They had a regular field day in London and the deputation which numbered over 350 women was repre- sentative of the principal working women's societieF, both political and industrial, of Lancashire, Cheshire, the North of England, and Scotland. The Prime Minister told them that whilst he himself was entirely sympathetic to their cause the Government were divided on the subject, and therefore he could promise them nothing. All he could do was to advise them to be patient, and not do anything to chqck the flowing tide of public opinion in their favour. In that event—and Sir Henry was no doubt thinking of recent incidents in Downing-street, and the Ladies' Gallery of the House of Commons—he thought that in the course of a few years they might attain their desires. Naturally this was rather a cold douche to eager enthusiasts, expect- ant of fully accomplishing their object within a few months. The saffragists claim that over 400 members of the present House of Commons are in favour of extending some measure of Parliamentary franchise to women, and that if the question were made a political issue their triumph would be assured. With such sup- port as this ready to hand, it is certain that they will not remain quiet, waiting patiently for years, and it is not surprising that the suffragists intend going on with their agitation with renewed vigour. Lady Frances Balfour.—Among those who give their support to the women's suffrage movement is Lady Frances Balfour, who is a daughter of the late Duke of Argyll. She has long taken a deep interest in politics and charitable organisations, and has little to do with that section of Society which does nothing but seek for amusement. She not only writes well, but is a first rate platform-speaker and addressed one of the meetings on woman's suffrage last wcek. Her husband, Colonel Eustace Balfour is an architect by profession, though perhaps best known for his connection with the Volun- teers, and is .a brother of the ex-Premier.</p>
        <p>The Wearing of Flowers.—The corsage" posy," with- out which no fashionable woman is seen, at any time, gained considerably in size this season, and at the present moment might almost be mistaken for a shower bouquet. Gardenias are in high favour, quite a dozen blooms being requisitioned in the building of a" posy" while orchids are preferred by many well-known Society women. Lilies of the Valley, malmaison carnations, and roses, are all favourites with our Qat er, and Her Majesty rarely wears, or carries, any other flowers. Coloured Boas.—To one coloured ostrich or marabout boa, one sees at least a dozen white ones, and we can but marvel how long it will t eke women past their youth, to discover that deep cream and pale colours prove very much more becoming to them. Of course these white boas possess the very great advaltage of "going with anything" but then co will cream, and this soft tone does not emphasise so greatly the lines and hollows which inevitably appear in a face that is no longer young. The best effect, however, for young and old alike, is unquestionably obtained by the boa that matches the toilette, but den, it will be objected, few comparatively can afford a boa to match each costume. All this is perfectly true, of course, but there are women who have to dress well on a comparatively small allowan,e, who remain faithful to one colour, the whole season through, ringing the changes in different tones of that partimar colour, or adding touches of white or black. To such as these, one good feather boa, in one of the palest shades of the colour fixed upon, is all that is really necessary, moreover it is certainly smarter than the white varieties of which we are already beloming somewhat weary. Net Ruilles.—Huge and very full Pierrot ruffles of net which end in front with long loops and ends of velvet ribbon are being sent out with all airy-fairy toilettes, by leading firms. Needless to say they match in colour the hats and gowns with which they are to be worn. These ruffles are by no means difficult to build, but the hams worker must see to it that she provides herself with plenty of net, and that she measures each pleat accurately. The present vogue is to keep the ruffle up closely to the neck all round, but the majority of women iill find that a more becoming notion is to allow them to fall to either side in front, and strew a dainty vest and collar band. Empire Scar ves.—The woman who does not wear a boa or ruffle, dons the Empire scarf, and the mode is likely to provide the artist on the look out for comic effects with some very amusing subjects during the summer, especially at the seaside. Already it is a very usual thing to see wearers of these scarves endeavouring to free the floating ends from the points of another per- son's umbrella or sunshade, and looking more or less unamiable while the disentanglement is going on—Some of the latest spnimers, with their mother-of-pearl and silver embroiderie,!. are particularly costly, hut the crepe de chine varieties simply interwoven at the edge in old Empire style, cost considerably less, and yet prove very effective.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAlLURES.—According to " Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of Commercial ; Failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, May 18th, was 216, viz. :—New Bankruptcy Proce3din,gs published in the London ! Gazette, 118; Deeds of Arrangement registered,9B. The; respective numbers in the corresponding week of last ! year were : Bankruptcies, 113 ; Deeds of Arrangement 66—total 179 ; being an increase of 37. The total num- ber of Commercik.l Failures recorded during the 20 weeks of the present year is 3,369 ; the toful number recorded in the corresponding 20 weeks of last year was 3,595, showing a decrease of 226.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>Crossed Cheque that Caused Trouble.</head>
        <p>A Crossed Cheque that Caused Trouble.</p>
        <p>fussing</p>
        <p>Does it pay?</p>
        <p>Does it pay to fuss? To save coal through not boiling the clothes? To do away with that objectionable steam smell in the house all wash- day? To do the wash in half the time and with half the work? To save a lot of clothes mending.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>In fact</head>
        <p>In fact</p>
        <p>to do that commonplace job, the wash, in the most scientific, sanitary and modern way.</p>
        <p>There is nothing "just as good" as Fels-Naptha</p>
        <p>soap.</p>
        <p>FELS-NAPTHA 39 Wilson Street London E C</p>
      </div>
