        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 26, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 26, 1906.</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>THE BATHECG SUIT.</p>
        <p>Why should not the bathing suit be a becom- ing article of feminine attire? asks a writer in the Delineator. There., is no excuse, says this writer, for the ugly, ill-fitting, meal-bag attire thaf formerly served as a bathing suit, for the woman who cares for her appearance will man- age to have a becoming costume, and it need not be expensive. The most extravagant fancies are now being introduced in these costumes, and rich materials and much hand work is used. Satin taffetas, Surah, China silk, or waterproof Japanese silk may be used for the up-to-date bathing dress, or, if woollen goods are preferred. tennis flannel, delaine, and cashmere commend themselves. One of the newest models has the blouse and skirt made in one and drawn in at the waist by several rows of gaugings. PRETTY SHIRT BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>Smart shirt blouses are still as much to the Lore as they ever wefe. A particularly pretty example of the kind is shewn in our sketch. The material of which it is fashioned is a coarse white linen, very loose and open in the weave, and exceptionally glossy and smooth in surface. Down the front runs a wide box-pleat cut on either edge into large scallops, which are bor- dercd with a narrow piping of pale blue zpphyr,</p>
      </div>
