        <head>SON OF EMPIRE.</head>
        <p>LA SON OF EMPIRE.</p>
        <p>BY MORLEY ROBERTS, Author of "The Western Avernus," " Strong Men and True," "The Earth Mother," aed Earth,' "The Great Jester,"</p>
        <p>; SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: Madge Gretton, while touring abroad with her mother, ' becomes acquainted, in an unconventional way, with a certain Captain Richard Blundellsa military man of 'great reputation as a traveller and linguist, but whose advancement in the army is hindered by an old feud with one of his superiors. Madge is an ardent Imperialist. She long ago made up her mind to marry a man such as Blundell. Her mother strives to make her respect the conventions, but one morning the girl secretly goes mountaineering with { Blundell, who is interested in her, and she falls in love with him in earnest. She goes to see him off when he departs for Dum-Dum, and he kisses her. It transpires that his greatest enemy in the Service is Sir Daniel Fielding, the Adjutant-General, the father of Madge's closest friend, Annette Fielding. Sir Daniel Fielding's intense hatred of Blundell prevents the latter from rising in the Service. When Madge returns home she casts about fcr a plan, whereby Ells can discover the cause of Sir Daniel's hatred, hoping afterwards to obtain some influence which can be used in Blundell's favour.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER VIII. , "I see I have no influence with you. Captain Melville's on my list of heroes, and I think he was atrociously treated. I suppose if there wag a war and you wanted the poor man's help you'd give him command of the baggage camels. Isn't that what they do ;with men they're jealous of, who have no influence ? " Sir Dan smiled. "And I've another man on my list, Sir Dan. I think more of him than I do of Captain Melville, for I've read more about him." "Who's the favoured mortal ? " asked Sir Dan._</p>
        <p>"He's really great," cried Madge ;"and I do wish I knew him. I think he must be quite entrancing. But why should I tell you who he is? You would only smile." "And smile and be a villain. What's this new hero done?" "He's not a new hero," cried Madge, "and he's a real traveller, a man who just walked through Africa, as if it belonged to him." "Eh?" said Sir Dan, rather more alertly. "He did," repeated Madge, "and what's more he said very little about it. "Then he's modest ?" "I should think so. People who hsvc really done anything don't bray about it if they are really great. •I've never hearcryon say a word about what you've done. But I know it all." "0 Madge, be sparing of your influence. But go on about the hero." finished v.— a stitchinT of tha same delicate ; "And if you are unkind?" shade. In the centre oi each scallop is a large "I couldn't be." white embroidered button. On either side of "Very well, 1 will then. He knows almost all the box-pleat come two wide tucks, scalloped in languages, and knows them well. Sanskrit and the same way; while the shoulder straps are Arabic, and Tamil and Pashtu, and lots of 'others finished in similar fashion. The sleeves have all over India." long tight-fitting cuffs, edged at' the top with , Sir Dan frowned a little. stitched scallops. The blouse is completed by a "Anything else?" , smart stock of pale blue silk. ; "He can turn himself into a TUrk or an Arab or</p>
        <p>INDICATIONS OF LONG LIFE. anything, and can't be found out. And he is very, The woman who desires long life must have • very strong and when Bobs Bahadur went from eyes round and wide rather than long and Cabul to k.andahar he was with him, and he narrow, says Modern Women. If they are brown fought ; oh, he fought just like an ancient knight, or hazel life will be longer than if they were though he was very young! black or violet. "Dear me!" said Sir Dan, drily. "This is an _ .</p>
