sed over continually. And when a man's now makes them hideous in the eyes of all, a good soldier and might be useful, I think that's ' , , cruel, and I'm sure if his enemy thought of it,' he A ROSE-TRIMMED HAT. The most striking feature of the summer would be different. 1..\0w, don't you? Oh, tell me 1 . millinery is the extraordinary popularity of the 9" do! ' rose. Morning hats, garden 1. hats, and thej But Sir Dan bristled like an angry cockatoo for 1 smartest of smart afternoon affairs are all pro- a moment. Then after an effort he smiled as nearly fusely adorned with this loveliest of flowers. I like a father as might be with his temperament and ' Our sketch illustrates one of the newest and Madge's phading. most modish of these rose-adorned chapeaux. "I am afraid I cannot help this new friend of The straw of which this pretty hat is fashioned is yours, Mad a smooth, light, flexibla braid strongly re- e." "He's notg a friend of mine," cried Madge. In her heart she cried, "He's my lover, my lover." "What is there against him ?" 1 Sir Dan shook his head. ' , "Well, is there anything ? ~ Sir Dan hummed and hawed a little. 1 1 "He's very unpopular, and when a man is that it's his own fault always. And these matters l really cannot discuss with you. And, besides, if ' he hadn't asked to be relieved of regimental duty, ' they couldn't have put him where lie is. Where's Annette? "I don't know," said Madge, obstinately, "and I don't want to now. I don't like to see unfair things done. I shall just try to find out what it was he did, and who his enemy is. And I'll do it, too! I wish I knew the Commander-in-Chief in India! " Sir Dan laughed. "Sir Eustace Camborne would be delighted, I'm sure. But would you worry him as you do me? ' "0 Sir Dan. I don't worry you, do I? I didn't mean to. But if you were at the beginning of your 'career and you had an enemy, I'de do more than woiry. I'd poison the whele War Office." She put her hand on his arm. "Won't you write to Sir Eustace for me ? ' Ch must I? " . _ .</p>
        <p>Sir Dan wavered, but not, for long. . "You had better do it, I think," he said, drily. "Or, wire him. ' Cl.i4, Simla,' is his telegraphic sernbling Panama, but of a charming green address." shade. The hat is round .in shape, with a sligand htly turned-up brim and round soft crown. A "Now ybu're cruel," cried Illad,e: indignantlys b nd -cm . of satin ribbon, in a deeper tone of green, is "a 3 ta:k to me as if I were a child and. . twisted round this crown, and tied in a small . laugh at me. And I won't he laughe4 at. Yes, I flat bow at the back. Right across the chapeau, ; will find Annette!" from side to side, is placed a band of largo I And going out in a towering rage, she dissolved creamy white roses, with beautiful golden centres , into tears. , and glossy, handsome foliage. A pretty and I "Confound him," said Sir Dan, whom she left original hat, which might be easily trimmed at ; fuming. "But for all Ido to shift him, he shall home. I stick where he_is till he dim,' .</p>
        <p>- FROM AN OLD PICTURE. !A most attractive gown for a young girl can .be copied, almost without alteration in any par- ticular, says the Queen, from a certain well- ! known painting of Princess Mary, daughter of : George HI. Tile long pointed bodice makes- the prettiest waist above the full plain silken skirt, 1 and the silken fichu, frilled above and beneath. crosses .just above the wakst quite in the fashion of to-day. The sleeves are most alluring. soft I poufs one above the• other held in by brace- lets" of the quaintest trimming (tiny round ! coloured velve' buttons set closely along a silver I ribbon) and fluffy elbow frills. There is a tucker held in by, the same ribbon, and the point of the ! bodice is further decorated with the buttons. NEW MATERIALS. • Among the new fabrics for the spring are a great, many silk gauzes. some very delicate, I . e, says' others possessing considerable body. Thee I London Opinion, will ' take the place of mousse- line de sole. For the most part they are made in delicate pearly grey colouring, ornamented with a small silk spray or flower. or made wjth narrow -coloured stripes. These lend, themselves to the present artisti,.. style of dress, and quite the prettiest gown worn at the Royal Academy private view 'this year was a pale grey gauze striped with light brown made in a Directoire style. and worn with a rliirfon mushroom hat and a brown feather boa. To-bay's RECIPE.</p>
        <p>But Madge did not look for Annette. She went straight home instead, and hada sweet tempestuous time in her own bedroom. When her mother wanted to see her she cried discourteously: "Who's there?" and after that "Mrs. You don't answer me " got no answer at all. This upset the mjuile house, and Mr. -Gretton. when he came in tc dinner found Medusa at the head of his t tble, and the butler 'turned into stone. And all because ol Sir Daniel Fielding, i ni because of Captain Richard Blundell, and because Madge had used language for which she ought to have been whipped outside the CaAle of Chalon in the Canton ol 'Vaud. And because of many other things, no doubt, the construction of the universe amongst them. And the next day, it being then nearly the middle of July, they went .elvivn to Oretton Park, that up till now 'Madge had adored, in spite of its looking like a workhouse. Her doe:s and her pony were no consolation, and though the dogs were puzzled it is quite possible that the pony didn't mind. • Fcr instead of tearing through the country on him she went mooning about the park instead, and was dissatisfied Nrith everything, herself most of all. A girl in a less perfect state of health would have ended in being ilk and-as it. was Madge lost some of her brilliancy mill developed a taste for snapping the nose s off everybody; and more 0 ty ilMse yming men iii county society</p>
        <p>BTUFFED SIIOIILDER !AlM—Bone a shoul- der of lamb, leaving the knlickle. Prepare a stuffing composed of a little cooked veal, 4lb. of sausage, meat, of flour. 1' egg, 2 chopped onions, and a seasoning of salt and pepper. Make into a paste, and spread it over the shoul- der. previouAy sprinkled with salt and pepper. Cut some slices (lengthwise) of cold tongue and lean ham. place them on the top of the farce. Fold the lamb over the top. pressing it into the s:lapo of a shog!der. Then place it in a large stewpan with a little dripping or butter, and brown it over a clear fire. Add some stock, a little white wine—if liked—parsley, salt, and mover. Stew until the meat is (mite tender. eovering toe pan. ;serve With young carrots, which may be cooked with the lamb, adding them when the latter is half done. Befo:e serv- ing, strain the gravy and thicken it with a little browned flour.</p>
        <p>especial., with , Natoin she had romped less than three years ago. What a dot es able, fat, rid-faced lot they were, she cried, and. because her ideal was long and thin and hard and dark without ;Lit ounce of fat, she despised the fair unit plump and rosy, and imagined Blundell like a new lfotsyaul killing a dozen , of, them before breakfast. •She wished that he would at least do it before dinner. Then would she be saved from an hour or two of ni:,.ery spent with a young man who knew not Sanskrit or Marathi, and couldn't turn himself into a Turk at a minute's notice,. and .would have been much astonished had he been asked to do so. Yet 'many of them would have Stood on their heads to please her, and made a themselves in many ways to obtain her</p>
