      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <p>chose waking. hours Iler ,•11.!0.1cyt pasr,ea train ner. Exhaling like the od,ui,:e of the ire her bud, it left her all a woman ; see unclerstoc.d her destiny. And as her strained and active mind canvassed all the possibilities of ItICC^IS and failure in the wild adventure of life itself, so she canvassed the chances of her doing what she meant to do. She saw many difficulties, many dangers of dangerous failure. But if it was easy; what credit? And disgrace? She hardly feared, it. He, at least, would not miitd, since it was done for his dear Bake. But it was some time yet till red war climbed the Sunburnt hills and ice slopes beyond Peshawur ; and in two months at, Gretton, mostly with a fup house of guests,'Madge made a thousand theories of telegrams to India. She was possessed With the notion ; and every man in the army now, and every man that had been in it, who came to Gretton House, was attacked in turn, and pumped of a little knowledge, till she saw how difficult it was to send a_wire without its being discovered as a—forgery. The word hit her hard. But she smoothed it down. It was not a crime— only deceit. It was a deceit in the cause of good, and had positive virtue. It was a virtuous and necessary deceit. • It was not really deceit at all, but virtue, Self-sacrifice. It was a self-sacrificing act sc virtue:l Oh, thank Heaven for the sweet conffiusion 1</p>
        <p>And to circumvent" Sir Dan—wicked Sir Dan, vicious old Sir Dan—gave it another savour. Why, when she thought of it, there was really nothing to be said against it. Only it must not fail.</p>
        <p>She had to decide many things. First, should it be in cipher or not? .And if cipher, how was she to get Sir Dan's cipher? And was it to be an official telegram or a private one? She was settled only in one thing, and that was the address, "Chief, Simla." - But even if these points were practically fixed, new and startling difficulties shewed themselves as she lay in bed, and thought the matter over night by night. Supposing she took a cipher telegram to the Post-office, would they accept it, from her? And if they did, would they send it witin.t. referring it to the War Office? She was suspicious of everything. They might even send it, but for- ward a copy to the War Office, and then it would be in vain. Most of their wires went direct from the office itself after having been seen and initialled by the Secretary of State. She knew that. There were sufficient reasons against its being official. She imagined hiring a soldier—a Guardsman—to send it for her. Yet that m;ght be dangerous, very dangerous. Then Paris came to her mind. Sir Dan was often there, and often sent telegrams to Simla from his beloved city of light. Could she get over when he was there, and send it then? At least the French Post Office people were not likely to worry about the English War Office. They might possibly decipher the telegram for themselves ;. but that did not matter.</p>
        <p>Then again, it was possible to get Sir Dan down to Gretton. If he would come she might send anything from their own village of Gretton or from Leigh, the other side of the park, whence she had sent her packet to Dum-Dum. But it was all difficult, and she was so much in the dark that sometimes when she felt tired and her courage was at ebb, she cried and determined to do nothing. Why didn't Richard leave the hateful army and carve out something for himself in Africa? Yet when the morning came, bringing sunlight, and her life was at flood, difficulties were nothing—they vanished like thin ghosts. She sent up to London for a little book on ciphers, and studied it at all, hours. Among Billy's books she found a copy of the Signalling Instructions for the Army. But what would they, say in India at Sir Dan Fielding asking for Captain Blundell to be placed in the way of honeur and glory? She put an analogous case to a bibulous old General, and 'he partially solved her difficulties. "They would think the Secretary of State and his Royal Highness had been at him and had instructed him to do something, and they'd lauph at his 4 having te."</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
