      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Bold-street Wesleyan Church. On Sunday morning the Rev. A. C. Parker was the Preacher at this church. 'ln the evening the Rev. Sidney Pitt occupied the rostrum, his subject being " The anointing at Bethany." Bewsey-rbad Wesleyan Church. thAt this Church on Sunday morning the preacher was e Rev. aoseph Manning who took for his subject "The Mr elrurir re cl in the making." At the evening service There ". J. Moss spoke on " The Light of the world." Were good congregations. Bewsey-road Wesley Guild.</p>
        <p>A ramble in connection with this Guild took place on Saturday. About thirty members went to Moore and in yr:Ate of the unfavourable weather spent a very enjoy- able time. They were met at Moore by the Wesleyan Bible Class who had mserabled at the invitation of Miss Bessie Dutton. Tea was provided and afterwards games were indulged in. Church Services at the Drill Hall. St. Paul's Chnrch is at present beim: re-decorated and for some weeks the services will be held at the Drill Frail. The (mentor: servioes at the hall on Sunday were well attended. The clergy. orcanist and choir occuilied the platform, and the congregation filled the remainder of the room.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Church. The Rev. .Tchn Yonge MIS the preacher at both services at this church on Sunday. In the morning he based his sermon on the words "I saw also the Lord sitting upon a throne, high and lifted,up, and his train tilled the temple " (Isaiah vi., 1) and in the evening his text "Trouble not the Master " (Luke viii., 49). Workingmen's Mission. The Christian Endeavour Society in connection with the above held a very successful "Prayer and Pralfe " rneeting on Wednesday evening, when, despite the .inclement weather, about twenty Endeavourers, inclurl- 'ng the president of the Warrington and District C. E. Union (Mr. Moss) journeyed from Lyrnm and received a very cordial we.corne. The meeting proved most njoyable and instructive. Latchford Wesleyan Church. The Sunday school anniversary services' in connection With the above, were held on Sunday. The preacher, Merning and evening, was the Rev. B. Scott B.A. of Woolton. Liverpool. A children's service, presided over by Dr. Ferguson, was held in the afternoon, at which address was given by Mr. C.M. Clark. Special hymns and anthems were sung by the choir and children,Mr. W. H. Ford officiating at. the organ. The collections for the day amounted to £l3 fis. an increase over the preceding sear.</p>
        <p>Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Church. I The Sunday school anniverssry is one of the principal !.vents of the vest in connection with this church and It is always looked forward to with peat interest. There were large congregations at the anniversary aervices which took place on Sunday. the special reacher for the day neing Mr. William Smitb, J.P., of angley Mills, Nottingham. The morning service was bld in the church and Mr. Smith delivered a powerful , 4scourse on " Lessons from the life of Saul." The t, liiiidren sang appropriate hymns and Miss Constance .._•Eaton, of Oakenvates. effectively interpreted the 42nd . rsalm as a solo. 'Tn the afternoon there was a largely attended musical service in the Part Hall. Mr. David .2arriett. of Sonthport. presided Find an address was - saVrta4 bv the Rev. Sidney Pitt. The choir and children -' stirrina nt.nel hymns in a very pleasing manner, . the exceptiona;l march "Fight on.::s.beinggivfeen .in an sang li Or, 010_7e444031e co!. 1/9. _ Eaton feelingly was held in ti-,e t•pt,:47lY Saviour." The evening service capacity. Mr. Smith all, which was filled to its utmost rZached on the transitoriness of human life as compa in the future world. with the Christian ''outlook Miss Eaton again sang a solo, and there were special kymns. Mr. R. G. Rickets ably »resided at the organ during the day and Mr. W. Hipkiss conducted. The collections amounted to nearly £4O. which is very satisfactory, especially When it is considered that at Easter £lOO was raised towards the erection of a new vestry.</p>
