      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>VILLAGE OF BASKET MAKERS.</head>
        <p>A VILLAGE OF BASKET MAKERS.</p>
        <p>"Make me a willow cabin," demands one of Shakes- Deere's characters in "Twelfth Night." To ordinary folk the request seems unreasonable enough, but in the 4,lUaint old village of Mawdesley. near Wigan. an ,IDteresting account of which is given in "The Wide World Magazine" for June, the willow 'weavers cen alto you anything from a willow cabin to a doll's ,tadle, or from n lounge chair to a paper rack. The nide, which is illustrated by an interesting series of notogrephs, dercribes the whole process of this pictur- ,lque industry from the cutting of the osiers to the ""spatching of the finished articles to all parts of the erld, and presents a striking picture of a typical th 'illage industry, which, from the primitive plaiting of „I e osiers on the Tjcpr hanks, has developed into one of '4le most skilled mild artistic handicrafts of the age.</p>
        <p>WHAT LONDON POLTCE COST.</p>
        <p>trovr many policemen are there in the metropolitan police force, and how are they paid ? The answer may gleaned from the police accounts for last year, officially issued on Tuesrlay. Numbers and pay may be set out thus :</p>
        <p>Per RDDIIM. 5 Chief Superintendents £6so to £OOO 31 Superintendents £32o to £420 539 inspectors 2,185 Sergeents 14,152 Constables</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>ST. HELENS SACRILEGE CHARGE.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS SACRILEGE CHARGE.</p>
        <p>George Pocock, a Sheffield printer, was again remanded at St. Helens, on Saturday, on a charge of sacrilege at Holy Cross Church. The Chief Constable .s.ted that the inquiries about the prisoner were not )rapleted. He appears to have assumed many namee in iParent parts of the country.</p>
