      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>(ALL RIORTS RESERVED.) EDUCATION FOR BUSINESS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>OR FOR CULTURE,</head>
        <p>OR FOR CULTURE,</p>
        <p>SIR WIRE SMITH (Julio,. of " Educational Comparisons," d'c.).</p>
        <p>I think it will be conceded that patriotism can take no higher form in any country than in makhig the most adequate provision for the training of its rising generation. Whatever material or physical advantages a nation may possess, can only be developed and utilised by capable and enterprising meu and women, and the highest ideals of civilization and of the as- cendancy of the mind and spirit, can only be reached by those whe have cultivated their moral and intellectual faculties. Britain is above all a commercial nation, and only as such can she support her population and raise the vast sums annually required for her purposes of government and defence. In tlle mining of coal ; in the building of ships and railways ; in the making of iron and steel, and machinery ; in the manufacture of textile fabrics; pottery, chemicals, and the vast range of articles that are required in the domestic service of the world, she has for a century taken and held a leading position. . Her business however is conducted under such artificial conditions as exist nowhere else in the world. She imports four fifths of her food, and nearly the whole of hex raw materials, and pays for both by the export of her manufactures. By the necessity of existence she is more a nation of shopkeepers now than she was in Napoleon's time, and although she cannot claim as un- questionably as she could 50 years ago to be " the workshop of the world,"—because each advanced country has started its own workshops, —yet proportionately to population her work- shop was never greater than now. As the ex- istence of Britain as a commercial nation de- pends on the success of her business so it seems to me to be the first duty of the individual and the state to see to the efficient education of those to whom the business is to be entrusted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CULTLiME ON AN EMPTY STOMACH..</head>
        <p>CULTLiME ON AN EMPTY STOMACH..</p>
        <p>So long as man's first and greatest interest is in his means of existence, the training wh.ch best helps him to maintain himself shatild have his first care, and, so long as the country's primary necessity is the efficient conduct of its business, so long must its educational machinery show a bias in the direction of business. It is from tide utilitarian and common sense stand- point Witt I have always taken a special interest in what is called technical education. It is not however that I have ever been in- different to education for culture. In my own experience I have come in contact with educationists who spent so much • energy in condemning whet they described as mere "bread and butter education," that they bad none left for promoting the only education which they deemed worth having, namely the education for culture, And yet these educationists were cognisant of the fact that in the schooling of the masses anything beyond the simple essentials of instruction has always been beyond their reach.- Lord Brougham in one of his eloquent orations in favour of popular education nearly a century ago, said that he looked forwarrfo the time when the British workman would be able to read Bacon. Cobbett. who followed, and was a bit of a wag, and knew of the great poverty of the country, remarked that his hope was for the time when the workman could eat bacon. Surely the aspirations of both these patriots were right. for it is only after a man is possessed of • the wherewithal by which he can eat bacon, that he may be expected to read Bacon with, any enjoy- ment. Even culture does not thrive on an empty stomach.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>LEARN ROW TO LEARN.</head>
        <p>LEARN ROW TO LEARN.</p>
        <p>In the fitting of young people for the work of life it is necessary to take into account the material to be dealt with and the limitations of time in which the .training can be imparted. It</p>
        <p>is however becoming recognised, that whatever may be the ultimate object in view, even frcun the very rudiments, all true education leads to culture. How important it is therefore that a sound beginning should be made, that the germs should be planted in the schools attended by the masses of the people. This question is sometimes discussed as if education which be- gins in the day school, ended there also. it is said that from two thirds to three fourths of_the boys and girls receiving elementary instruction leave school before reaching the age of twelve, and the factory half-timers in manufacturing districts leave at thirteen. What training either for business or culture can they have obtained ? And yet with this slender equipment these boys and girls start out on life's journey, and many ot them never enter a school again. Fortunate are those who in their brief career at school have learned how to learn and have implanted in their minds- a love of knowledge. For such,</p>
        <p>with appropriate facilities within reach for continuing their education afterwards. judging by many examples it may be said that every- thing is possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>PROMOTION FROM TES BANNS.</head>
        <p>PROMOTION FROM TES BANNS.</p>
        <p>We have no right to consider the attainment of culture at in any sense a monopoly of the upper and middle classes. Opportunity for all must form the basis of any system of education worthy to be called national. And just in pro- portion as our schools are graded from elementary to secondary, preparing for the technical school and the University, I am convinced from what I have seen in other countries, especially hi tSlitvaittzienrl aonrdea, t GB erirnthliaiiii yt,h the and e nthe d IbT ioilytse d States, d even among the' humblest, will find their wily to the top in ever increasing numbers and will be made available for the nation's most important work to the great advan- tage of the community. lam convinced, also, that the field that promises the richest return for culture is to be found among the young men and women who are engaged in working for weekly wages. True they leave school at an early age and their only chance of continuing and specialising their education is in the suitable pro- vision of night schools. Yet there is no country in the world where organised workers in factories and machine shops have so much laisure as in Britain, and where happily at the present time Lin many industrial centres it is more extensively taken advantage of. It is gratifying also to flud that while there is an enlarged interest taken in Science and Art and in such technical subjects as bear directly upon the daily employment of the students, there . are great numbers who take up literature, poetry, history, music, art and other. kindred subjects for the sake of intellec- tual culture. alone. And speaking from obser- vation, I have found that among this class the most zealous searchers after culture in the higher fields of literature and poetry, are those who have already received advanced instruction in the sciences and have been allured by what they have tasted, to the Elysian fields beyond. The thoughtful apprentice whose first idea in attend- ing an evening school is to obtain knowledge that he can turn to practical account in the day time, is more likely to extend his courses of study in the direction of pure culture, than if he had never taken up science at all. His habit of study is formed under the stimulus of bettering his material condition, and it leads him to seek the -solace and the 'pleasure that he can get from books which elevate his moral character and contribute to the refinement of bi 2 Datum Lnd viinan ra conaidar dna i,lllo,</p>
        <p>-11 V,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 26, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 26, 1906.</p>
        <p>tl/I.#4lo42tejr4W'it.s,</p>
        <p>, • '‘.•</p>
        <p>!I'4aw,p</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>ence Of such Y-oung men Upon their assoCiaiee In the workshops and in their homes, we may realise how valuable and wide is the field that is being brought under the influence of culture in its most attractive and most beneficial forms. I think it may be ' said of many students in hulhble _ life who are attending evening classes. that as they become less and less absorbed in providing for their bodily wants they give more and more attention to, and derive greater enjoy- meet from the studies that tend to the enrich- ment of their 11111IdS. SEEK FIRST TRE NECESSARIES. Thus it is that in considering the question of education for boe;ness. versris education for ,niture, in relation to the millions Idiot scholastic. training in the day school stops sholi before any specific training either in business or culture Call be more than entered upon, I are strongly of opinion that education for businem is not only most fitting in itself but in moss. instances it forms the best foundation for,' the extension of culture, and often acts as n stimulus towards its attainment. My advice therefore to students, especially to those in evening schools would be—" Seek ye first the necessaries of education and the luxuries will be added unto you." With regard to the education of the middle mid upper clitsses the time is not hung passed when there was no such thing as education for business ; the intended career of the student was the last thing thought of in his scholastif -"wining. It was all education for culture and one cannot deny that the discipline of the classical course under the influence of great teachers in many instances furnished the student with an intellectual grasp and a power of analysis which made him formidable in the regions Of thought and imagination' and developed his taste for literary and intellectual pursuits. The public school play-ground must also be credited with its due share of influence in the hardening of his moral fibre and in the formation of character which affected the schoolboy through all his career. The intention of the training was to make the student e scholar and a gentleman ; it was not always successful in the combination, but it produce., many scholare who were also gentlemen, and ninny who though more or less defective in the scholarship were strong in the qualities of the gentleman. The rich man was proud of he son's achievraents as a scholar, but still mop. of his reputation among his associates as gentleman. The wealthy busieess man yedrs ago had great difficulty in finding it school in which his son could be suitably trainee for a business career and in most instances hi: schooling was therefore cut short. Ile went, into his father's factory in his early teens am: " learned by doing." In other instances the boy was sent to a public school, and from theece to the University where he became more or less distinguished, and when he entered hi, father's business he had to &amp;peed on other, for the technical knowledge of processes which lie was called upon to manage or direct, and which in too many instances he never mastered. A BUSINESS TRAINING FOR A =amiss CAREER. Of course there were exceptions. The discipline of mind, the methods of study which by abilite and industry had enabled the student to achieve intellectual distinction, and the skill of hand and eye Which had been developed by athletic snorts helped him also to master the practical details of business. There are many young men destined for a business career still receiving an education that has -not the remotest connection with business who are in effect being educated for culture, but who nevertheless subdue their natural inclinations, and will apply themselves to business with energy and success. Yet ex perience shows that the training of the student should as far as possible relate to his career. If he is intended for business by all menus give him a business training ; if he has coMpliCated scientific work to do let him qualify for it by theoretical and practical instruction ; while on the other hand if he is intended for a profes- sional career, or he is likely to enter public life, what 'could be better than that he should re- ceive the broad training represented by an edu- cation for culture? I have no fear that this canary will suffer in its highest interests from too much attention being paid to- the utilities of life. It is not so much what a young man learns, as the spirit in which he enters upon his studies, that deter- mines the formation of his tastes, and culture is 1 he bourne towards which the seapher for know- ledge is ever tending, no matte in what field that knowledge may lie. The promoters .of that somewhat narrow ' education that trains our young people for the development of our noltetvil resources and industries, have not fail d ti recognise the greater importance of training their faculties according to their natural bent 'A DEFINITION OF TRUE EDUCATION. I heartily accept Mr. Ruskin's definition as covering the ground which I have presumed to take. He says " Education briefly, iS the lead- ing of human souls to what is best, and making what is best out of them ; and these two objects are always attainable together, and by the same means ; the training which makes men happiest in themselves, also makes them most serviceable to others. I believe that -what it is most honourable to know it is also, most profitable to learn ; and that the science which it is the highest power to possess is also the best exercise to acquire." These words written 4u years ago equally justify education for business and for culture. The learned Dr. Elliot, the eminent principal of Harvard University, at u recent educational congress at Boston, remarked that " Emerson taught that the acquisition of some manual skill and the practice of some form of manual labour were essential elements of culture, and this idea has become more and more accepted in the systematic education of • youth." Would that it had been accepted in England as in America. As to the bearing ' of the practical side of education on literary cul- ture, Mr. Henry Smith Williams in an article on "the Literature of Science," shows by re- markable examples that some of the greatest masters of literary style have , been men of scientific training. I select the following _among many. Button, famed a century ago for his mastery of literary style, was by profession a naturalist. Dante was learned ;in every phase of the known science of his time. Keats one , of the few writers of English whom critics have ventured to name in the same breath with Shakespeare " was trained to the profession of medicine. Goldsmith was a practising physician; so also was Schiller the second poet of Germany. BOMB EXAMPLES AND OPINIONS. Goethe, "whose genius raised the German language to a new plane as a medium of literary expression," would be remembered as a dis- coverer in Science had he never penned a page that can be called literature. Darwin's " Origin of Species' m " owed much to the for of its pre- sentation, but much also to the greater artist Huxley, who in" Man's place in Nature," and in a score of other essays brought all the re- sources of a marvellously flexible literary style to the aid of the equally revolutionary doctrines that Darwin had inaugurated. It is well to re- member also among the teachings of history that material prosperity in the true development of civilization must go hand in hand with in- . tellectual culture, and none have more ardently ' desired the spread of the later than those who in their day were the great economic pioneers of the former. In the stress of the campaign for Free Trade in 180, Mr. Richard Cobden said " there will be but one test for the future greatness of Manchester" and that will be a " menthl test, andd, not a Material test,—that • our destiny will be decided, not by the expanse of bricks and mortar,, not by the multiplication , of steam engines, not by the accumulation of wealth ; but just in proportion as mental development goes forward, and in proportion to the development of wealth and mental re- sources just in the same proportion will our destiny be advanced or exalted, or the veiy reverse." At Manchester, also in 1847, the second great apostle of the " Manchester school," Mr. • John Bright spoke in a similar strain. After enumerating ..soine of the inventions, discoveries and the commercial progress of the country, he • asked : " With these increased comforts and advantages that we enjoy shall we neglect thnt which is most noble, because it is the indestruct- ' ible portion of our being ! Shall we be victors in the material world only, and gain no laurels , in the intellectual? Or shall we dive to the deepest depths, and soar to the loftiest heights ; growing in mental stature, and adding to all -.those outward blessiege that surround us—yet neglect those which are purer and more last- ing, and which spring up as a rich harvest from the culture of the iniud ?" lutscLe vensus MINI). Here we have the loftiest and mOst eloquent tributes to culture from the most Iminent res prelentatives of bushiest; that this country has produced ; I could gm, ninny others but I Must content myhelf with a brief appreciation of this same culture , by the greatest business man</p>
        <p>of our time—nay of t time—Mr. Andrew Carnegie. A year ago in his rectorial address to tin- students of St. Andrews he said " Of what value is material compared with moral and intellectual ascendancy—supremacy not in things of the body, but in those of the spirit ! What the barbarous triumphs of the sword com- pared with those of the pen ! What the action of the thew and ,sinews against that of Godlike reason, the murdering savage armies of brutal force against the peaceful armies of literature, poetry, art, science, law, government, medicine and all the agencies which refine and civilize man and help him onward and upward !" And so to sum up—as to whether it is best for the boy that he should be educated for business or for culture, must depend upon his natural proclivities and upon his intended career; but independently of any professional or industrial occupation that he may se ect, in proportion as his education moulds his heart as well as his brain he will be impelled through life to seek for intellectual enjoyment in the higher realms of culture.</p>
        <p>legislation was put on the Statute Book of the country. (Hear, hear.) He was satisfied that they would never find the House of Lords standing out against what was known as the reoognisei will of the country. Finally. the speaker asserted that they would yet see their fiscal policy—the policy of Mr. Balfour and Mr. Chamber- lain—carried out. (Applause.) Mr. J. H. WELSFORD supported, and the resolution was adopted.., _ _ _</p>
        <p>tr.JS.HAnMooD BANNER M P., proposed a resolu- tion protesting against the Education Bill. He remarked that they opposed the Bill, for one reason, because it was the first step to Home Rule, inasmuch as it provided for a separate Council for Wales. The bill, as it stood, only transferred the grievance from the shoulders of one person to those of another. What they wanted was religious equality, and a system of educa- tion which would stop improper inflnence on the part of the priests of all sects. (Applause.) Mr. HARRY BOWLER seconded, and the resolution was adopted. Mr. MACIVER, M.P., afterwards, on behalf of the Liverpool Association, moved a resolution in favour of encouraging working men to take a larger part in the welfare of the party, by giving them more representa- tion on local committees and by establishing clubs in rural and urban constituencies. Mr. RUTHERFORD, M.P., seconded. Mr. J. GRACE (St. Helens) supported, and the resolu- I tion was passed with enthusiasm. 1, •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>TORY WORKINGMEN'S CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>TORY WORKINGMEN'S CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>SIR H. SETON KARR ATTACKS THE LABOUR PARTY.</p>
        <p>Several hundred delegates attended the eighteenth annual conference of the Lancashire and Cheshire Con- servative Workingmen's Federation which was held at the Parr Hall, Warrington on Saturday afternoon. Sir H. Seton-Karr presided. There were with him on the platform the Hon. A. Stanley, M.P., Mr. W. W. Ruther- ford, M.P., Mr. D. Mclver, M.P., Mr. Harmood Banner, M.P., Mr. J. H. Welsford, Mr. H. Sowler, Alderman Bennett, and other Conservatives. Mr. J oynson Hicks wrote that he was organising a series of meetings against the Education Bill. He believed if Lancashire stood solid there would be another general election before many months were over. The report of the Executive Committee, submitted by Mr. F. W. Deacon (secretary), attributed the electoral disaster which had overtaken the party to "torrential abuse "and "colossal lying "by their opponents. The Government was charged with alienating the colonies, and the Budget described as a" financial fiasco." Signi- ficant signs of reaction, it was declared, were apparent. The CHAIRMAN, in proposing the adoption of the report declared that there was only one redeeming feature about the Government's policy and administra- tiod. That was their foreign policy—which had been borrowed from the Unionist party (hear, hear). But, apart from this,they might ransack the political history of the country and would fail to find any Government that had done so many wrong things. The last five months had, in fact, been a legislative and administra- tive nightmare. (Arplause.) He described the Labour party as "suffering from swelled heads and getting a little bit too big for their boots." They were now attacking the House of Lords. (Laughter.) Mr. Keir Hardie was initiating a campaign against the House of Lords, which had existed as many centuries as the Labour party had months. In sporting language It was 1,000 to 3on the Lords. (Cheers and Laughter.) The Labourparty claimed to be placed above the law—to do what they liked without incurring responsibility. He did not think that would commend itself to the starling good sense of these two counties. (Cheers.) As for himself, he did not care two straws whether he returned to the House of Commons or not. He was enjoying his rest very much, But he would spend his last shilling and his last breath in eUdeavouring to turn the present Radical Government out of Parliament. (Applause.) Alderman SALVIDGE (Liverpool) seconded and the report waa,approved. Alderman A. BcoIETT submitted the treasurer's state- ment, which showed a balance in hand of £5O. The election of officers resulted in the following appointments :—Mr. Arthur Balfour, M.P., president ; Sir Henry Seton-Karr, chairman ;, Mr. F. W. Deacon, secretary; and Alderman A. Bennett, treasurer. Alderman BE'SNETT moved a resolution condemning the Government's colonial policy as calculated to pre- vent the development of Imperial union. The resolu- tion reiterated faith in the policy of Imperial union, based on preferential trade, and emphasised the desir- ability of continuously associating it with a broad and generous policy of genuine social reform." He said that when the Conservative Government went out of office, things were quietly settling down in every part of the empire and there was a very manifest ten- dency in7the direction of union. Now the whole state of things seemed to have changed. We were in the middle of a fight in Natal, the end of which none could possiblyrforesee. We had had trouble in Nigeria and we had set practically the whole of our colonial fellow subjects iq the Cape in a state of extreme unrest and dissatisfaction. The result was that throughout the Empire there was a feeling of unrest and dissatisfaction such as never existed for many a long year. It was a most extraordinary thing to him that this home Rule Government should, above all other governments, have shown a disposition to meddle in the affairs of other people. Dealing with fiscal reform Alderman Bennett contended that it was-only on those lines that we could with real hope of success approach the problem of Imperial union. He thought no more unfortunate thing could have happened than that just when the feeling of unity was developing so strongly in all our self-govern- ing colonies, a Liberal Government should have come into power and immediately begun to meddle and interfere with the Colonies and create a feel- ing of dissatisfaction and disquietude. He thought as strongly jos ever that in spite of the misleading pl ards and cartoons and devices issucd at the election by their Liberal friends—the old lies about the big and little loaf and all the other old fallacies—the policy of tariff reform was a sound, statesmanlike, rational, and sensible policy and that Federation had done wisely and had been extremely far-sighted in deciding to stand by it. He was sure the present phase was only temporary, and he believed that before long the Conservative party would carry that great cause to victory. Dealing with the last part of the resolution, Alderman Bennett said in his view one of the causes of their defeat at the polls was that they had not been quite as keen and energetic in the direction of social reform in the last few years as they were some years back. He wanted them to return to their old position and to see the Conservative party maintaining and substantiating their claim to be the friends of the workingman and of sober and democratic progress. The Liberals professed that they wanted to do great things for the working-classes and continually told them of great reforms they wanted to carry out; but when they were brought to book and working-class representatives in the House asked them to make good their promises, they immediately met them with the cry that they had no money, and it was impossible to carry out those ideals. Old-age pensions was a policy with which the Conservative party had been honourably identified some years. They had, however, let it slip into the background, and their opponents might appro- palate the cry and carry it to victory if they had the pluck and courage to do so. He did not, however, think they would do that, because when reminded of the sub- ject they said they had not the necessary cash. If the country adopted tariff reform we should have the neces- sary cash to carry out that and other reforms of vital interest to the working-classes. One of the incidental results of the Education Bill' was that it would saddle us with another burden of a million per annum without any earthly benefit to any sect, creed or class, so it seemed to him. The probability was that under the Liberal Government the Virden of taimtion would grow more severe rather than be reduced. Alderman Bennett concluded by saying that if the two great parties failed in their duty to the workingmen, they , would turn in the direction of another party. It seemed to him tliat the true way to check the Independent Labour Party was to pursue the old policy of honest, straightforward social reform and that could he done without anything revolutionary or dangerous in the least. (Applause.) The Hon.Aßnatft'Srimmr,M.P.,seconded the resolu- tion. He doubted whether. there had ever been three months in which they had seen the House of Commons so degraded as it had been recently. Touching neon the rejection by the House of Lords of the Independent I Labour party's bill, Mr. Stanley said that if the Govern- ment had only supported the measure in the House of Commons they could have got it passed in the House lof Lords. The fault, if fault it was, of the bill not I having passed rested solely at the door of the Govern- ment. The House of Lords acted in a right and con- ' stitutional way, for it was the right and duty of the ' second chamber to see that no hasty and ill-considered</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>WHAT A SCRATCH DID.</head>
        <p>WHAT A SCRATCH DID.</p>
        <p>Bread Winner's Poisoned Foot : Zam-Buk to the Rescue.</p>
        <p>The danger of poison or disease entering a small abrasion of the skin is illustrated in the experience of Mr. Frobisher, of the Leeds tramways permanent-way staff. To a reporter who dalled at his home, No. 11,Cardwell's Yard, Chatham Street, West Street, Leeds, Mr. Frobisher, gave some interesting evidence of Zam-Buk's hee.ling:/powers. „ - . . . .</p>
        <p>"A few weeks ago," said he," I had a sort of irrita- tion just above my left ankle. I scratched the place till I broke the skin, and my sock made the place very sore. The dye got in, and soon the ankle was greatly inflamed, swollen, and painful. It was impossible for me to lift or even move my foot. I had to stay away I from work, and sit in a chair all day long with my leg resting straight out before me. From ankle to toes was one great swelling, puffed, tender and discoloured "Every day my foot grew worse, until I could scarcely bear to touch it. The pain seemed to run right through me. We tried ointments, embrocations, and plasters, but nothing did it any good. At last I happened to think about Zam-Buk. A few applications of this balm had a most soothing effect, and in a few days the irritation had quite died away, and the swell- ing was so far reduced that I got back to my work. Now, not only has the festering been ended, but the sore IMS completely healed." . . . . . . .</p>
        <p>Guard against complications from injuries or small sores by keeping Zam-Buk handy. It is soothing, anti- septic, and healing,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>MR. BRODRICK AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>MR. BRODRICK AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Criticism of the Government.</p>
        <p>A demonstration in ccnnection with the Lancashire and Cheshire Conservative Workingmen's Federation was held at the Parr Hall on Saturday evening. The principal speaker was the Right Hon. St. John Brodrick, ex-Secretary of State for India. Sir Henry Seton Karr presided over a large attendance, amongst those present on the platform being the Hon. Arthur Stanley, M.P.' Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall, Mr. and Mrs. Robert Pierpoint, Mr. J. S. Harmood Banner, M.P., Mr. D Mac Iver, M.P., Mr. .1. 11. Welsford, Mr. Harry Sowler, Col. Pilkington, Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, Rev. T. A. J. Ridpath, Mr. John White, and Alderman Bennett. Mr. BRODRICK, who was very cordially received, at the outset paid a tribute to Mr. Austen Chamberlain (whose place he had taken) and to his father. Proceed- ing, he said that Mr. Morley had said that it was a mis- take for a party to be always feeling its own pulse. He did not think anyone who came to feel the pulse of Lancashire and theshire would be disappointed; they had no reason to fear the action of any political doctor professional or amateur. The Liberal party was, how- ever, suffering at the hands of too many plivsicians, some of whom never took out any official diploma. (Hear, bear.) Since the passing of the great Reform Bill, continued Mr. Brodrick, he believed there was not a case where any party after six years in office com- manded the confidence of the country. After six years no Prime Minister had remained a hero in this country. He was not sure that six years candid criticism and casual caricatures could ever leave a man a hero. (Laughter.) If the late Prime Minister had not avoided that fate which overtook both Mr. Gladstone and Lord Beaconsfield it was because he had to face a reaction after the war, unsupported by that enthusiasm which carried them through that emergency. The most remarkable thing achieved during that period was the excellent understanding with France and the alliance with Japan —(applause)—two things which might be placed to the credit of the Unionist party even after a great reverse in domestic politics had changed the state of their internal affairs. The present Government came in with high hopes, a vast majority, together with a fair field and hopes for success. How many of those hopes had already been dissipated, how many of that majority were already sullen and discontented, how many sections of the country were already aggrieved! If they could achieve all this in five months what would be their position after five years? (Applause) "Why," Mr. Brodrick said, "the babyhood of this ' Ministry has been a succession of blunders, and they began retractation almost before they had learned to talk. (Hear hear, and laughter.) They have had a strange House of Commons to deal with, and they have cut a very sorry figure in dealing with it. When they entered on their electoral campaign they professed complete certainty as to the result. I wish they had really believed their own professions, for if they had they would have been cautious in the attacks they made, and they would have spoken as do men who knowing they would shortly be in power, are afraid to give promises which they cannot carry out. Had they done so they would not have been plunged into that morass of misstatement from which they are now endeavouring to extricate themselves ; they would not have clutched at every straw of misrepresentation they could find— straws which are now setting their house on fire, before they have been six months in office." (Cheers).</p>
        <p>CHINESE LABOUR. Without arguing the question of Chinese labour, Mr. I Brodrick said the professions and the practice of their I opponents were entirely different. Thoughout the election they endeavoured to persuade the electors that I the Government which they had displaced had, in the interest of the rich men who owned the mines in South Africa, engaged a form of labour which was abhorrent to the principles of British statesmanship, and which ousted British, labour. The whole of it was withdrawn within a few hours after Parliament met, and Lord Elgin himself admitted that the word' slavery "was inapplicable to Chinese labour. That razor was made to sell at the election time, and he ventured to predict ! that it would remain to cut the hands of those •••tho I wielded it.—(Hear, hear.) The vote of censure on Lord Milner was a permanent blot on the escutcheon I lof this Government. The late Government had haen charged with extravagance. Just before the general election, they had a speech from the Prime ! Minister in which most definite promises were made that economy was to be the order of the day, that so / large a revenue would rrot be requirec,and that the army I nod navy need not be kept upon a war footing ; above! all, that money would be found for social reform and , the reduction of taxation. It appeared in a very few I weeks that those words were full of emptiness. The ! Secretary for War said,as far as he was concerned there I would be no immediate reduction. " I wish atr. Haldane every good wish in, his great work. I am t delighted that he is enjoying that halcyon period I which comes to secretaries of war when. they first take office. I trust he may be the exception which proves t the rule,. because the eight or ten secretaries of state for war who have served since Lord Cardwell have found that that period was rendered more halcyon by its extreme brevity." (Laughter). ! Had any of his hearers grasped tke astounding Budget which Mr. Asquith had put before them ? (Laughter.) Beforehand, this Budget had been pro- , claimed as likely to remove taxation and to create economy, but it was now found to consist of</p>
        <p>-- DOLES AND MAKESHIFTS -- and far from pleasing the poor man it would only have the effect of bringing profit to the rich man. The late Government had been attacked for giving doles to the farmer and to the Church. Were there no doles in Mr- Asquith's Budget? What about the grants to Poplar and the East End? They might be quite right, but they were not a policy—they were meTely a palliative. Then, again, a more preposterous pr Tosal was never yet put before the nation than that they should pay £1,000,000 from the taxes in order that those who told them that minorities must suffer should enable a minority in their Church schools to exclude for four days per 'week the teaching of religion to a denomination which founded the schools. (Applause). A more wilful waste of public funds was never before' proposed. By abolishing the export duty on coaLnobody had been benefited except the great coal owners and those to whom they sold in Iforeign parts. (Hear, hear). Whilst as to the tea duty the reductiou was of no assistance to the man who bought tea in two-ounce packets. (Applause.) Deal- ing with the question of a general tariff, he said that what the Government should do was to proceed on lines of justice and expediency, and not be frightened lest somebody should do wrong. Why could not the Government trust the people in this matter? It was strange that what was done with impunity in India the Government was afraid to do here. There was a general i 1 tariff in India,. and it did not press hard on the people, I did not create protectioe, and did not enormously</p>
        <p>increase the price of necessaries. They ought to take to heart the advice of Mr. John Morley—to TRUST THE DEMOCRACY, and not run away from them. (Hear, hear.) Did the Government stand up to the democracy in regard to the Trade Disputes Ball They expected a Govern- ment to lead and not to follow under threats. (Hear, hear.) Referring to home politics, the speaker implored them to stand by the proposals of the late Government. In the struggle, which must be long, bat the end of which would be glorious, he believed Lancashire would show an example. Whether the time was long or short they would realise the one object they had in view and on which they were all agreed—the maintenance of the institutions of the country, the strengthening of our position abroad as it had been strengthened under Lord Salisbury and. Lord Lansdowne, and the greater develop- ment of the colonies, which would gain them the reputation of having done their duty in their day and generation. (Applause.) The subsequent speakers included Mr. ROBERT PiRneOINT, who said there was nothing for their party to be disheartened abont. He did not think they ought to get back to power too soon—they had better give the Government a little more rope and a little more time and they would very decently hang themselves. (Laughter.) Mr. Pierpoint commended the work of 6ir Edward Grey who, he said, was showing his willingness to continue the linionist policy in foreign affairs. With regard to Chinese labour if what the Liberals said at the election was true no money should have been spared and every slave should have been sent home at once. (Applansdi Mr. Pierpoint contended that Government interference in the Transvaal had militated against colonial confidence and also com- plained that in the coming conference of the colonies with the mother land the fiscal question would not be , allowed to be discussed.</p>
        <p>COIOGAI PILKINGTON said the electors had turned out a Government which was a credit and which was digni- fied in the face of the whole world and had replaced it with" a miserable jelly fish of a Government." The Hon. Arthur Stanley, M.P., Mr. HARMOOD BANNER, M.P., Mr. D. Maclver, M.P., Mr. J. H. Welsford, and Mr. H. Sowler also spoke and a resolution expressing dissatisfaction with the Government was carried with two dissentients.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S A. Annual Meeting. There was a good attendance at the annual mcating of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society, which was held on Sunday under the presidency of the Rev. John Yonge. THE REPORT. The' Secretary (Mr. B. Farrar) was absent through indisposition, and his report was read by Mr. R. Anus- soN.lt stated that the meetings held during the year had been helpfuLand the members' every-day, work had gone down better because of the hour spent in worship, praise and prayer. There had been nothing extraordinary to record—their meetings had been held under a sense of duty, joy and praise. The committee had been attentive to their duties, and had assisted Mr. Mills in visiting the absentees. The members had been present at the Sunday meetinga in fairly large numbers, but, of coarse, there was plenty of room for improve- ment. He would like to urge -the brethren to take an'even greater interest in constant attendance at the meetings, as absence too often influenced for bad. Their desire from the commencement of the P.S.A. had been to fill the church, to bring in men who pre- viously went to no place of worship,to make live; better and homes brighter. They had not yet been able to 1111 the church but they felt certain that if 500 of them were determined, it would not be impossible. They had from time to time received testimony as to the good effects of the- meetings. The markers, stewards and absentee secretary had all worked admirably' The officers of the sick club had constantly looked after the interest of its members but they were sorry that Mr. George Donaldson had been obliged to relinquish the secretarial duties and Mr. Mills the treasurership of the club. The indebtedness of the members was expressed to Mr. Joseph Owen, benevolent secretary, the savings bank officers and all who had helped in making the services successful and in other ways. Mr. J. .1. Evans, an efficient worker, had had to resign the secretaryship of the savings club but he had a hard- working successor in Mr. J. Ratcliffe. The membei- ship of the Society totalled 543,46 lower than last year, with an average attendance of 360. The prizes gained numbered 736 an increase of 31, the total number of prizes received since the inauguration of the Society being 11,432. What an influence and power for good ; for all their books were calculated to uplift. The open meetings* had been very successful, several well-known speakers having given their services. The secretary concluded by urging all to do their utmost for the advancement of the movement. Last year they noted the great awakening amongst the Christian churches. especially in Wales, and they hoped they would become sharers of those happy seasons. Now their attention was drawn to the dreadful calamities on the other side of the world. The eruption of Vesuvius and tho terrible earthquake in San Francisco all reminded them of the great uncertainty of life and of the certaihty of death. With all the bereaved families they must sincerely sympathise, and pray that these great lessons might be taken to heart by all the world. Let them thank God for his blessings on the pist work of their Society and look to him for guidance in the year upon , which they had entered,. -</p>
        <p>FINANCIAL. ",111r. R. AnciNsox,hon. treasnrer, submitted the annual financial statement. The total receipts amounted to £124 14s. and the expenditure to £126 Ss. Id., leavinx a deficit of Zl 14s. Id. The receipts in the holiday savings club for the year ending June 1905 amounted to £2OO ISs. 3d. ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The following officers were elected :—President, Rev. John Yonge •' general secretary, Mr. 13. Farrar ; hon. treasurer, Mr. Richard Atkinson; assistant secre- tary, Mr. William Farrar ; registration secretaries, Messrs. William Rutter and J. E. Hewitt ; visitation secretary, Mr. R. Mills ' • benevolent fund secretary and treasurer, Mr. Joseph Owen ; markers, Messrs. William and Henry Farrar ; stewards, Messrs. T. Wood, J. Lank- ford, J. Dutton, Henry Taker, William Sparks and E. Smith ; committee, the above officers, with Messrs. IV. H. Payton, George Wallington, Villiarn Beswick, G. Smiley, W. Crosskill, J. Bailey, A. Healey, .1. Simcock, J. Garner' G. Colley, C. R. Cotton, .1. R. Palmer, J. West, J. Gresty, J. Riley, G. Ford, William Miles, T. Ellison, D. W. McEachern, Sam Massey. J. Ratcliffe, R. Lewis, Archer and Hilton. CHIYRCH RENOVATION. The PRESIDENT introduced the subject of the church renovation scheme which has been agreed upon, and it was enthusiastically received. It was decided to raise funds by weekly subscriptions to defray the cost of the repairs to the organ which is estimatea at from .CSO to 2100.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr. W. H. PAYTON, the officers were heartily thanked for their past services. A message of sympathy was sent to the secretary in his absence through illness.</p>
      </div>
