        <p>PREPAID A DVERTISESIENTS. APARTMENTS WANTED. HOUSES WANTED. APARTMENTS TO LET. HOUES TO BS SOLD. ARTICLES LOST. MISCELLANEOUS WANTS. ARTICLES POUND. MISCELLANEOUS SALES</p>
        <p>One Three Six Thirteen Words. Week. Weeks. Weeirs. Weekt. 18 ... Os. 6.1. ... 15. 3d. ... 25. Ott. ... Fs. 9d. 27 ... Os. 9d. ... Is. 10d. ... Se. Od ... 55. Od. 36 ... Is. id. ... 2s. 6d. ... 45. Od. ... iia. td. 45 ... 111. 3d. ... 3s. Id. ... ss. Od. ... 7a. Cd. 64 ... IP. 6d. ... 3s. Bcl. ... 6s. Od. ... 10.. Od.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINEE,</p>
        <p>MAY 23th 1906.,</p>
        <p>The discussion of the Educe- MR. tion Bill in Committee is CHAMBERLAINS full of points of interest. OUTBURSTS. The fact,that first emerges is that the Opposition means to waste as much time as possible. The time is more easily wasted over what is unimportant than ov'er what is of first class importance. The object is to force the Government into a free use of the closure before the more disputable part of the # Bill is reached. This is a resort to those " tactics" of which we had so much in the last Parliament. They turned out ill then, as the General Election • proved, and there is no good reason for sup- posing that they will turn out well under the circumstances at present existing. One thing is quite certain ; the Government will resort to the closure as little as possible. It desires to have the Bill thoroughly discussed in all its parts. Accusa- tions made in the Conservative Press to the effect that the Ministry and its supporters are arrogant and overbearing, are wholly without foundation. They are echoes of what was said with real justice when Mr. Btasoun was forcing his Education Bill through the House. The present measure is in charge of a Minister who has groat patience, tact, and humour. Mr. BIRRELL may be relied upon not to adopt any course which will strengthen thr case of the enemies of the Bill.</p>
        <p>There was a painful scene on Tuesday betwi the Prime Minister and Mr. CHAMBERLAIN. The incident arose out of Mr. cuelananLatres usual way of insinuating sornethingwhich he is not ready to stand by, and which he guards himself against actually saying. As the writer of a popular song remarks, "It isn't what he says, but the nasty way he says it." The Prime Minister, as we chance to know, has not only been closely attending to his work, in the 11.011E0 of Commons and at the Treasury, but has been sitting up with his sick wife at night. Under the circum- stances it would be only natural if he allowed Parliamentary insincerities, which be would ordinarily pass over with a good-natured shrug of of the shoulders. Bqt it is precisely under these circumstances that ME. CHAMBERLAIN chooses to insult him before the House and the country, by describing one of his quite proper questions as "impertinent." Mr. MYER was injudicious in asking whether the word was in order. Of course it was in order. There would be no freedom in Parliamentary debate if it were not, hut it was also in the worst of manners, and Mr. ClizareEe.- Lenses insult to Sir EIENRY CAMPBELL-13ANNERMAN , simply marks another stage in his moral declension. So far as matters have gone, the Government must feel itself much encouraged to keep to the Bill as it stands. It started with thb intention of leaving certain matters to the decision of the House. Arrogance is the last quality with which such a Government ought to be credited. It has from the first displayed every disposition to take the course most likely to command as near an approach to general consent r as is possible, but ifs must now be feeling that the Bill has been drawn up on the lines that must fully meet the conditions of the case. We have ouraelves expressed the opinion that thether the vital proposition in Clause IV. is made mandatory or not is of little consequence, and we adhere to that opinion ; but we cannot disguise from ourselves the fact that if it is made mandatory, the Government will lose a largo amount of what is at present enthusiastic support.</p>
        <p>The Bill as it stands commends itself not only to the majority of those who support the Ministry in the House of Commons, but as to those who were the makers of that majority the Government is between two fires, that of the advocates of secular education on the one hand, and that of the extreme Churchmen and Roman Catholics on the other. The wiser course, it would seem, is that the Government should keep to its own entrenchments.</p>
        <p>- "What is the Church for ? " THE SECULAR a member of the House of SOLUTION. Commons interjected across the floor during Wednes- day's debate on the secularising amendmmt to the Education Bill. There was both point and appro- priateness in the inquiry, for underlying all the 1 speeches in favour of dogmatic teaching in the schools there was the assumption that if the school , teachers are not permitted to teach dogma the : Church will not take the task of religious instruc- tion in hand. There is no adequate religious f teaching'in Sunday schools,Mr.BALroun contended, I and he wont on to affirm his belief that neither in. A 1 the Colonies nor in the United States iø there any adequate religious instruction of the , obildran. BURKE affirmed that it is not possible to draw up an indictment against a nation, but 4 Mr. BALFOUR does not hesitate to draw up 50 11 indictment against three Continents. If his argu° "? ment were sincere, it would drive him to the con- clusion that soole' ' - Colr qnd of the</p>
        <p>that the people of the lonies and oL United States are falling into a general condition</p>
