        <p>of Godlessness because dogmatic religion is not taught in the day schools. We know, of course, that this is not the case. The American people are, if anything, more strict in their pr4fession of religion than ourselves. No accusation of Atheism can be brought against our Colonies. They, too, have their churches and their chapels. The fact that they do not mingle secular with ' religious instruction in the elementary schools is responsible for no such effects as those which ; Mr. BALFOUR predicts with regard to this noun.. try. In the course of a generation,says the leader - of the Opposition, the religious education of children, if it is not included in the curriculum , of the day school, will be a thing of the past. " We cannot believe that he believes it. What is the Church for ? Indeed what are all the Churches for ? They exist for the purpose of teaching religion to the people; and Mr. BALFOUR takes it for granted, for the sake of argument, that they entirely neglect their function, and that religion is kept alive in the country by the teachers 7 in day schools. : Mr.Citamaxna.ktg carries the impeachment of the ( i I.Courches even further than Mr. BALFOUR, if that be possible. He considers it necessary to reply to the perfectly reasonable argument that when the Churches are paid rent for their schools they will , in all probability devote the money thus acquired ' to the purpose of religious teaching. That is, of ' course, what they ought to do; but Mr. CHAMBER- LAIN believes that they cannot be relied upon to alake this use of the money. fie may be right. But how completely such a contention gives away the case of the Established Church with regard to the Bill now before Parliament ! It amounts to the statement that the Church does not care enough for the teaching of the young to devote to it any sum of money that can be otherwise appropriated. We do not think so meanly either of the Establishment or of any other of the Churches which are the possessors of elementary schools. Mr. BALFOUR may depend upon it that the interests of religion are safe in the hands of the clergymen and ministers of the various denominations. Children really do not,as we all know, team their religion in the elementary schools. They learn it at the knees of their mothers, or they learn it in Sunday schools. There . a hundred agencies operating upon them Which will be in no way impaired, even should the Education Bill,in the course of its passage through thellonse,assurne a purely secular complesion.That however,as we have already said,is unlikely. When sturdy Nonconformists like Mr. Gnonon WHITE defend the Bill as it stands, including the com- promises which it contains, as Mr. WHITE ;did on Wednesday in a speech of very considerable power, it does not seem likely to become necessary for the Government to resort to that purely secular solu- tion of the religious difficulty into which Mr. BALFOUR and his friends seem to have foolishly resolved to drive them.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert Pierpoint has POLITICAL HONESTY, emerged suddenly from the well-earned retirement he obtained as the result of the General Election. Speaking at the meeting in Parr Hall on Saturday )vening he indulged in a few terminological inexactitudes. The phrase is one which he intro- duced himself and endeavoured to apply to the 'tions of the Liberal Government and his late ipononts in Warrington. Mr. Pierpoint alluded ifically to "the blatant placard put up-- 4orious news about Smith Africa. Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman has forbidden the importa. tion of Chinese Labour in South Africa." This he places .under the head of " terminological ',l3.sxactitudes." Why? We defy Mr. Pierpoint to s?Prerc'g th 3 absolute accuracy of the poster. pbell-B annerm an did, at once, sl'oe:ts riaY Cam importation of Chinese so far as was possible, by forbiddinge the issuing of any more licences. He was, of cours, unable to stop the fulfilment of those contract's which the expiring Government hurriedly sanctioned just before relinquishing, office. This is a fact about which Mr. Pierpoint ought to have reminded his audi- ence. To add that the Chinese were still there, and that they had increased since then, was a piece of barefaced misinterpretation of fact unworthy of the late Member for Warrington, He knows that the increase is entirely due to the action of the last Government, which he supported, and if he had been politically honest he would never have used such a transparently false illustration Of what he means by a "terminological inexacti- tude."</p>
        <p>Three Warrington licences Enocilia LICENCES. are to be extinguished. The Compensation ithority mat yesterday(Thursday)and sealed the of the Rope and Anchor Inn, the Queen's ...A, and the Boar's Head Inn. Esch licence is lapse, subject of course to the granting of corn-: knsation to the owner and tenants. This is in accordance with the policy, which magistrates all 'ver the country are adopting, of thinning out licences in Congested areas. In lees rigorous days Imblic-honses were indiscriminately and thickly planted in all the large towns. 'Warring- ton has its heavy legacy of overcrowded (listricts, and there can be no gainsaying the necessity for a reduction of licences. For some years the magistrates have been alive to the great importance of their duties in this respect, but, of course, the process must be gradual, and there is still considerable room for improvement. As to the refusal of the licences mentioned above, we should imagine that no one will be disposed to question the wisdom of the justices' action. While nothing could be urged against the conduct of the respec- tive businesses—which was admitted to be quite satisfactory—there was ample proof that the 'Nes were not needed to meet the requiremenls the neighbourhood. Anyone with a knowledge local conditions will bear this out and the . gistrates will have :the support of all well- shers of the community in their eudeevours to lake the number of local licences commensurate the needs of the population. -- There have been numerous Tirn LORDS AND public referencs during the LABOUR, last week to the inevitable conflict between the House '3,f Lords and the Labour party as representing ‘he democracy. Diarent speakers, possessing gifferent convictions, of course hold ditterert l'iews on the matter. Mr. Seddon, the member for Newton said : "If the fight came, it Would be a short and sharp one and Labour would ,coree out on top," On Saturday at Warrington Henry Seton-Karr said that in the event of a 'zinflict between the Lords and Labour, he would lay a" thousand to three" on t:.e . Lords. Obvi- 2,tisly these two gentlemen cannot both be right in 'noir forecast,but we should say that Mr. Seddou's P,„ntediction is more likely to be folfilled than Sir enry is likely to win his bet if he ever puts it in concrete—i.e. cash—form. The Lords, being well- 11:lereatened, have lived long, but their indestructi• Allity is by no means assured. In rejecting the ',•lietis (Blacklegs) Bill they revived the dormant 4estility of the nation to that hereditary Chamber uich toils but little, and spins only mischief. The It:1-1g,c.;le when it comes will not be between the trords and the small band of Labour members in the •1.-ouse of Commous.ll it were,then Sir Hmry Seton- Vlrr's odds might not be disproportionate. A j:tle more blind obstruction to Government Bills the Upper House. and our hereditary legislators 11114.31 sudaenly lied themselves face to face with a Lal,ted Commons determined on their removal,and eled by the cpinion of the country. The people dlt/le a lord, but they are not as willing as in the 41.:Y,s of old to he down while their rights are ad and their privileges trampled upon. Mr.</p>
        <p>1</p>
