      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <p>Gladstone forecasted the doom of the House of Lords, and we are inclined to think that the day of legislative emancipation is ahnost in sight.</p>
        <p>The circumstances attend - STRANGE SUICIDE ing the death of the man SEQUEL. Gibbing who was found hanging in his house last week were so unusual as to justify the coroner in postponing the inquest. On Monday the inquiry was concluded, a verdict of "suicide while temporarily insane" returned, and a certain amount of blame apportioned. With the verdict of the jury no fault can be found, but we dissent from the censure which both coroner and jury passed upon Allison, the infirmary porter. It looks rather to us as if this man in a humble position was made the scapegoat for somebody else. The facts of the case, it will be remembered, are these. Gibbins, the deceased:man, arrived early on Tues- day morning last week at the infirmary suffering from hallucinations brought on by the delirium of pneumonia. Very soon after, his wife and a neighbour called at the Institution and asked that he might be kept there for about an hour, until they sent a cab for him. After a brief , interval the two women returned home where, to their horror and surprise, they found Gibbins hanging and dead. The story from the Infirmary is that Dr. Brown sent the man home in charge of the porter with instructions to place him in charge of a trustworthy man or a policeman. This the porter lid not do, and is, perhaps, blanieable for not strictly obeying orders. But he has the excellent excuse that he did not know Gibbins was insane, and was told when nearly at the door that he (Gibbins) would rather go into the house him- self. The porter, not unnaturally therefore, released his charge and returned to the Infirmary. Because the man committed suicide almost imme- diately after, the blame is laid on the porter, and the Coroner, very unfairly we think, suggests that his position at the Infirmary should be re-con- sidered by the Board of Management. Under the circumstances and seeing his limited knowledge of the patient's mental condition, the porter only did what probably ninety-nine men in a hundred would have done. If responsibility for the unfor- tunate occurrence can be placed on anyone's shoulders, we think the bulk of it should rest upon the doctor at the Infirmary who disregarded the women's request that the man should be detained until they sent for him. Dr. Brown honestly admits having made a slight error in judgment—a fault we are all liable to—and the matter might have been left at this stage without passing such harsh criticism on the porter, who is, in our opinion, the least blameable man of the lot. We hope the Infirmary Board will be lenient in its consideration of these errors of judgment, and recognise that the element of mischance entered very largely into the whole unfortunate aflair.</p>
        <p>There never was a time THE ART OF IBSEN. when Ibsen did not deal</p>
        <p>with strong situations and strong motives. Ho has been compared with Carlyle because of this Titanic fury—and forebears of his were Scotch, as is the case with that other Norwegian artist, Grieg, who makes us melody through which, in a more delicate way, breathes Ithe primitive spirit of the mountains. Ibsen, of 'course, perfected his art by first-hand knowledge of the exigencies of the theatre (.which, of course, does not lay it down as a rule that first-rate play- wrights must be actor-managers), but the force of the man was inherent from the beginning. One has only to look with sym- pathy at such early work as "Madam Inger of ' Oesteraad " (1854) to realise that ele- mental passions are ever the theme, with their processes and consequences—" there are always consequences "—and the fidelity and power with whi3h they are portrayed exactly fulfil the Aristotelian requirements of drama. Ibsen was a modern prophet. Nobody like him understood that paradox, the value of were speech in promo- ting the action of a drama. Ills characters may never move from their chairs, bat one's concen- trated attention is chained by " developments " all the same. In this respect, by his fearlessness and by his symbolism, he had created a "new drama." But lie must be intellectually understood. MI symbolism is drama, and -no modern author better than Ibsen knew the potency of this imita- tive art. In this particular he has good title to be included among " the last of the Greeks."</p>
        <p>The Colonial Marriages Bill, THE DECEASED which was accorded a second WIFE'S SISTER. reading in the House of 0.;•w;i1 1121 Lords without a division, is a measure which, as Lord Elgin pointed out, Colonists have neer% demanding for years. When all but one of the British Colonies have sanctioned the practice of marriage with a deceased wife's sister, it is not for them, at any rate, to raise the question of morality or desirability. They have disposed of that, and having done so, it is surely only an act of common justice that those who have acted in accordance with the law in their own country should find, on coining to tee Mother Country, their children not debarred from the rights of the children of legally married couples. Lord Strathcona and Lord James of Hereford gave moving pictures of the position of parents and children who find themselves thus ostracised. It was contended that marriage practices in other parts of the Empire ought logi- cally to be legalised in this country if the bill becomes law; but the Lord Chancellor pointed out the British law only sanctions those unions as legal contracted in other countries which are not repugnant to the general sense of Christendom. To those who have religious objec- tions to the class of unions to which the Bill applies it will he,as it was to the Archbishop of Canterbury, some consolation to know that the measure does not in any way interfere with or alter the existing marriage law of England.</p>
        <p>The Russian Dunn knows</p>
        <p>RUSSIA'S DEMAND exactly what it wants, and OF THE CZAR. does not mince words in saying so. Political amnesty, aI- socialistic division of land, the subjugation of officials to the law, freedom of speech, Press, and labour'sins andrigintivti°tl:bsti,iril Ittoszwthit7lt ,of a rteh the pedemanded,rson— and the abolition of the Council of the Empire. ' But nothing is so important as the request for political amnesty. Toat Democratic lead.re should be in prison while their comrades are voting in the majority of the .Darna is a thing inconceivable, and it was the first and most imperative demand that iiiitant and wholesale liberation should be granted to these political offenders. The position is an intelligible one. The expropriation of land is a question pro. fourylly vital—the laud of the Crown, the Church, and the land-owners—but it becomes insignificant • by comparison with the generous and popular stroke of reform by which the lives of comrades land fellow-workers are affected. It is the out- ward and visible form of the new order which they helped to bring about. The peasants may need the land for settlement ; the poverty of their lot may cry out for it; that is still a thing of the future. Other elementary liberties cited in the draft address to the Throne may be of the very essence of Constitutional government ; this amnesty is a stroke of clemency on which the nation has set its heart. The Czar has had opportunities of doing some handsome thing for his people ; this in the view of all the progressive elements in Russia, was his supreme opportunity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Warrington Clerk's Suicide,</head>
        <p>Warrington Clerk's Suicide,</p>
        <p>The suicide has been reported to the police of William Hornsey, a clerk employed by Messrs. Fletcher Russell and Co., Limited. Deceased was, found in h's house, 17, Oxford-street, having cut his throat with a razor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>FINED £3 AND COSTS 21 TIMES..</head>
        <p>FINED £3 AND COSTS 21 TIMES..</p>
        <p>John Corns, a bookmker, of Bold-street, was sum monad at Wigan on Thursday for frequenting a passage for the purposes of betting. It was stated that Corns had been before the justices on 21 occasions for betting, and that be had been finer' £5 and costs 20 times. The magistrates again fined him £5 and costs.</p>
