      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 26, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 26, 1906.</p>
        <p>OVER-INDULGENCE.:</p>
        <p>At Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday, last week, W. H. Griffiths, grocer, Earlestown, was charged with persistent cruelty to his wife, and a separation order was made by the magistrates, the defendant to pay ss. per week.—On the following day he was brought up at Earlestown Police Station, before Mr. Ormand, J.P., having been apprehended by P.C. Rimmer for drunkenness in Oxford-street. In default of paying a tine of 10s. and costs he was committed for 14 days.—William Preston, for drunkenness, at Winwick, was also sent down for 14 days.</p>
        <p>KNOCKED DOWN! BY A CART. Last Friday, Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquest at the Earlestown Town Hall, concerning the death of Sebastian Wilson, aged 66 years, a waggon builder, of 16, Booth-street, Earlestown, who died on Tuesday, May 15th.—Alfred Massey, 16, Booth-street, said deceased, who was his father-in-law, went out on Wednesday, May 9th, and on his return he said he had had a serious shaking, telling witness's wife that he had been knocked down by the shaft of a cart whilst cross- ing Cross-lane. He made no complaint against the driver of the vehicle, but could not tell how it had happened.—Michael Murray, who is employed by Mr. T. Boardman, of the Pied Bull Hotel, Newton-le- Willows, stated that on Wednesday afternoon, May 9th, he was driving a horse and trap in Cross-lane, proceed- ing at a slow trot. He saw four men in the middle of the road, and shouted, and it was only as he came close to them that three moved out of the way. Witness had no chance to get to his own side. Deceased's hand caught against the shaft, and he fell down, but witness pulled up before the wheels had passed him.—The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death," and attached no blame to the driver.</p>
        <p>1 ...........</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>It is said that the Lords' " lift " may be like the Irishman's " rise." .</p>
        <p>* * * The passion for painting the map red has subsided. We ask for nothing better than to be left alone in order to "develop the estate."—" Imperialist " in the" Sunday Sun."</p>
        <p>* * * Mr, Brodrick has a decided penchant for metaphor This mode of expression was entertainingly employed in his speech on saturday and in some degree com- pensated for the lack of convincing argument. * * * A motorist driving between the Wilderspiol cross- ings and Warrington Bridge was under the impres- sion be was proceeding,over cobble stones, the jolting sensation being suggestive of such paving. Motorists' opinions after negotiating Sankey-street are better left unrecorded. .</p>
        <p>* * The notice calling a meeting in an Essex parish to protest against the Education B:11 runs "All parishioners ar,, invited to attend,the parents of present and future children being especially urged to do so." .</p>
        <p>* * * "The Free Churchman drinks his lemonade (poor fellow) and then preaches lemonade. The upper-class Anglican drinks his glass of wine, and then founds a "Band of Hope" for the lower classes."—G. K. Chesterton in the "Daily News."</p>
        <p>-* The 2,500 members of the Juvenile Branch of the Beautiful Warrrington Society possess a flower-pot each, and want a plant to put it in. Will the general public—those who have gardens— kindly oblige by sending any spare plants to Mr. John Fairhurst, Bank House.</p>
        <p>* It is a long time since the Council sanctioned the widening of Warrington Bridge and let the contract. Nothing is, or has been, done so far. Why ? And echo answers, Why ? This masterly inactivity in defiance of the wishes of the townspeople and the official sanction of the Council needs inquiring into. Will Councillors Pemberton or Lord oblige?</p>
        <p>* 5 * A clever Widnes collie has solved the problem of how to make free usa of the transporter bridge and regularly crosses from one town to the other without paying toll. The dog trips up the spiral staircase for 100 feet and then makes its way across the huge span, along the narrow steel girders on which the car runs. Its passage always attracts a crowd of sightseers and it is to be hoped that the daring adventurer will not reach an untimely end. * 5 * The report prepared for the delectation of the delegates of the Conservative Workingmen's Federation who assembled at Warrington on Saturday was quite a masterpiece of invective. It was freely besprinkled with such choice expressions as " colossal lying," " torrential abuse " and " calculated misrepresenta- tion." Painful memories of electoral disasters still rankle. in the Conservative mind, but they might accept the verdict of the nation in a more sportsman- like way. * * * To stand by and see the number of evidently intelli- gent people who will not walk under a ladder makes it easier to realise the continued exNtence of the hereditary House of Legislature. The fear that some- thing would happen if the House of Lords were reformed is just as baseless as the fear that ill-luck would follow the innocent act of walking under a ;wooden ladder. Perhaps they are afraid of something dropping on them if they reform the House of Lords. "The Times" newspaper for instance.</p>
        <p>*55 The erratic behaviour of a cow which was being driven along Sankey-street on Monday morning created no little amusement. After making several desperate attempts to elude its custodian, the cow suddenly decided upon a visit to a newsagent's shop near Bank Quay Bridge. Having entered the establish- ment, it was content to calmly survey the collection of literature and postcards and received the advances of the shop-keeper in quite a friendly way. The driver, however, arrived upon the scene and the cow had to make an undiguided exit, being rudely dragged into the street by the tail. * Bishop Lawrence, one of the ablest of the American bishops,has been investigating the economic condition of the clergymen in his diocese. It will surprise people who Imagine all salaries in America are double or treble what they are in Britain to learn that 117 clergymen in Massachusetts averaged only .±-:250 a year as salary. Only 13 received a salary of Fifty could not afford to marry. The Bishop found real want and distress in some clergymen's homes, owing to the small income: It must be remembered that .£250 in America would hardly go further than £l2O in England, so that the salaries are really smaller than they appear.</p>
        <p>:1‘ In reference to the motion placed by Mr. Swift Mac Neill on the notice paper of the House of Commons, as a step towards the removal of Mr. Justice Grantham from the Bench, which is supported by a memorial to Sir Henry Campbell Bannerman urging facilities for its discussion, signed by nearlyhalf the members of the House of Commons, the " Freeman's Journal " points out that a vote of censure on an English judge has never been carried in the House of Commons since the Revolution. A vote of censure on the conduct of Lord Westbury in 1.865, when Lord Chancellor, was followed by his instant resignation of the Great Seal, and it is believed that thP motion with reference to Mr. Justice Grantham will, if carried, be. followed by a resignation, to whose enforcement, under the provi- sions of the Constitution, it is the first step.</p>
        <p>* * In addition to making his little bet on a Lords v. Labour contest, Sir Henry Seton-Karr took other means of expressing contempt for the representatives of the workers. In denouncing the Trades Disputes Bill we can commend his audaeity, but nothing else. Less than six months since, from his platform in the Sutton National Schools, he gave a pledge to the local trades unionists that if elected he would support the Trades Disputes Bill. At that time there was no Liberal Bill before the, count 4. The Bill he was asked from the audience to support was the Labour Bill, Mr. Shackleton's Bill, which is the Trades Disputes Bill now before the Rinse. Really, Sir Henry's despera- tion in defeat, and his bad temper at the Labour Party, make him forget things which he ought to remember. Two hundred and ninety-five Years ago this week the first creation on this earth of baronets took place (says the "Westminster Gazette"). The creator way his Majesty King James I. He was pressed for money; and the Earl of Salisbury suggested that if two hun- dred gentlemen would pay a fee of £l,OOO each for the honour of calling themselves baronets, why, 1,000 multiplied by 200 made (and he figured it out) :£200,000. James said such a step might offend the general body cd the gentry. "Tusli, sir," said the cynical Salisbury; "you want the money ; it will do you good; the hon,ur will do the gentry very little I harm." The King yielded to his Salisbury, and in this manner was the ancient and most beautiful Order I of Knighthood superseded by the new Order of Baronets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>i – RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>i - RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>WIDNES GIRL BURNT TO DEATH.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse county coroner, held an inquest at the ' Accident Hospital, widnes, on Tuesday touching the death of Agnes Mannion, aged thirteen, of 56, James- street Widnes. About twenty minutes l past .seven o'clock on the 17th inst, whilst her mother was out shopping,the girl was in the act of looking for a half- penny which had fallen in the fireplace. She had a piece of lighted paper to aid her in the search, and, unfortunately, her dress caught fire from the burning paper.—A verdict of" Accidental death" was returned.</p>
        <p>• - - FATAL FALL. At Widnes Accident Hospital, on ruesday morning Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner, held an inquest touching the death of Thomas Byrne, aged 24 years, Adderson-street, Liverpool, labourer, at Mr. Thomas Davies's ironworks, Ditton-road, who met his death by falling down a ladder on the 15th inst. Dr. Brebner stated that he had made a post-mortem examination. The body was that of a well-developed man. Upon the skull there was an extensive fracture, from the top to the base. This had caused hemorrhage on the brain, the pressure of which had undoubtedly caused death. Henry Kershaw said he , was engaged painting the tank inside when deceased fell from the ladder outside. The ladder was strong, was not greasy, and was reared up in an easy slanting position. He only fell about five feet. Thomas Cleaver said he was working eight yards from the ladder. When Byrne was five rungs up he fell backwards upon his head. The Coroner remarked that the case was a very curious one for a healthy man to meet his death by falling so short a distance from a ladder.—A verdict of , "Accidental death " was returned.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN FOOTBALL CLUB. NOMINATION MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of members for the nomination of officials for next season was held in Watson's Hotel, on Monday evening. Mr. H. Cawley presided. The following were the nominations :—President, Mr. John Littler ; treasurer, Mr. C. Pownall ; secreta:ry, Mr. A. Abel ; assistant secretary, Mr. J. H. Littlemore ; audi- tors, Messrs. T. C. Cooper and E, Lunt; captain, first team' Messrs. J. Jolley and I'Moran ; vice-captains, Messrs. J. Jolley, J. Moran and A. Richardson; captain, second team, Messrs. J. W. Hoxworth and F. Dutton ; • vice-captains, Messrs. J. W. Hoxwortb, F. Dutton, and W. Shaw; committee, Messrs. W. H. Cawley, A. Garner, R. Garnett, W. Gregory, G. H. Grounds, T ; Huxtable, J. Jolley, senr., G. Jones, G. Moores, G. Robinson, A. Turner, A. E. Woodier, F. Ellison, R. I Moore, W. Grounds, W. Kirkham, W. Jones, and F. W. ; Hancock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>RUNCORN WOMAN'S SAD SUICIDE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN WOMAN'S SAD SUICIDE.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, on Wednesday, Mr. R. Dobson, coroner, held an inquest on the body of Mary Rhodes, wife of William Rhodes, a carpenter, who committed suicide on Tuesday morning by cutting her throat with a razor. The husband stated that the deceased had been suffering from nervous debility since last November. When she had pains in her head she wished she was dead, and had asked him to get some poison and put her out of her misery. On Tuesday mbrning,at half-past five, he made some tea and took a cup up to the deceased. He thought she was asleep, but on turning up the blind he saw blood all about her chest. Her throat was cut, and she held a razor in her right hand. A piece of paper was found between the bed and the mattress, and con- tained the following :—" Pains in myhead unbearable; cannot live to bear it any loneer. God bless my chil- dren and comfort them, as I cannot help. Borne it night and day since last November, with very little sleep. Nights and nights and never closed my eves. God forgive me.—n.a." The jury returned a verdict of" Suicide whilst of unsound mind," and expressed their sympathy with Mr. Rhodes and family. BOOKNIkKERS PROSECUTED AT WIDNES. MAXIMUM PENALTY INFLICTED.</p>
        <p>1 At the Widnes Petty Sessions, last Thursday before Councillor Frederick Neil, C.C. and Alderman Major I J. W. Wareing. two summonses C.C., infringement of the Borough bye-law prohibiting street-betting came up for hearing.—The first defendant wac Edward Gordon. of Elliot-street.—Mr. J. W. Knowles' who prosecuted on behalf of the police, said that on May 9th, whilst con- cealed behind a sleeper fence in thevicinity of Lugsdale Bridge, Detective-sergeant Davidson and Constable Farish saw defendant, who was not in his working clothes, but was" dressed up." They watched him from 12.5 pin, until 12.25, and saw eleven men hand to him slips of paper and money which he put into his pocket. The last person was John Drury, who handed him a slip of paper and 35.. after which defendant gave Drury some coppers with which to get a gill." The constable assisted the sergeant to get over the fence and, upon seeing him, both men ran away. The sergeant knew the defendant well, and knew him to be a bookmaker. The Bench had power to inflict a fine of £5, which many county judges regarded as being inadequate. He asked that the maximum penalty might be imposed.—Evidecce bearing out the advocate's state- ment was given by the sergeantand constable.—Defend- ant called Drury, who at first declined to give eviden'e, but ulimately consented, and stated that during the time that he was in the street no-one else was in sight, defendant being alone.—Cross-examineri, he admitted that he gave defendant 3s.—Defendant denied that he had dealings with the other eleven men referred to.— The Bench imposed a fine of £5, with advo- cate's fee and costs, £6 13s. Od. in all, the alternative being fixed at a month's imprisonment.— Michael Graham, of Kent-street, was similarly sum- moned in respect of the 11th inst.—Ar. Knowles stated that between 12.5 and 1.45 on the afternoon in question the same officers, whilst concealed in St. Paul's ohurch- yard, saw defendant take four bets; from a man, and one from a womart, The latter subsequently accompanied him to the Grapes Hotel, whither the police followed and found defendant making entries in a book contain- ing names and figures.—Evidence to this effect was , given by the officers—Defendant asserted that he merely had a conversation with the woman referred to, and that tie took her to the hotel in preference to standing talking in the street. He asked that the hear- ing might be adjourned a short time in order that the woman might he sent for. The Bench held that he had' had sufficient time in which to secure witnesses—lie saidrae had not known that her prerence would be necessary.—A penalty similar to the one in the preced- ing case was inflicted.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON. LOCAL SUCCESS AT BOOTLE CARNIVAL. The proceedings in connection with the Bootle May Day carnival were favoured on Monday with lane weather' an large crowds of people tamed out to witnessthelorse procession through the town. Mr. J. Makeague of Newton secured first prizes in the gentle- men's turnout and saddle conpetitions (open). ' FORTHCOMING ELECTION AT NEWTON. A contest for the representation of the Newton Ward on the Newton-in-Makerfleld Urban Council in lieu of the late Mr. John Simpson, will take place on 31onday next, May 28th. Saturday was the day for withdrawals, hut neither Mr. Ritchie nor Dr. Walter Latham felt justined in availing themselves of the opportunity. Mr. Ritchie is backed vp by the Conservative interest, De. Latham being reprbsentative of the Labour and Non- conformist section.</p>
        <p>LORDS AND LABOUR. SPEECH BY MR. J. A SEDDON, ALP. Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P., was tha principal sneaker at the eighteenth annual congress of the National Union of Dock Labourers on Monday morning in the William Brown Library, Liverpool. He declared that the Independent Labour Party had made their mark in the House of Commons. They had not only had their own way, but they had succeeded in altering the set *icy of the strongest Government of modern times. (Heir, ' hear.) Trade unionism, however, was still in bad odour not only in the law courts, but amongst the employers, whilst legislation in the Rouse of Commons had hitherto been inimical and contrary to the wishes of the working classes of the country. The fact that the present strong Government had accepted the Trades Disputes Bill of the Independent Labour Party rather than their own was an indication that if the workers of the country Parliament.in th e ut tiv enbesi e sveendd theirt hs were eo ;Nemo orienpgr et 0- swelninttaotdiveasnyttohg done * * * get justice from the present Government, and that An interesting case at the County Court in which the was,,going to be passed a Trade Disputes Bill which would give 'v e them an anti-Taff Vale decision. A a Warrington cyclist claimed 31100 damages from compensation Bill, of benefit to the whole of the a motorist resulted unsatisfactorily for the claimant working classes of the country, and other Bills would and only served to emphasise the danger which lurks in time come along as the result of the Labour party's at wilderspool crossings. The accident happened at eaorts. The proposal to make light dues a charge upon this point,after the gates had been opened and the con- the Imperial exchequer -rather than upon the shipowners ! gested mass of vehicles was struggling to get through. had had his support until he learned that the shipowners , There is a similar scene dozens of times every day, were no t c carrying dock outcer labourers.tai n d4itctek irneleapi Independent for the and the nuisance and danger are being aggravated as protectionparty accordingly blocked the measure, and the result times goes on. The crossings are a terrible legacy was that the shipowners had agreed to a conference at from the past. How long have they to be endured which the grievances of the dock labourers would be ! in the busiest thoroughfare leading to and from War- discussed. He hoped that before long one of the rington ? Their removal should be one of the earliest greatest dangers that beset dock labourers in the pursuit public improvements considered by the Council. But iof their calling would be removed.. (Hear, hear.) is their future not part and parcel of a larger scheme arokuirnit3iFlf b thethe e rejectiontlous House oof Lords. thers. said Alien Seddonni3 which embraces the whole area from the Bridge to the Bill had y been endorsed by the Liberal, Tory ' the Crossings ? Nationalist, and Labour parties in the House of Commons. So just was the Bill that there was no dis- cussion. Neither the Liberals nor the Tories dare oppose it, the Nationalists were in sympathy with it, and it went through the House of Commons as the expressed will of the country. It was a Bill which was a complementary Bill to that passed by the late Govern- ment. The Labour party maintained that if it was wrong to bring in an alien destitute to compete with a workman oft his country, it was equally wrong to bring in alien labourers under contract to take the place of Bsitish workers locked out or on strike. (Hear, hear.) He welcomed the challenge to the democracy thrown down by the House of Lords. If they were prepared to fight the Labour party, the latter were pre- pared to fight the peers. If the House of Lords thought that they were going to ride roughshod over the democracy of this country they were very much mis- taken, and they would have to fight. it would be a (hort, sharp fight, but he was confident that the Labour party would come out on top, for the House of Lords was out of place in the legislation of the present day, Aspplause).</p>
        <p>* * lir. Robert Cameron, the respected member for the Houghton-le-Spring division of Durham who is so well known to Warringtonians, was on Saturday the recipient of hearty congratulations and wishes for his good health from the Liberal Association of his con- stituency. It is understood that when Mr. Cameron ceases to represent Houghton the seat will go to the Labour party and the suggestion was recently made that there was ne• reason for continuing the Liberal Association. It was, however, decided to reconstitute the Associa- tion, and on Saturday, an adjourned beating was held at Sunderland for that purpose. Colonel Fenwick, who was elected president, said that the presence of so many Labour members in the House gave satis- faction to them all in the North. So far from the , Labour party being anarchical and desiring to burn ! and kill everybody, as some supposed, they were intellectually as, able and as staunch, and probably a great deal more hard-working, than any other class , in the House.</p>
        <p>• • ••1•1=1.•..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>ST HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST HELENS.</p>
        <p>St. HELENS BICYCLE THIEF SENTENCED. John Allen Naylor, a blacksmith's striker, of 51, Talbot-street, pleaded guilty, at St. Helens on Monday, to stealing a bicyle on •Saturday night from a shop in Liverpool-road. The evidence was that Mr. James Nock, cycle dealer and tobacconist, of Liverpool-road, put his bicycle in his shop, and ten minutes afterwards missed it. Naylor was afterwards found in possession of the bicycle. The police said that Naylor had been con- victed three or four times, and he had served twenty- eight days for stealing a bicycle. That was not his first "temptation."—The bench inflicted a punishment of six months' hard labour.</p>
        <p>AN UNHAPPY ST. HELENS HOME. The affairs of Mrs. Lilian Crouch, of 101. Eldon-street, and her husband and family were very fully investigated at St. Helens Police Court on Monday, when Mrs. Crouch was in the dock on a charge of being incapably drunk. The Clerk said Mrs. Crouch on Saturday morn- igg applied for a warrant for an aggravated assault by her husband.—lnspector Luff said that on Saturday night he was called to the woman's house. She was drunk, and her children were in a most neglected and wretched condition, and at the request of the father he removed them to the Workhouse. The woman's husband gave evidence and blamed his wife for all the trouble.—Mr. Holmes, the police court missionary, said he had done his best to help the woman, but she gave way to drink, and she complained that her husband gambled. Her husband had been brutally cruel to her, even according to his own statement.—The bench dis- missed the charge of assault, and and fined the woman for drunkenness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>YEAR'S HEALTH AT ST. HELENS. – –</head>
        <p>A YEAR'S HEALTH AT ST. HELENS. - -</p>
        <p>- -- Dr. Buchan, medical officer of health for St. Helens, on Wednesday submitted to the Health Committee the annual report on the health of the borough for 19011, which was ordered to be printed for the use of members. Dr. Buchan states that the birth-rate was 36*05 per 1,000, which is again one of the highest recorded in the great towns. The death-rate was 17.21 per I,ooo—the lowest on record—and the infantile mortality rate was 132 per 1,000 births, which is also the lowest on record. The prevalence of infectious disease was low, that of typhoid fever being the lowest in the records of the town. AM these results are exceedingly satisfactory, the remarkable reduction in the death-rate with the low infantile mortality rate and the maintenance of a high birth-rate, being specially gratifying. The population is estimated at 90.934. There were 529 marriages during the year, a rate of 11.70. The average infantile mortality rate for the past ten years was 171, but during the last year the rate has been 132, as compared with 174 in the previous year. Only 2•8 of the 3,375 i children born in the town n the year have been unvac- cinated. The prevalence of typhoid fever during 1905 was the lowest on record, and for the first time the mortality rate for this disease was lower than the rate for England and \Vales. THE BISHOP OF LIVERPOOL AT ST. HELENS. The Bishop of Liveriool spent the greater part of Sunday in the neighbourhood of St. Helens. In the morning he preached at Holy Trinity Church, Parr-1 maunt, where there was a large congregation. The Rev.l J. M. Courtenav (vicar), conducted the service, assisted' by the Rev. R. S. Davies. The Bishop, in his sermon, said he was afraid the tendency of the times was to reverse the command of God and to put the duty to our neighbour before the duty to God. As they looked abroad they saw how, acting under this impulse, men were ready to subscribe money to hos- pitals, to homes for children, and to the needs of those who were in poverty and sickness, but it was very difficult ioa obtain from them money for the building of churches, for the support of more clergy, and for the extension of Christ's kingdom at home and abroad. One 1 reason why the commandments were reversed was that it was easy to see their duty to their neighbour, and it was somewhat difficult to realise their duty to God. In the afternoon the Bishop preached at the Mission room at Laffak, connected with St. Mark's Church, Cowley Hill ; and in the evening he addressed a very large congregation in the newly-formed parish of St.. Mark's, Haydock. ST. HELENS GLASSNIAKERS' WAGES. A TEMPORARY SETTLEMENT.</p>
        <p>• The sheet glass workers employed by Messrs. I Pilkingtou Brothers, St. Helens, held a meeting on ' Saturday night to receive a reporr on the negotiatiors I that had been carried on with tne firm during the welt ith respect to an all-round increase of 5 per cent. in wages which had been applied for, as well as an altera- tion in the form of contract under which the men work. The previous Saturday the men were met by members of the firm, and the" flatteners " and " blowers " were given the increase asked for, but it was denied to the " gatherms." The gatherers are an indispensable section of the sheet glassmakers, and a great deal depends on their skill and judgment. They extract .the molten," metal" from the kilns on the long blowpipes and have to judga the quantity and also " gather " the glass in such a way that it will blow out into a long cylinder tube free from blisters add other imperfec- tions. Deductions are made on account of blisters, and the blowers have in the main to depend on the gatherers for freedom from blisters, which mean deduction from the rates paid for the "pieces," as the glass cylinders are called. During the present negotiations the rates of deduction have bean under review, and the firm have agreed to some modification in the deductions. During the week the firm granted the increase to the" gatherers " of 21-ounce glass, but refused it to the 15-ounce men. The, whole position was con- sidered on Saturday night, whbn the men decided to accept the terms offered, but expressed dissatisfaction that the firm had declined to grant the increase to the 15-ounce gatherers, hand and machine. A resolntion also expressed regret that they had not agreed to revise the contract as requested by the men, and asked for this disappointment to be noted. For the time being the terms may be considered as accepted, but the com- munication to the firm states that in no sense must it be understood that the men have relinquished the strong sense of the reasonableness of their application.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>WHITSUNTIDE HOLIDAYS. Where to Spend Them.</head>
        <p>THE WHITSUNTIDE HOLIDAYS. Where to Spend Them.</p>
        <p>As will be seen from our advertisement columns the London and North-Western Railway Company have arranged a large and varied list of cheap trips from their Warrington (Bank Quay) station and the attention of our readers may be drawn to some of the principle 1 trips which have been fixed :—London (by the direct route) June 2nd and 4th with bookings to the Continent. South •Coast, Brighton, Bournemouth and other principal pleasure resorts on June Ist, 2nd and 4th; South and Central Wales, June Ist and 4th ; Carlisle and principal Scotch stations, June Ist, 6th and 7th by the West Coast Mail Route; York. New- castle and North Eastern line, June 2nd ; West of England. Juno Ist and 2nd by the direct route ria Severn Tunnel ; Dublin, Cork, Killarney, and other stations in the interior of Ireland by the short soli route /la Holyhead, June Ist, 2nd, 4th, and every day in Whit week. The local tripper is also well catered for, cheap excursions having been arranged to Blackpool, North Wales coast stations, Southport, Liverpool, New Brighton, Enstham and Douglas, either via Liverpool or Fleetwood. The usu d week-end short and long date tickets to the seaside and other pleasure resorts will be issued on Friday, June Ist, and Saturday, June 2nd, the short date tickets being available for return up to and including the following Wednesday. The Great Western Railway Company also announce splendid facilities for visiting the various popular resorts on their system. Special attention is paid to Llangollen, Corwen, Bala. Dolgelly, Barmouth, and Aberystwyth. Week-end tickets are offered at greatly reduced rates and day trips are announced for June 4th, 7th, Bth, and oth. Details will be found in our advertis- ! ing columns.</p>
        <p>OIDIMER NOVELTIES. GRAND SRLECTION BLOUSES, GIAWES. SKIRTS, PRINTS, SUNSEIADES, &amp;c., Scc v. T. WILLI&amp;%t?, WHITE 11017:3E, SI &amp;NKEY St, WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>STRANGE STORY OF A BABY.</head>
        <p>STRANGE STORY OF A BABY.</p>
        <p>At Wednesday's meeting of Lamboth Board of Guardians it was reported that a boy Hrought to the workhouse a well-dressed child. He stated that on one of the platforms at Waterloo railway elation a young woman asked him to hold the baby while she went to purchase a ticket. The boy took the child, and waited for nearly an hour, and as the woman did not put in an appearance a polipe-constable recommended him to take the child to the Workhouse. 1151</p>
        <p>- ,</p>
        <p>CRISP AND CREAMY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>N otice. The Warrington "Examiner" js now published on Friday afternoon, price one penny, and is on sale at the various newsagents at about 4 o'clock. Should any difficulty be experienced in getting the paper the proprietors will undertake to &amp;liver a copy at any Address in Warrington by 5 o'clock on Friday after- noon without extra charge. Address all co:maul:tin, tions to" Examiner Office," Bewsey-street Warrington The Prosecution of a Councillor. The Liverpool Quarter Sessions, at which the charge of alleged corrupt practices against Councillor Arthur Peacock is down for hearing, open on Tuesday next. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, LINDER THE CLOCK. BRIDOE-STREET.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Ragged School. A special service was held at the Ragged School on Sunday last,the occasion being the annual distribution of prizes for regular attendance and good conduct,and the number of prizes gained afforded ample evidence 'of the good work the school is doing. The distribu- tion was made by Mrs. Dodds, who also gave an interesting and helpful address which was much apprisCiatecl by the scholars and workers. Memorial to Fallen Soldiers. We understand that Ur. Alfred Drury, A.T?..A.., the sculptor entrusted with the commission for the memorial to local soldiers who fell in the war in South Africa, has completed the full-sized model and has invited inspection by ,members of the Soldiers' Memorial Fund Committee. Messrs. Singers, of Frome, have undertaken to cast the statue in bronze. The Committee will shortly meet to make arrangements for the unveiling of the statue. The Proposed Cycle Parade. • A meeting will be held at the Patteu Arms hotel this (Friday) evening to elect officers and sub-com- mittees to take in hand the arrangenurts for the Fancy Dress Parade to be held in July in aid of the Infirmary extension fund. Mr. W. P. Rylands has accepted the invitation of the committee to become the President of the Parade and amongst those who have notified their willingness to act as vice-preshients are: Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., the Mayor ()Alderman Bolton), Mr. J. Charlton Parr, and Mr. F. Monks. Local Band's Success. Messrs. Crosfield's band competed in a contest held at Damen on Saturday and obtained third place. The order of merit was distinctly interesting. The first and second places were won by Shaw and Horwich Hall respectively, the fourth and fifth by Rochdale 4 Public and Goodshaw, while the sixth place fell to Wincrates Temperance who, it will be remembered were second in the Crystal Palace competition of last year for the 1,000 guinea trophy. Jr, R. WOO D, Piano Dealer and Tuner, 47, Old London-road., Stockton Heath.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Beautifying Industrial Centres. tfany societies in different parts of the country in • various ways, are tiorking towards making our cities ' and large towns as beautiful as circumstances will allow. It has been thought that a Conference of those interested in this object might promote an interchange of ideas which would be very helpful to workers. The Lord Mayor of Manchester has expressed his approval of tips Conference, and has kindly given the use of the Mayor's Parlour at the Town Hall for Tuesday, June _Nth. The following four subjects have been proposed for discussion : —l. How. to interest children in the cult of the beautiful; 2, How town-dwellers may make their homes, small garden-plots, and back- yards beautiful; 3, What rnunicipalties may do to' give the towns a more country aspect ; 4, How our taWns may be made brighter and cleaner by the abatement of smoke, dust, &amp;c. The Secretary, Canon Morley. Stevenson, will be glad to receive suggested natnes of speakers for any of these subjects and to give any information he can. Six-GUINEA CYCLES. Excellent value. Call and inspect.—J. S. GarmAND,5l to 55, Bridge-st.—Advt. Music in the Park. The opening of the season on Sunday afternoon was marred by wintry weather, and there was'only.a small attendance to hear the selections by the Volunteer Band. The following programme was submitted in highly creditable style :—March, " Vent Vidi Vici," (flawkins) ; overture, " Latona " (L. Ham); selec- tion, " Souvenir de Meyerbeer " (Swift); duet, " Excelsior" (Balfe), sOlotsts, Messrs. Finch and Rutter ; selection, " The Bouquet " (Ditlgood ; march, " 0.H.M.5." (flume); "God Save the King." The band was conducted by Mr. J. T. Ricardson.— On Thursday next the Legh-street Brass Band occupy the stand in the Park at 7.30 pan., and will render the following programme,fMr. Fred Podmore conducting :—March, " Yeomen 'of the King," J. Ord. I Hums; intermezzo, " Thei Setting Sun " ; Morc,eau, H' The Turkish Patrol," Michaelis ; cornet solo, " Spring Song," Mendelssolin, Mr. A. Landor (from Songs without Words) ; waltz, "Amorous° "; duet, 1" The Village Blacksmith," Messrs. H. Banner and IW. Yates ; march, " Viscount Nelson," W. Zehle ; God Save the King" On Thursday evening the bandstand was occupied by Mr. T. J. Down's military band under the con- ductorship of Mr. A. Down. Rain considerably inter- fered with the pleasure of the proceedings. The pro- gramme was published last week. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASTIIRE.—Advt.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S CREAM CRACKERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Rain on Saturday prevented any play on the part of local enthusiasts, and all matches had to be abandoned. The second of the games in the Works' Competition, between Smethurst's and C.L.C. was productive of great excitement, the railway men winning by two runs. To-night's (Friday) match is between Corpara- tion (Gas and Trains) and the St. Helens Cable Co.</p>
        <p>Bowling. Rain interfered with the progress of the first round of the Warrington Works Competition on Saturday with the result that only one match was decided. This was between C.L.C. and Longford Wire and resulted in an easy win for the railway men. On the first two games they obtained a lead of 22 points and this they gradually increased ultimately winning by 49 chalks. As a general run C.L.C. men were much ahead of there opponents. The best game of the afternoon Isy between D. Bromley and J. Bartley. The C.L.C. man early on obtained a lead of 3-0 but with a double and a single Bartley equalised and then went to the front. Peeling at 4,5, 6 and 8, Bromley again took up the running and carried the score to 14-11 when Bartley secured the jack and with three singles obtained one of the four victories recorded by the losing kam.</p>
        <p>To-day (Saturday)the first ronnd will be continued when the following matches will be played: Rylands (No. 3), v. Robinsons ; Armitage and Rigby v. Penketh; L. and N.W., v. Crosfield's (Nu. 2), and Guest BrOs. v. Whitecross (No. 1).</p>
        <p>It is unfortunate that only seven clubs have seen their way to become identified with the Warrington and District League, especially as this year the competition is to be conducted on the knock-out principle. One local ors.anisation, Penketh Park has seceded whilst two new clubs, Earlestown and Heatley-lane have entered and while the conipetition this year will be only small there is little doubt that in time the opera- tions of the League will be extended. The draw for the first round took place on Thursday and resulted as follows:—Cemetery v. Heatley on Sankey Chapel House green, Earlestown v. Sankey, on the Cemetery ' Green, and Bulls Head v. Horse and Jockey on the Farmers Arms green; Pearnhead were given a bye. The round is to be completed on or before June 23rd.</p>
        <p>1 The Cemetery • Club this year are largely devoting their attention to the Lancashire and Cheshire County Championship, which they mean to bring to Warrington 'On Saturday they successfully negotiated the second round, winning in a surprisingly easy fashion against I Liam. The match was played at Manchester, and the heavy rain which fell was not sufficient to damp the , ardour of the Cemetery men, who carried all before them and won every single game, ultimately claitahag a ; big margin, although the absence of one of their team ' forfeited 21 points to Irlam.</p>
        <p>Cycling. Seven runs cancelled out of ten is the Warrington Cycling Club's record for this season. Really the , weather will have to improve, if we are to have any cycling at all. The miserable conditions on Saturday made it quite impossible to venture on the run to Chester, and so threatening was the outlook on Tuesday evening that the favourite fixture of " Budworth via Arley' MS also abandoned. To-day's run is to Cranage, and on Tuesday the destination is Glazebury—always, I of mune, weather permitting !</p>
        <p>----- To-day the Sankey Wesley Guild Club go to Arley Mill. This is one of the most picturesque spots itt Cheshire, and the route lies through some delightful. country. A good muster is conlideatly expected.</p>
        <p>Association Football. The annual meeting of the Warrington and District Association League should have been held on Thursday evening but owing to the illness of the secretary, Mr. C.l'. Co; the proceedings had to be postponed. In the place of the meeting an enjoyable smoking concert was. held at the Marquis of Granby at which songs were given by Mesh. Wyatt, Dennett, J. Nfoorfield, Hawthorn, H. Leigh and Foulkes. Mr. Jackson acting as accompanist. Much pleasure is felt in League circles at the acceptance of the presidency by Mr. James Dodds, who has signified his interest by announcing that he will present a shield for the champion club. In the past the need for some such trophy has been keenly felt and Mr. Dodds' generous provision will result in an increased interest in the League tournament.</p>
        <p>It is to be regretted that Mr. T. P. Bradshaw has resigned the position of chairman, and the Warrington and District Association will also suffer by his retire- ment from the office of secretary of that organization. Mr. Bradshaw for many years has -taken an active interest in the progress of the association code in Warrington and it will be diffinit to find a successor who will enter upon the works with an equal amount of enthusiasm and ability.</p>
        <p>Water Polo St. Helens hare established their right to enter the first round proper of the English Cup competition. They met Burslem in the preliminary skirmish on Tuesday and triumphed by seven goals to nil. Imme- diately on starting St. Helens pressed. but Rushton kept a splendid goal, and saved time after titue. Eventu- ally, Lomax, after severe pressure, put, through for the home side,, and followed this up by scoring another couple of goals in trick succession. The defence of the visitors was very shaky. Close on half-time Heaton added another, bringing the score to .1-0. Burslem strove hard to reduce the lead, but their forwards missed several easy chances. Ripley received the ball, tricked several players, and finished up with a terrific shot, which added the fifth goal. Continuing to press, the home team added two more goals, and retired winners by seven goals to none.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>(ConCaut.,l from. Pax 2.) Preachers for Sunday.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptist Church : 10.30, Mr. W. Carter; 6.0, Mr. W. Arnold. Golborpe-street Baptist Chnrch : 10.30, Rev. U. Dunnico(" The Creed of the Christian ") ; 6.30, Rev. H. Donnie() ("The Creed of the Fool.") Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church : 10.30, Rev. S. Pitt; 6.30 : Rer. W. Mellor.</p>
        <p>An open meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society will be held on Sunday afternoon. Mr. J. W. Slack will pre- side, and an address will be delivered by Mr. Dunkerley, of Ashton-under-Lyne. Solos will be given by Miss Marie Houghton. Wycliffe Church : 10.30 and G. 30. Rev. John Yonge. Evening subject, " Christian responsibility for sweated industries and drugged people."</p>
        <p>The Sunday school anniversary in connection with Bold-street Wesleyan Church will be celebrattd by the holding of special services, at which the Ripv. William Potts! of Nottingham, 'will be tee preacAer. At the morning and evening services, special anthems will be given by the choir. A dower service will be held in the afternoon at which an address will be given by Mr. J. W. Deakin. S)loe be given by Miss Carrie Hastings and Mr. A. Weer and violin solos by Mr. R. H. Pearson.</p>
        <p>Sunday School Anniversary. " The anniversary services in connection with the Latchford Primitive Methodist Sdnday School were held on Sunday, when two sermons were preached, morning and evening, by Mr. S. Drdught, of Widnes. In the afternoon an address was given by the Rev. P. Vaughan, Councillor Luke Ellison occupying the chair. On Itionday the Christi&amp;t Endeavourerspf the above place, under the presidency of Mr. Moulton, gave an admirable concert. the Rev. C. L. Stowe being in the chair. The collections which were in aid of the school funds realized £5.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>EVENING CONTINUATION SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>EVENING CONTINUATION SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>Local Scholarship Winners.</p>
        <p>The following who were students at the Evening Continuation Schools last Session have been awarded scholarships tenable at the Technical Institute next Session upon the result of a reoent examination : FAIRFIELD EVENING ScnooL.—Lily Crocker, Sarah Kirkham, Lily Profit, Emily Sankey. HAMILTON-STREET EveNtNo SCHOOL.— Wilfred Altmann, Edward Byrne, Harry Dutton, Henry Garnett, Stanley E. Gleave, Charles Longshaw, Frederick W. Povey, Edward Whittaker, Thomas Wilson. HEAVISIDE EVENING SCHOOL.—Arthur Brobbin, Samuel Dolphin, John IL Grainger, Ernest Hill, Henry S. Horrabin, Samuel Hughes, William Jolley, Fred C. Massey, Fred Platt, Joseph Price, Percy Taylor, Thomas rWilson, Walter N. Winstanley. WARRINGTON BRITISH EVENING SCHOOL: Boys.— Arthur Bodle, Ernest Booth, Gough H. Brandwood, ! Harry Cmgg, Arthur Furness, Beniamin Johnson, John Keeling, Fred Kimberley, William Lowe, Arthur Mason, James A. Merchant, Robert Pepper, James H. Shakeshaft, Herbert V. Wakefield, Arthur Webb, William West, Joseph Whitmore, Harold Woodin, ; Girls.—Bertha Churm, Margaret Forster, Alice ,ronlkes, Elsie Gee, Clara Hatfield, Alice Holland, , Annie Kendall, Kate Malone, Annie Minshull, Gertrude I Moran, Lily Perry, Dorothy Robinson, An examination for the " Charles Middleton" ! (Junior) scholarships tenable at the Evening Continua- tion Schools and the Evening School of Art has recently I been held and the following have been successful, ; William J. C. Booth, Fredric Hartree, Charles E• McKean, Francis Flood, William Daley, Leopold G. J. Stamp, Charles Challoner, Sidney Groarke, Albert J. P Si gge, James Hennessey,</p>
        <p>.</p>
