      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>' THEFT FROM WHITECROSS WORKS. At the Borough Police Court on Monday, James Clough, 24,New-road,was charged with stealing between the 18th and 19th of May, from the N'Vhitecross Works, a brass step and scrap brnss, of the value of £l. Prisoner pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable stated that about five minutes past ten on Saturday night P.C. Hughcs was on duty in Crosfield-street, when he saw the prisoner, who seemed to have something bulky about his clothing. The &amp;Beer followed him, and when he got to the Drill Hall, he questioned him, and he admitted stealing the articles (produced) from Whitecross. A fine of 20s and costs (£2) or one month's imprisonment with hard labour was imposed.—The Chief Constable, during the hearing of the case, complained of the large number of robberies from local works, which were, he said, no doubt owing to the fact that there was a receiver somewhere. The police, however, had an idea who he was and his time wonld come.</p>
        <p>TIIEFT PROM 4 PUBLIC-ROI:SF. At the Warrington Police Court on Tuesday John Ward, 28, Hardy-street. pleaded guilty to having been drunk and disorderly in Winwick-street at 6.15 on Monday night and also to stealing from the Unicorn Inn a spirit glass value 2d., the property of Samuel Appleton.—Nlr. Browne, solicitor,who prosecuted,stated that at six o'clock on Monday night three men went into the Unicorn Inn. Mr. Appleton was having tea and heard the men trying to play billiards. He went and ordered them out of the house. He then noticed a spirit glass and a jug on the table,and when he returned to the room too minutes later—he had been called into the bar—he found they had gone. He went after the men up Silver-street, and two of them volunteered to return and be searched. Thereupon they went back, and prisoner produced the glass from his pocket. The jug had not been recovered. Mr. Appleton had missed in the last twelve months about £1 worth of glasses and he had taken this case up an an example. Al! the publicans in the town were subjected to a similar annoyance.—P.C. Gilchrist stated that prisoner was under the influence of drink, and on the way to the Police Station he became so violent that P.C. Hesp had to assist witnt ss.—Prisoner said he was very sorry, and would never go into a public-house again.—lle was fined 5s or seven days.</p>
      </div>
