      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <p>- -</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Ellesmere-street Bazaar. The total proceeds of this highly successful bazaar, which was brought to a close on Friday evening, amounted to £l6O. Local success at a Dog Show. The second annual Moss Side Dog Show was held on Saturday at the Botanical Gardens, Old Trafford. Among those who won first prizes were the following :- Bull dogs, D. Widnall, Warrington; schipperkes, C. Lockyer, Lymm. PREMIER CYCLES, 7,8, 10, 12 and 14 guineas Cash or 18 morahly payments. Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51, 53 and 55, Bridge-st.-Advt. Injured by a Circular Saw. On Monday evening Charles Turner (20), son of Mr. Fair Turner, of the Coach and Horses Jun, Liverpool-road,vras injured while at work at a cireular saw in one of the works of the town. His face was cut and he also sustained slight concussion of the brain. He was removed to the Infirmary where he is progressing favourably. Knocked Down by a Motor Car. Thomas Williams, aged 71, residing at 1, Charles- street, was admitted to the Infirmary on Monday morning satering from injuries to his right leg. He was crossing Winwick-street when he was knocked down by a motor car, driven by William Robbin; 14, Park Drive, Partick. His injuries were not oi a serious nature, but Williams was detained in the institution. The Swimming Club. , Considerable interest was taken in she first monthly handicap for the junior members (under 15 years) which took place at the Baths on Tuesday evening. IThere were 10 entriee, and all turned out. The he7t winners were : -First heat: 1, J. Boscow (11) ; 2, C. ; Taylor (11). Second heat : 1, Arthur Pemberton (20) ; 12 Richard Atkinson (scratch). Third heat : 1, R. I .i Chalmers (80) and H. Campbell (17). Final : 1, J. 'lBoscow ;2, R. Chalmers; 3, C. Taylor and H. I Campbell (dead heat). STILL LEADING, the aaERALD"KITCREN RANGES and " PEYERIL " Pi.:E GRATES Sole Aeon t.-J. S. GARLAND, 51 to ara, Bridge-st., Warrington.-Advt. Warrington Ragged School Band of Hope. A concert in connection with the above took place on Monday evening. Mr. W. Woods presided and his well chosen remarks were greatly. appreciated. In addition to the members the following contributed to ithe programme :-Cornet and euphonium duet Eixcelsior " Messrs. Brandwood and Faster ; songs, ; "Killarney," by Miss Howell ; " \Under the old apple ! tree "and " Alice where at thou " by Mr. Stout ; " Let her down" and "I'll open the door for you " IMr. F. Bennett. Messrs. W. Martin and J. Blakemore I were efficient accompanists. The singing of the ' Doxology brought to a close a most successful conceit UNDER TELE CLOCK for Good BOOTS.-Advt. Spiritualism. I Miss Ruth Sage, a popular young worker in the Spiritualist cause, paid her first visit to Warrington on Sunday, and at the meeting in the Druid's Hall in the afternoon, gave an address oa " Whieh is right, and which is wrong ?" She contended that we were none, of us in a position to condemn others for not doing what we think is right or not believing as we believe. We were not competent judges of our fellow-men in matters of belief or actions. In the evening the hall was crowded, when Miss Sage gave a forceful address on "Wanted, men and women "-men and women with backbone and principle; not those who merely swam with the stream and continuously bowed to authority, but those who dared to act according to , their conscience in spite of unpopularity. On Monday ! evening there was a large -attendance when Miss Sage ! gave an address on the aim of Spiritualism, followed by a considerable number of clairvoyant delineations, all except two being rec I LAWN TENNIS, CROQUET, and other outdoor sports.-J. S. GARLAND, 51,Bridge-st-Advt. Warrington P.S.A. , Mr. R. Nelson presided at the weekly meeting o the Warrington Society which was held in the Co- operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Sunday. There was a good attendance. The Chairman stated that one of the greatest needs of the present age was more sym- pathy. Life was full of difficulties, and men needed a helpinghand. They must do all they could to make life more pleasant for those with whom they had to live and work. Mr. E. Wynne Jones gave an address on" Loyalty to Jesus Christ." The only power that could purify business, regenerate social conditions, elevate our politics, and make the nation a brother- hood, not only in name but in conduct, was the power of Jesus Christ, It had been said, that the motto of the old Greek Empire was " Beauty," of the Roman Empire " Strength," but of Jesus Christ "Purity." What they wanted was, that human life, wherever lived, should be lived in nerfect loyalty to Jesus Christ. Mr. A. Pownall presided at the piano. Councillor Clegg, read the Scripture lesson, and Mr. T. Taker also took part in the service. PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER TILE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.-AdVI. Prize Distribution at the Liberal Club. The prizes won in connection with the Liberal Club Christmas and New Year billiard handicaps were distributed on Wednesday evening. The ceremony,in the absence of the president, Mr. A. H. Crosfield. was performed by Alderman Tinnion at a well attended gathering held in the billiard room, the alderman heartily congratulating the committee upon the success of the events. A vote of thanks was accorded him for his services on the motion of Mr. John Worr.ill seconded by Mr. J. T. Locker. The competitions each attracted a large number of entries, and judicious handicapping resulted in some exciting finishes. The prize winners were :- Christmas Handicap: 1, oak mounted tray and silver plated tea set, G. Simpson; 2, gold guard, S. Jakeman ; 3, case of cutlery, J. Parkinson; 4, travelling hag, J. Rudd; 5, case of silver mounted carvets, ; 6, 4 bottle dinner cruet, E. Clare; 7, silver plated rose bowl, T. Lowe; 8, silver plated sardine dish, J. Jennings; 9, slid silver handled walking stick, J. Gorst ; 10, mahogany inlaid timepiece, C. H. Hibbert ; 11, breakfast cruet, T. Pemberton; 12, clock, J. Davies; 13, letterweight timepiece, J. Sankey ; 14, timepiece. W. Langley: 15, pair of silver mounted salts, G. Baker; 15, silver plated jam dish,E. A. Sanders-New Year Handicap: 1, walnut bracket striking clock,W. Patrick; 2, gent's dressing case, W. Whittaker; 3, salad bowl and servers, H. Sommerville; 4, long gold guard. T. Locker; 5, oak mounted ink stand, W. Ford; 6, silver plated tea-pot, M. Cunningham; 7, silver plated cruet, IV. Woods; 8, pair silver mounted salts, W. Pickup. LAWN MOWERS, Garden Tools, &amp;o. Snlendid selection.-J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-at.--Advt. Local Oddfellowship. On Saturday the" Loyal Trafford" Lodge, Eccles, paid a return visit to the '6 Prince of Wales" Lodge, Warrington. H. Maddock, N.G., presided, supported by Q-. F. Freeman, P.G., T. P. Bradshaw, P.G.M., IV. Holbrook, P.D.G.M., J. Norbury, P.P.G. M., T. Reddish, P.G., A. Dempsey, P.P.G.M. (Eccles), and 13,.,everal other Eccles and Stockport officers and brethren.- Bro. H. Maddock welcomed the visitors, and hoped that they would be again spared to visit the lodge as the inter-changes of visits tended to cultivate a good feeling and were also instructive to the younger mem- bers.(Applausa.)The Grand Master of the district (Bro. T. P. Bradshaw) proposed the health of " The Loyal Trafford Lodge," and spoke in high terms of P.P.G.Y. Dempsey, who, he said, he considered would be unani- mously elected Deputy Grand Master of the order. He was justly entitled to the honour, and he would have the support of himself and Bro. Frank Webster (CS.) at the A.M.C. to be held at Barrow, in Whit week, P.P.G.M. H. Mclntyre responded, and thanked " Thel Prince of Wales" Lodge for their kindness. .He was much struck with the excellent manner in which the lodge affairs were conducted. P.P.G.11. Dempsey (Eccles) proposed "The Prince of Wales" Lodge, and gave an address on the affairs of the Unity, which he as a director, knew a great deal about. He was pleased to see the lodge's sound financial status, and he had no doubt the result of the recent quinquennial [valuation veould show the lodge quite solvent with a fair surplus. (Applause.) He also thanked the Grand Master of the District for his promised suppott at the A.M.C. He was pleased to see that the lodge had a good staff of business officers, who would no doubt later be able to assist the order by attaining higher honours. The question of juvenile lodges would be dealt with at this A.M.C., and if satis- factorily arranged would be a fillip to the strengthening of the lodges. N.G.lkladdock responded on behalf of the lodge, supported by T. Wadsworth, P.G. (lodge secretary). Songs were given by the Crosfield Qvartette Party, assisted by Bro. J. Willett, Bros. T. Reddish, R. G. Patton, E. G. Walsh, J. Mort, and T. Wolfenden (Eccles). Bro. Fred Webster was the accompanist.</p>
      </div>
