      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 26, 1906. OUT OF CHAOS INTO---? THE FREE CHURCHES': SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 26, 1906. OUT OF CHAOS INTO---? THE FREE CHURCHES': SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>• THE EDUCATION BILL.</p>
        <p>The interest of the Free Churches is keenly •xctited by the Government's Education Bill. TM,.._next few weeks, or even months, are bound to be critical times. A special meeting of the General Committee of the National Free Church Council has just been held to consider what action should be taken in refer- ence to the Bill. The committee believes it to be a sincere attempt to establish a national and just system of educations and that it goes far towards carrying out the will of the people as expressed at the last General Election; but, although it is recognised that the Bill as a whole should receive the hearty support of the Free Churches, it is felt that certain sections are open to grave objection. The committee, therefore, feel constrained to promote such amendments as will bring the details of the Bill into better correspondence with its prin- ciples</p>
        <p>DEEP -SEA WATER FREE OF MICROBES One of the curious facts noted 'among results of the Prince of Monaco's deep-sea soundings in the Mediterranean, was that at the bottom of the Mediterranean Sea the tenterature of the water was at 15dez. above zero (Centigrade) at the very lewest, whereas, in the larger oceans, such as the Atlantic, the tramperature of the water at a depth of 10,000 ft. or 12,000 ft is scarcely. Vest. above zero. Another interesting discovery made by the Prince's expedition was that though the water of,the sea near the mouth iq rivers was usually full of microbes, ad though harmful germs were even found upon the sur- face of the sea, in mid-ocean, at a depth of about 3,000 ft., sea water is absolutely sterile and con- tains no microbes whatever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>[By Mr. H. Whitham.]</head>
        <p>[By Mr. H. Whitham.]</p>
        <p>I have already written you in Warrington several letters and quite a number of postcards have added to Uncle Sam's revenue, , but, if they have ever left this most calamitous-looking city or not, is unknown to me. Judging from what I see going on at the various mail centres, I rather doubt it. You might think from the above that I was in San Francisco. I , am not. I took all I possibly could out of the city, leaving two daughters there, one of whom is a • hospital nurse, the other married, and her home is safish. Out of a population of 400,000 it is now acknowledged that at least 300,000 have left, and : there are ,many thousands 'being cared for in the 1 Golden Gate park. I havp had a walk with an occasional ride on a car, and a Oft on a waggon through the city. And what a i sight it is! A few years ago the City Fathers estab- lished a fire lirrit, with the view of limiting as much as possible the spread of fire. Consequently as fire destroyed the older buildings, and, as increased busi- ness demanded more warehouse and storage room, brick, granite, steel-frame sky-scrapers, and 'an alto- gether first-class style and quality of building were giving us as fine a city as the United States had within its borders, and now to see it as one walks through the city—it is just pitiful, for city there is none. Steel frames stand up here and there, skeletons to look at. Possibly these may be declared safe, and the work of finishing them start at once. Immense dead walls and buildings that once stood ten, twelve and fourteen storeys high are standing on every block and at every corner, a menace to the workers and the sightseer. These will be dealt with by the dynamiters when their turn comes. Relic hunters abound and of theives there is no scarcity. Hundreds of safes may be seen among the ruins. These are waiting for the expert at safe opening, and judging from the ad, that are seen at every corner we must have had a good supply of cracksmen on hand in the city. As the safes cool and the debris is removed steam derricks place them to be opened. In one district 576 safes and vaults were opened and in not more than 60 per cent of these were the contents found intact ,In one instance a safe was dragged out and when cool enough, opened. There was i". 8,000 worth of jewellery .n— or should have been. There was nothing but a pile of dust and ashes. The insidespalls had melted ,like so much fat. TIN safe was purchased as fireproof. The safe ques- tion has been a very serious one to business men. The fire, and the blowing up, of buildings, seems to have made a greater impression in the minds of the people than even the quake. Immediately after the quake we could move about but as the fire made its way, beginning near the docks, and slowly yet surely making its way among the warehouses and striking its way into the great shopping districts it soon became impossible. Dynamite was largely used, but no matter the fire swept ton until one side of the city.was laid in ashes, and an entire population had lost all they had. While this was going on in the down-town pertion of the city,the fire was creeping around what is known as the North ' Beach and the district in which I first lived oncoming here was in its grasp. On it swept up the hills, and getting around to the well-to-do districts it swept its way up Nob Hill, and away went first-class hotels and the mansions of the wealthy, until exhausted and having burned its supply of fuel up it left us hundreds of small heaps of smouldering fires leaping into blaze with each gust of wind that came from the ,ocean. Saab ;a_ a brief description of the fire that in extent and dollar-cost surpassed the historical fire of Chicago. But Chicago had no earthquake to think of. Behind our big fire we had something else to think of ' • not an accident that the foresight of man could make provision for, but an earthquake that no insurance society take account of again,--soinething that neither fire proof buildings nor steel skeleton frames can guarantee immunity from. Alone in a workhouse at 5.15 on the morning of the 18th of April it came with no warning and in 50 or 60 seconds it placed me in a state of total darkness, with the floor rising and falling ~and cases of goods rattling. My state cap be better imagined than described. It took me at least 15 minutes to find and open a door,and then what a sight lay before me. All around the railroad tracks were showing huge humps and the rails were twisted into all kinds of fantastical shapes. In front of me a string of loaded cars lay on their sides, and a silence not at all natural in the busiest freight yard west of the Rocky Mountains prevailed. No, not at all natural ; it was decidedly unnaturaLand it made one feel creepy. Of course my first anxiety was : What of my family? Making my arrange- ments as soon as possible I started to walk ho e, and my home was a long way off. Street cars had ?topped just as the clocks had stopped and just as the electric lights had stopped ; all were switched off at 5.15. On my way home sigbts came in my way- I shall never forget. The streets were crowded with half-naked people. No one had bad time to dress. Blankets and sheets were wrapped around in the hurry to escape from the falling buildings. Here I saw house after house just squashed flat. There a house with the whole front down; the Free Library of the district lifted bodily half out into the street. And the horror of it was that a great number of these collapsed houses held tight scores of living, mained and dead. I scarcely think they will ever know how many were killed', as San Francisco has a very large transient population. At length I reached my own home. To my surprise I found my wife completely dressed and the girls partially, and all in the street. Indeed I am not sure, but I think my wife had her gloves on. When I questioned her she said she had been cool over the whole trouble. They said they had found themselves on the floor. How they got there they didn't know, but the severe shake was over, and as for the slight tremors, well she was used to them, and the time to quit had not come yet, and she might as well make a good job of the dressing. And so we were together again and went into the house and viewed the situation. It was a wreck that met our gaze. The furniture, ornaments and book. cases all lay in heaps on the various floors. Plaster all on the heaps, and confusion seemed to be in a most confounded state. Doors that once were a fairish fit were now jammed and doors that never would fit were now just perfeete But there was only one thing to be done, and tlAt was, quit. Easier said than done! By this time it was becoming very clear to us all that thousands were in the same predicament and in another day it was a case of a good many thousands wanting homes, and from now on for two weeks we had a great experience. Tents and wooden shacks began t spring up on all the hill sides. A very many people had during the first two nights to sleep on the ground. We found a home with one of my daughters who lived among the hills a good mile away. To those of us who had escaped with our lives, troubles without end came I and had to be met. The earthquake had ' bnrst the water pipes or disrupted them and wasl as bad an enemy as the fire had been, for we wanted ,• it and the water, if we were to eat and live. Fires I were not allowed, but now we have at the front of every tent, shack and houQe the best arrangements I that he who would eat could erect for cooking. The ! feeding of all these thousands of homeless ones has been a big and difficult problem to solve' as also has it been a bit of sharp work to keep the rough and tough element in order. It has been accomplished as ' well as human thought and foresight could possibly do it. There is here what we call a large foreign element, no need to name the nationality, that has ; behaved rather hoggishly in the matter of getting food. At the various centres where food is given Out! and where we all stand in lines, and sometimes very I long lines, these selfish hogs put in all the membeis ! of their families and all their time, and where the police have failed to raid, are certainly provided for, as far as tinned food is concerned, for the next six months. Soldiers, marines and police have done good work —a few exceptions in the way of shooting good ' citizens are to be deplored, hut we have had a very exceptional time. For my own family we are now located in the home of a very nice family who kindly offered us a share of their home. We paid about Y.,172. to have what we had saved removed, and my wife is now in another friend's home doing her washing. We ate living " across the bay " and I am told we are at least ten miles from our late home. It is a nice and very quite i town, flowers in such abundance that it reminds me . somewhat of Hale. They are now busy building the : chimneys as the earthquake got in its dreadful work over a great stretch of country. I should like to have told of the quake at the famous Standard University where very, very, costly buildings were destroyed, but I must now get to something else. I have seen no " Examiner "since the first letter of mine on Canada, nor any mail whatever, since the middle of April. H. WHITHAM, 12855, Jackson-street, Alamada, California. May 4th, 1906.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>'SOME OBJECTI- ONS.</head>
        <p>'SOME OBJECTI- ONS.</p>
        <p>' The committee passed a resolution explain- ing why they objected to Clause 4. Among the reasons given is that the permission to teachers to give denominational teaching is in itself absolutely fatal to the existence of the clause. It claims that the clause is an indirect viola- tion of the three principles on which the Bill ,is based—(a) That of the abolition of tests; for tests must be enforced where the provision as to four-fifths is carried out. (b) That of full popular control, for such control would be practically in the hands of the denominationa- lists. (c) That of relieving the citizen from the compulsion to pay any sectarian rate, for these schools are on the rates. The committee further says that it introduces into legislation the false principle of giving the parent, as parent, an additional direct control beyond that which he exercises as a citizen; and this inflicts an injustice on other citizens, and it makes inevitable the canvass'.ng of the parents. known to be so injurious to the self-respect of the citizens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>SUMMER WORK.</head>
        <p>SUMMER WORK.</p>
        <p>A little known department of the work of the National Free Church Council is the mis- sion van, which in the summer-time of every year tours through ,the smaller villages in the country, under the charge of the Rev. Jesse Mayo. In spite of the variable weather we have been experiencing, the van has already made a start in Somerset, and reports state that the visits have bpen much appreciated, crowds gathering at the different centres, and the local ministers well supporting the eforts of Mr. Mayo. The van is at present working in connection with the Chard, Yeovil, and Gillingham Councils. Many Free Church Councils, in all parts of England and Wales, are now completing their arrangements for open-air services during the summer months. This is now part of the regular work of the Councils, and is of exceptional value in reach- ing those who do not attend regular Church services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>GIPSY SMITH IN AMERICA.</head>
        <p>GIPSY SMITH IN AMERICA.</p>
        <p>For some time arrangements have been in hand for a great mission to be condueted in ,the United States by Gipsy Smith. The (*Tv's tour in South Africa made a prominent im- pression on that vast country, and the previous visits which he has made to America were all immensely successful. / He will sail in the middle of September, and coMmence missioning in October. He will spend five months in the States, and will probable start with a month in Brooklyn, then, possibly, proceeding to New England for two months; the balance of time is to be devoted to the West. The organisation of the meetings will be in the hands of the National Congregational Evangelistic Com- mittee, of which Dr. Hillis is the chairman.</p>
        <p>REV. J. 11. JOWETT, The Spring Sessions of the Congregational Union Were made memorable by the Presiden- tial address of the Rev. J. H. Jowett, M.A. Mr. Jowett is one of the foremost preachers amAg the Free Churches. He has been in- vited to the pastorate of Christ Church, West- minster-bridge-road, which Oe Rev. F. B. Meyer vacates next May, ill order to devote kis time to work among the Free Church Councils. Mr. Jowett, after a few clays' de- liberation, has intimated that he cannot accept the invitation. He is already minister of a very flourishing Church--Carr's-lane, Birming- ham, where he succeeded the well-known theo- iogian and preacher, Dr. Dale.</p>
        <p>DR. HORTON ON BUSINESS LIFE. Amon z our leading preachers, few, if any, can write and speak better on devotional and mystical topics than Dr. Horton, the ex-Presi- dent of the National Free Church Council. His volume, " The Open Secret," it is widely felt, will rank with the great classical devo- tional manuals. Dr. Horton, however, can be as practical as any man. He contributes to this month's " Frec Churchman " a common- sense _paper on" The Christian ;-,nd BUS:11CIS." Dr. Horton does not deny adherence to prin- ciples may involve business failure. "I say it, he says, gfeeling the extreme difficulty of speaking to men who are often exoosed to great temptations, but I say it with Jeep con- viction, it is always Possible that the true man and the good will have to be ruined, and it is always necessary that the- true man and the good prefer ruin to one slight clevation from the path of simple iruth and righteousness."</p>
        <p>SABBAI'I3 013$ERVANCE. The United Conference on Sabbath. Obser- vance, held at the Chston Hall, Westminster, •recently; should do goad... On that occasion the Archbishop of Canterbury presAetl, and among the speakers, were latters as diverse in type and .order as Rev. .1. L'Og2t7; Mit.. the President of the National. Free Church Coun- cil, Bishop Johnson of the Roman Catholic Church, Mr. Will CAolcs, M.P.., the Duke et Norfolk (England's prini2i Roman Catholic layman), Dr. Horton,. and Mr. J. Compton Rickett, M.P. It is intended to hold a Campaign on the Sabba;ll ,Quekicii • some timetouring the autumn Or ,tv.nter, most prob- al4 in Novembc.r.. A, srong emumittee, com- posed of Angl.'eans,— Free Churchmen, and Labour leader 4,:., has been formed, to carry out this campaign!'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>CRURCH I,DERATION ABROAD.</head>
        <p>CRURCH I,DERATION ABROAD.</p>
        <p>In this month's issue of tile " Free Church Chronicle informat:on fs g.vcii as to the pro- gress of Church Federation abroad. An illus- trated article deals w:th the International Church Commiwion of Maine, U.S.A., an insti- tution which, although rto't quite en the tines of the Federation movement in England, is doing similar Work with Considerable success. The article also describes the work of the Wit- wabersrand Church Council, covering Johan- nesburg and district. The Education fight and the Sabbath question are equally with the people of Johannesburg as with those at home, and the Council has had to take action on lonese, among other question.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>THREE MEN KILLED.</head>
        <p>THREE MEN KILLED.</p>
        <p>At Ashton-under-Lyne on Wednesday a crane at a cotton mill in course of erection collapsed, four men being thrown from a height of 60 feet to the ground. Three of them died soon afterwards, and the fourth man is in a critical condition.</p>
      </div>
