      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>TO DARKEN ALUMINIUM.</head>
        <p>TO DARKEN ALUMINIUM.</p>
        <p>In order to impart a dark colour to aluminidm plate, the following process is used. The surface of the sheet to be coloured, is polished with very fine emery powder or finest emery cloth. After the polishing pour a thin layer of olive oil over the surface and heat slowly over a spirit flame. In the case of large sheets, they must, of course, be heated in the drying-oven. After a short while pour on oil again, 'in order to obtain abso- lute uniformity of the coating, and heat the plate once more. Under the action of the heat the plate turns first brown, then black, according to the degrees of heat. When the desired coloura- tion has been attained, the plate is polished over again, after cooling, with a woollen rag or soft leather.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>OLD AGE AND AUTO-SUGGESTION.</head>
        <p>OLD AGE AND AUTO-SUGGESTION.</p>
        <p>Life is not only what we make it, but its length depends very largely upon ourselves. A French psychologist, M. Finot, urges that a most important means of prolonging it is auto-sugges- tion, or will-power. He states that the possible life span can be shewn theoretically to reach 150 or even 200 years, and that practically it is much i greater than s usually imagined. Old age comes prematurely through abuse of auto-suggestion, by loss of confidence in our strength and powers. It is pre-eminently essential that we keep cheer- ful and hopeful, that we remain active, and that we make rational use of the functional organs. Statistics shew that the critical period of old age is actually between eighty and eighty-five, after which probabilities favour reaching or ex- ceeding the century mark.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>SPAWNING OF THE EEL.</head>
        <p>THE SPAWNING OF THE EEL.</p>
        <p>The spawning of the eel, and the early stages in the development of the young, were for long among the unsolved problems of natural history. It is now well known, however, that in autumn eels descend the rivers to spawn in the sea. Floating eggs have been found in the Bay of Naples and identified as those of eels. Young eels in the marine stage of their existence are also known. A peculiar group of small fishes, the Leptocephali, had_ been known since 1763, when the first species described was captured at Holyhead. These fishes, so called from the small proportional size of the head, are ribbon-like in shape, and of a glassy transparency when alive. In 1864, Gill, an American naturalist, concluded that one of the Leptccephali was the young of the conger-eel. And since then others have shewn that the whole group is made up of the young of different species of eel. Leptocephali have also been observed to change into young eels in captivity. In nature the change takes place in the sea, and in the year after the spawning, gene- rally in May, the young eels, or elvers, ascend the rivers in immense numbers.</p>
        <p>. GREY HAIR. When Byron made the "Prisoner of Chillon gay:</p>
        <p>My hair is white, but not with years; Nor grew it white in a single night, As men's have grown from sudden fears. • he was, it appears, writing within the limits of strict physical possibility, thofugh he probably knew nothing about it. Professor Metchnikoff, of the Pasteur Institute, has laid a clear state- ment on the subject before the Fretich Academy of Sciences, though it appears to be scarcely more than a theory. According to him, the whitening of the hair is neither physical nor chemical in its cause. It is chic to a vital phe- nomena, the presence pf living elements or ac- tive cells, which absorb the colouring pigments. These he names chromophagons, and as their destructive energy is more or less violently ex- cited, the blanching process proceeds with greater or less rapidity. The Professor claims to be able to produce results analogous to those of the chameleon, and to the winter blanching of certain animals and birds, and to the peculiarity of certain frogs to become white in twenty-four hours. Excessive emotion or fear may produce similar results in man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>FORAMINIFERA.</head>
        <p>THE FORAMINIFERA.</p>
        <p>There is perhaps no animal which has played a more important part in the building up of the rocky crust of the earth than the foraminifera. It is theirs to integrate the waste of the land which has been carried in solution into the sea, and build it up into solid rock. Many of the limestone rocks of the earth's crust are built up of their remains. The Nummulitie limestone, many thousand feet thick, and extending over hun- dreds of miles, and .the chalk, hundreds -of feet thick, and extending over vast areas, are largely i so composed. And curiously enough, says a Globe writer, the borings on the island of Funafuti seem to indicate that a coral reef is largely built up of foraminifera. It is usually believed that foraminifera are I mostly denizens of the deeper parts of the sea. But although individuals are more abundant in the deeper waters, species are much more nume- rous in shalloW water of Mr. Arthur Earland in collecting these organisms between tide-marks at Bognor, Sussex. His collection includes no fewer than 140 species. Of these, fifteen species are recorded for the first time in Britain, and one is a new species. Many of them are rare in Britain. Bognor thus obtains a record as a col- lecting ground for foraminifera. The young brood in two cases was found within the parent shell. Mr. Earland has given an account of his work in the Journal of the Quekett Microscopical Club.</p>
        <p>ELECTRICALLY WELDED RAIL JOINTS. A new method of electrically welding rail joints is now being employed in Germany, and has been adopted on several railways. There are three kinds of, welded joints which have come into general use in this country and 1 abroad, narhely, the Falk, or cast-welded joint; 1 the Lorain, or the joint which is electrically i welded by means of current from a transformer; I and tho Goldschmidt, or thermit-welded joint. This method does not depend upon the produc- tioh of a welding heat by- the resistance and transformer method, as in the Lorain system. 1 Instead, a high temperature is secured by the use of a large electric arc, which melts a quan- tity of steel at the point of application, namely, at the joint. The negative pole of this arc is 1 formed by the rails themselves, and the positive ' pole is a carbon supplied with direct curtent from a special generator and other apparatus provided for the purpose. The carbon or posi- tive pole is attached to a holder so that 4 can Ibe moved back and forth by hand over the I 'pieces of steel, which are to be reduced to a liquefied condition at the joint to form the weld- -Img portion. During this process the joint itself is held in a form or mould, so that as the metal melts it flows underneath and around the base and under the head of the rail. A small quan- tity of steel is first melted, and additional steel is fed into the are until enough has been melted to form the weld. The steel employed is of the same composition as that used in the rail itself, I and is obtained from old rails.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>FACTS ABOUT ANIMALS' EYES.</head>
        <p>FACTS ABOUT ANIMALS' EYES.</p>
        <p>For some years past the eminent ophthalmolo• gist, Dr. Lindsay Johnson, has been invesfigat• hig the eyes of ' animals,. and has made some valuable discoveries of great interest to zoology and our knowledge of the evolution of various animals. One of the most remarkable of these researches is a confirmation of Darwin's theory that man is closely related to the primates From his investigations •Dr. Johnson has found that ,the eyes of all the apes, including man, are prac- tically identical. Each has the highly complex system of veins and arteries, and the direct or parallel vision. According to this authority, the Oog has two ancestors, one round-eyed and the tither oval-eyed. The first is the hyena, and the latter the bear through the raccoon. All animals exposed to chase by enemies, such as the hare, rabbit, and squirrel, can see all around, and all the rodentia squint. The lower an animal in the scale, the further is its eye from parallel vision. According to this authority also, the corpus Inger, or black body of pigment, in the eye of the horse, which has proved such a source of srikulation to the naturalist, veterinarians, and zoologists, reveals through the ophthalmoscope a new means of tracing the ancestry and relatibn- Q'iip of the horse. The eye curtain is precisely the same as that which is found in all tropical rzinals, such as the onega, camel, antelope, &amp;c., and fulfils one important function—the Protce- non ot the eye troth sunught. yr. JOnnson's re- searches, according to Prof. IRay Lankester, the celebrated zoologist, will necessitate a reclassi- fication in one section of zoology.</p>
        <p>Mr DEAR CRIL,</p>
        <p>Many of you haie sent me essays on "!Spring," during the last few weeks, and I am ileaed to find that some are very good indeed. Next week I shall give the names of the winners, and the prize compositions. I must give just a word about the Easter Holiday Competition. I was pleased that I had such a large number of replies, but many were too stilted—l mean that the letters were simply a string of events ; this is all very well, but I want more than that.</p>
        <p>If you were talking to a friend and telling him about your holidays, you would" not only gve him detags, but would tell it in a more chatty way. I hope you will all bear in mind to write me nice chatty letters, as though you were talking to me. The prize this week hae*been forwarded to Miss France Kirby, With all good. wiales from</p>
        <p>UNCLE NED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <p>-.4.---</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l received my album a few weeks ago, and lam very pleased with it. I am sure it is very kind of you to sehd them. I have decided to send you a story called "Tell the Truth," hoping you will accept it with pleasure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>TELL THE TRUTH.</head>
        <p>TELL THE TRUTH.</p>
        <p>There was once a little boy whose name was George, and his father one day gave him a small axe to play with. George was greatly pleased with• it, and took it with him to the garden. He roamed about, trying it upon nearly everything that came in his way. At last he came to a young cherry-tree, and began to try the axe on it. He soon made some deep cuts in the tree; and then he walked away to another part of the garden. The cherry-tree was greatly prized by his father, but George forgot all about that. By and by his father came to the part of the garden where the tree stood. He was very sorry when he found his fine cherry-tree almost ruined. " George!" said he, sternly, to his son, "who has done this?" Did George answer,'" I don't know, father"? Oh, no. Did he lay the blame on any one else? Oh, no. W:th tears in his eyes he looked up to his father's face. " Father," said he, "I can't tell a lie—l did it." Noble boy! He was not afraid to tell the truth; and his father at once forgave him. -</p>
        <p>Boys are often tempted to speak what is not the 'truth. When that happens let each one think of George, and try like him to say, "I can't tell a lie."</p>
        <p>Speak the truth, and speak it ever, Cost it what it will. He who hides the wrong he did Does the wrong thing still.</p>
        <p>The boy of this story became one of the greatest men that ever lived. He was George Washington. Froxv• your loving Niece, FRANCES KIRBY.</p>
        <p>My dear Uncle Ned,—f received your lovely prize eafely. I was so pleased with it. I never expected one at all; tharfk you so much. I was staying at Mansfield when it arrived, but Father cent it on to me.</p>
        <p>I have not seen this week's paper yet, as I il cannot get one here. I enjoy reading your pa.per ry much. With est love. Thanking you once again for your nice prize. . I remain, Your loving Niece, , MARGARET SARLL.</p>
        <p>ncle Ned,—l am sending you a nice ittle tale, hoping you will like it very much. :t is called,</p>
        <p>MAKING THE BEST OF IT.</p>
        <p>Little Minnie, only three years old, to am a home-sick cousin who was visiting at house, brought out her choicest playthin Among these was a tiny trunk, with bands gilt paper for straps—a very pretty . _ _</p>
        <p>Se her ngs.</p>
        <p>„Iret.. toy—but. Freddy- bent the lid too far 'hack and broke it off. He did not mean to do this, and when he saw what he had done he was frightened, and began to cry. Then dear little Minnie, with her own eyes 'full of tears, said: "Never mind, Ilreddy; just see what a cunning little cradle the top will make!”</p>
        <p>That was certainly a great deal better than fretting. _ _</p>
        <p>She Made the best of it.</p>
        <p>From M. WooLDRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned.—l wish to become a raem- ber of the "Children's Corner." May I do so? I am eleven years of age, and am in the Fourth Standard. I am sending you a short story. With 4ove from HORACE POTTER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>GOOD EXAMPLE.</head>
        <p>A GOOD EXAMPLE.</p>
        <p>A poor boy once went to a ragged school, where Mfg; face was well washed. " When he returned home the neighbours looked at him with astonishment. They said, " That looks like Tom Rogers, and yet it can't be, for he is co clean." Presently his mother looked at him, and seeing that his face was GO clean, she fancied that her own': face was dirty, and forthwith she washed it., The father soon came home from his work, and, seeing his wife and son so clean he tholight that his face was dirty, and so he followed their example. 'Father, mother, and son being clean, the ,rnother,began to think the room was dirty, and so she, too, speedily set to work and cleaned the floors: .</p>
        <p>A woman lived next door. Seeing so great a change in her neighbours, she thought that her face and her room were very dirty, so she went down on her kneEo and scrubbed the floor clean. This story shows how two houses and thir occupants were made tidy and comfort- able 'simply by the example of the clean face of one ragged school-boy. Children, as well as grown-up people, should always set a good example. We never know how much good may be done in that way, nor can we tell how much harp results from a bad example.,</p>
        <p>From ANNIE ROWLEY.</p>
        <p>NNW</p>
