      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>"IN A MINUTE."</head>
        <p>"IN A MINUTE."</p>
        <p>If you asked Dora to do anything she would reply, "In a minute." One day the bird was hopping about on the floor. Somebody went out, leaving the door open. Dora's mother said, "Dora, shut the door, or the cat will have the bird." "Yes, mother, in a minute; I just want to draw this line." But the cat did not wait till this was done. In it popped, and in a dart had the bird in its mouth. "In a minute" came back crying, with the bird in her hand, but it was dead. Mamma was sorry for her, but said, "A great many things happen 'in a minute.' Dora, I hope the next time you are told to do a thing you will do it at once."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>GERALD'S RIDE.</head>
        <p>GERALD'S RIDE.</p>
        <p>One day Gerald's big brother Jack went for a ride on his pony with his father, who had a lovely brown horse. When Jack came home he saw Gerald playing on the lawn. Jack called him, and asked if he would like to have a nice ride.</p>
        <p>Of course Gerald said he would, so Jack pct his little brother on the pony's back, and held him that he should not fall. They went qulte a long way, and when Jack brought him baci:, he said, "Gerald, if you will run and ask nursa to give you a lump of sugar, you shall give it to my pony, because he has given you uoll a nice ride."</p>
        <p>Gerald was rather afraid to hold the sugar; and, he was very young, but Jack said "Don't be afraid, I will hold your hand, and it will be. quite safe." So Gerald gave the pony the sugar. and he was pleased to see him eat it. , He then thanked Jack for taking him out and ran away to the nurse. Dear little readers, I hope that you will be kind to animals, though they cannot talk as we do, yet, if you watch them you will find that they wish tor make you understand how please.' they are when you notice them, and do lariz:. things for them. Miss Florence Nighting,le was kind to them. When quite a little girl ene bound up a poor dog's broken leg, and \Nil she grew bigger she went to nurse the poor si;lc soldiers in time of war.</p>
        <p>Thus you see that kind children make lir ivt. men and women.</p>
        <p>Wm. T. CHATTERTOV.</p>
        <p>GOD BLESS MOTHER.</p>
        <p>A little child with flaxen hair, And sunlit eyes so sweet and fair, Who kneels when twilight darkens all, And from those loving lips there fall The accents of the simple prayer— " God bless—God bless my mother."</p>
        <p>A youth upon life's threohold wide, Who-leaves a gentle mother's side, Yet keeps enshrined within hie breast Her words of warning—still the best; And whispera when temptation tried— " God bless—God bless my mother."</p>
        <p>A white-haired man who gazes back , Along life's weary, furrowed track ' And sees one face—an angel bright, Hears words of light that lead aright, And prays with reverential brow— "God bless—God blees my mother:"</p>
        <p>EVA HALE.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l have been reading about your League lately, so I wanted to bscopae a member. I am thirteen, and in the Sevcnth standard at school. I am sending you a p.',ce of poetry which I hope you will accept, and receive me as a member. I remain, Ybur sincere Niece, , MARGERY. HELEN HOCELEY. I LITTLE HELPEkS. Two Mile babies._ laughed and crowed,</p>
        <p>Two little babies cried. One lived up in the Castle grim, One on the moorland vaide. One little babe grew straight and strong; One little babe, alas Never could climb the Castle hill, Never could run on the grace.</p>
      </div>
