      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <p>A</p>
        <p>Sinners in the Past.</p>
        <p>Although to-day public censure is concentrated against the motorist, whose enormities are reported throughout the land, there are many wheelmen who can recall the period, about sixteen years back, when the cyclist was denounced as a road pest and public nuisance. Organised road races were respon- sible for most of the outcry, as packs of riders, often exceeding a score in number, swept along certain highways, raising nearly as much dust from their wheels as a modern motor-car. Cases of assault committed by affronted pedestrians, who regarded the cyclist as an interloper on the highway, were by no means infrequent at this period.</p>
        <p>47, .;;'.4.514 '4* ,- • •,4t BY Tim CLUBMAN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Obsolete Municipal Methods.</head>
        <p>Obsolete Municipal Methods.</p>
        <p>Although every year sees the election of cyclists to the borougli councils, much scope .for improve- ment still remains in respect to th 6 watering of urban thoroughfares in summer. Undue profusion of watering, possibly due to the laggard pace of the van-horse employed, results in the drenching of the road, and the formation of miniature pools in every hollow of its surface. However fine the day, woe betide the unwary cyclist who shall leave his guards at home. Moreover, where the road has not been properly cleansed before the passage of the watering demon, the liability of side-slip is an ever-present menace to the cyclist, especially when he is forced by other traffic to hug the curved sides, near the gutter.</p>
        <p>In watering a lawn or conservatory people go to considerable trouble to distiibute the water in the form of a fine spray, which does its work gently and efficiently. It would cost but little to fit our water catts with a fine spraying apparatus in place of the present arrangement, and the saving of water would recoup the cost in a very short time.</p>
        <p>1,- , -,_=._:._-__:-zi.,,T-7 '`l • 1:31;.,, , I I .(#„... „,,L\_,,,.„.,, •;--7.:4.,4'7e-``.:ltir‘di, "14---7.-------'•-•':.-C-_-..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Back from Athens.</head>
        <p>Back from Athens.</p>
        <p>'Ate little party of English cyclists who repre- sented 'this country at the Olympian gamps have now all returned. , The stories current regarding the poor accommodation provided by the Greek Government for their visitors were almost inevitable, considering That the entrants numbered 900. After easily winning the 20tltilometres path race, W. J. Pett, of the Putney AC., was expected to pull og the Marathon road race; but, owing to a fall he sustained in rounding a sharp curve, he was knocked out of the contest, the winner turning up in the Frenchman Vast.</p>
        <p>Warning signs.</p>
        <p>Before brakes were brought up to their present- day efficiency, cyclists were largely dependent for their safety on the "danger boards" erected throughout the country in the vicinity of steep descents It was sometimes complained that these warnings were put up where there was no real need for them. Strangely enough, the same grumble now emanates from motorists, in respect of the red caution triangles familiar on every road. In connexion with the tragedy at Markyate Street, it is urged that the warning signs should be placed at a greater distance from 'the point.of danger, What drivers might have time to slow up without vfolent use of their brake.. It is also contended that the ' signs-should be of a bolder character, and that steps should be taken for their illumination by night. '7 Most cyclists will be inclined to hold the opinion the accidents are mainly due to the excessive • indulged in by the drivers of powerful cars. - , ' A Useful Repair 'Plaster. The latest device for repairing the cover of a tyre which has been badly torn by it burst consists of a piece of stout falu ic, of double thickness hi- the cent, which is sointioned on the inside of the cover. The thinner edges of the plaster form wings, which fit round the flanges of the cover, so that a snug fit in the rim is ensured. After the plaster has been applied and the tyre inflated, the external damage to the cover can be repaired with a similar plaster, and it is claimed that this repair will outlast the life of a tyre.</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
