      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Northern Rugby Union. IPROPOSED DRASTIC CHANGES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Platt, the hon. secretary of the Northern Union, has issued to all the clubs in membership the usual circular letter giving details of all the proposals of uhich due notice has been given for the annual tqeting in the second week in June. These comprise a number of very radical propositions for the alteration of the laws governing both the game and the Union. The first on the list is a proposal. by Salford,seconded by ,Broughton Rangers, to abolish the present constitu- tion of the committee of the Union which places a sort of foundation management" in the hands of the eighteen clubs which 1 ourided the Union, and leaves the other clubs which subsequently joined to be represented on the committee at the discretion of the committee. Another proposed amendment of the same bylaw stands in theiname of the Swinton Football Club. Warrington and Leigh propose that the number a..f players shall be thirteen. Several amendments are proposed id regard to law of touch and bringing the ball into play from touch. The present rule is as follows:—" The ball is in touch when it or a player carrying it touch or cross the touch-line ; it shall then be taken into play ten yards from, and at right angles to, the touch-line; and there put down and scrummaged." The ',proposal of the Warrington and St. Helens Clubs is that when the ball is kicked in touch it shall be taken back to where last played, and there put down and scrummaged. Broughton Rangers and Salford propose to adopt a combination of the punt-out and the scrummage to return the ball to play. A Bradford pro- posal 14 to brim, the ball into play again either by the thrown-in at right angles to the touch-line or by the ten yards scrummage, at the option of the side to which the ball belongs; and in the case of a not straight throw-in the ball to be scrummaged, a throw-in not to be claimed as a throw forward.</p>
        <p>--...-- - Under the present rule of penalties for knock-on "a player who accidentally knocks the ball on shall not be penalized if he obtains possession before it falls to the ground." Hull. Oldham, and Wakefield Trinity propose to delete that clause, and to provide for the case by enabling a player accidentally knocking on or any opponent to play the ball, but in the case of any other player on his side a scrummage shall follow. In the rules of the Challenge Cup Competition the question of members paying to all cup-ties is again raised.At present everybody has to pay for semi-flnaLand final and for the other rounds members entitled to vote in general m eeting or sebscribers of not less than 10s. per annum have the right of free admission. Leeds and Bradford propose to give that right to all members at all ties except semi-finals and finals. Broughton Rangers and Salford propose to also make members pay when the ground is a neutral ground selected for a re-played tie.</p>
