9a *faars of the Rope and Anchor, stated that Christ for judgment, it will be found that creeds and catechisms, and even sermons and prayers, will have no his average takings at the house were from £:11, to .232 p place in the awful Assize. Truth, Justice aed Mercy per week. He left the house simply for the benefit of and the love of God will be the only pass-ports that his children. Lockett had conversations with him after will admit to the" glory, honour and immortality "of entering upon the tenancy of the Rope and Anchor and the everlasting Kingdom of Righteousness which shall he said hq was perfectly satisfied and was doing good be established " under the whole heaven." Those business. whose Christianity was confined to preachings and other Mr. 13. E. Watson, traffic clerk at Messrs. Greenall's sectarian forms will, to their unutterable dismay, be brewery, said be was present at the valuation of the excluded for ever.—Yours truly Rope and Anchor and he and Donworth and Taylor had a conversation. Taylor said he had let the trade of the house go down willfully because the firm had not fulfilled a promise that he should have a better house. He said that as he had not been given another house he had bought an insurance book. He bad been working the book for three months and had let the trade of the house go down. He had, he said, bought the book out of the profits of the business. Walter James Potter said he had had conversations with Lockett whilst he held the tenancy of the house and he always said be was doing pretty well—better than Mr. Horlock had done. Even when the music licence bad been taken away he never said he was losing anything. Taylor also told witness a few weeks before he left the house that he had lost nothing and was making a living. Witness thought the house was required as he had always seen it pretty well patronized. Edward Replay, manager of the shop 41, Butter- market-street, said the business of the Rope and Anchor had increased since Mr. Donworth took it over. William Rogerson, lodging-house keeper, Ship-yard, gave similar evidence. Mr. Browne intimated that forty people had volun- teered to give evidence but he did not propose to call any of them. William Donworth, licencee of the Rope and Anchor said he was formerly a recruiting officer in the army and left with the highest testimonials. Previous to taking the house he saw Taylor who told Lim he was doing well and that he (witness) woeld make a com- fortable living. Witness paid him ,£laa for stock Stc., Taylor said the brewery company had not fulfilled their promise to give him a better house and he would make it hot for them. He had also said, that he would do everything in his power to injure the house and witness. The average weekly takings of the house since witness entered upon the tenancy were £l7 4s. and he was making a living and thought he would continue to do so. He was doing so well that he had had to employ extra waiters at the week ends. Mr. Browne addressed the Bench. He said the house cost in 1888 £2,500, and the licencee himself had spent £l5B when he entered upon the tenancy. It would, he contended,be unfair to Mr. Donworth. who was improv- ing the business, if he had to lose the money. During the past twenty years Messrs. Greenall had given up eight fully licensed houses and eleven beerhouses for five new houses. That, he believed, was following Out. the principle of the Licensing Act. The same course was being followed by Manchester and Liverpool, War- rington having set the example when Mr. Monks pre- sided over the Licensing Committee. Mr. H. Roberts : You say Messrs. Greenall have acted in accordance with the spirit of the DOW Act and that the same course is being followed in Manchester and Liverpool. My reading of the newspapers does not justify that( statement. Mr. Browne: Anyone reading the newspapers should bare a different opinion. You see the chairman saying to the brewery representative : " You haven't given enough." "We want more. Mr. Roberts : Not for new licences. Mr. Browne : Yes, without a doubt. But, with all great respect, I ought only to answer questions from the Chairman of the Bench. Continuing, he said Messrs. Greenall submitted that there was no redund- ancy of a certain class of licences in Warrington. He would call the attention of the Bench to the condition of Buttermarket-street as compared with twenty years ago. Within a fifty yards radius of the Rope and Anchor four fully licensed houses and one beerhouse had been closed. Mr. \V. Roberts : Yes. But how many are left in Buttermarket-street ? Mr. Browne: You have the Pelican, the Crown and Sceptre. Higher and Lower Angel, and then you come to the Rope and Anchor. Continuing he said Messrs. Greenall thought that considering they had given up such a number of houses one ought not to be taken</p>
        <p>Mr. Ha'limey said the compensation authority thought in their wisdom that the tenant of the Queen's Hotel was not making enough to live on and that he was paying £125 for the privilege, as they put it, of losing money. He did not think that was an attribute of Warrington people. He submitted that as the house was taking £2() a week the licence ought not to be taken away. Of course the house was a small one but small houses were necessary and a certain class of people preferred going to a small house to entering a glaring gin palace.</p>
        <p>THE DECISION. , After a brief retirement the Bench refused to renew all three licences, subject to compensation.</p>
        <p>lONA.</p>
        <p>May, 190 G.</p>
