      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>FIGHTING THE FLAMES. -- Warringtonian as Amateur Fireman in</head>
        <p>FIGHTING THE FLAMES. -- Warringtonian as Amateur Fireman in</p>
        <p>America.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. R. Beswick, 17 Bewsey-road, has just received the following graphic letter from his brother, Mr. J. C. Beswick, who left Warrington for America a littlo over two years ago : North Yakima, Washington. May 9th, 1906. Dear brother,—l should have written in answer to your letter on Sunday had it not been that I was just about exhausted with w6rking the hardest four hours of my life, from ten o'clock Saturday night till two o'clock on Sunday morning. Doubtless you will wonder what I was doing, well I jut gave a hand to fight the flames which destroyed a whole block of warehouses, besides the Northern Pacific Freight Depot. I was just about to go to bed, when looking through the balcony door I was faced with the fiercest glare I have ever seen in ray life. I knew then what it was, so I dressed and ran nut to see how things stood, but nothing stood when that blaze touched, and it did not need an experienced eye to see that if every willing ' man did not help, very little of that town would be left. As it was, the checking of its advance was only due to the good brick building which stood at the Pod of the block. So I set to with the others and hauled the hose and straightened it. There were but a few of us in this part, for the flames were two fierce to allow a crowd to gather, and I had only just laid the hos down when there was a sharp report, and the next thin I knew was seeing someone stagger, and at my feet fe,g, a youth of about 12 years, who had been pluckily givin" me a pull with the hose, and when I raised him up, did I ever witness such a sight ? a great hole was pierced through his face, and his oyes looked at me without meaning. He could not speak. but Oh what that young lad must have suffered! However, it was no time to be running away. Two men took him to a doctor, and the chum who was with me wanted me to come out of the way. He said there might be more. But it was not a time for hiding. I turned to, with three other men, and mounted to the roof of the brick building I referred to before, and there we stood as long as the flames were ten or twelve feet away, pouring in the water, for it needed four men to a nozzle ; such was the engine pressure. Not one of the men on that roof was a fireman, but the time came when we had to vacate the roof and continue the jet from the street, and there in that building it was held from advancing, and by early morning most of the experienced men were able to take our place, and [just went to my room isnd to bed, but a dry stitch of clothes I had not, and dirty, well, cheer up ; it's great fighting flames. You should see American buildings burn ; shu ! like that! Most of them are wood. It is Wednesday now, and still it smoulders. Well, I guess I am getting too warm to write any more about fire, but it certainly was hot on that roof. I thought I should never get my face cool again. I may say that I am still bard at work and in good health, thank God, but I expect to vacate this town for beautiful Vancouver Island this month.—JAMES C. 131:S \ Ic</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
