      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>Successful Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>, 1 With the lendable object of raising funds to carry out a scheme for the renovation/ of Cairo-street Sunday : school and ladies' parlour, a sale of work was held in the schoolroom on Thursday afternoon. The members , . of the congregation had taken considerable interest in the arrangement of the sale, and there was a very credit- able display of useful and ornamental wares. The i following presided at the stalls :---Fancy stall : Mrs. : Wm. Long, Mrs. Shipman, Miss Dowson and Miss I Thornleigh. Flower stall : Mrs. F. Monks. Miss D. I Monks, and Miss Leigh. Sewing Society's stall : Mesdames Pemberton, Hollingsworth and Cotton, and I Miss M. Worrell. Crockery and wool stall :Mrs.Marleley I Miss D. Madeley and Miss Perris.Mother's meeting stall: Mrs. Harvey Cook, Mrs. Crowther, Miss A. Houghton. I Sunday School stall : Mrs. George Shaw, Misses E. ! Pemberton, B. Shaw, L. Spence, and L. Mellor. Refresh- ' meat stall : Mesdames J. Hill, Percy Rtthbone, 1 Webster, W. Webster and Hampson, and Miss M. Hill. I Post !card stall : Mr. D. Plinston. The sale was ' opened by Mr. G. H. Leigh, of Menton, the proceedings being presided over by Mr. Wm. Lon.. I The CHAIRMAN said they would all agree as to the ' deserving nature of the object of the sale which was to I raise a large amount towards the decoration of the I school and adjacent buildings. There was certainly I plenty of room for the expenditure of any surplus 1 which might remain after carrying out that work. The , articles displayed on the stall were almost entirely the work of the ladies of the congregation and he thought they might go a long way before they saw at a sale of , I work goods of such general excellence. He was glad I that so many old friends had rallied in support of that I effort. Senior on the list was Mr. Holbrook whom be (Mr. Long) remembered sixty years ago as a member of the congregation. He had now passed his 94th year and his daughter wrote to Mrs. Long that morning I stating that he was spending a holiday at Colwyn Bay. I Amongst others who had lent assistance were Mrs. Vance • Smith, of Bowden,:tliss Valentin. of Hale, Mrs. Briggs, Lady Mayoress of Leedsond Miss Gaskell.in these days the Education Bill was the subject in the minds of everybody with the e•xception perhaps of one gentle- man who had just told him that he had never read the Bill and should not take any notice .of it until it was passed because he did not know how much it might be altered. (Laughter). As the subject I was so much to the fore he supposed the ladies thought ; they had better set their house in order and see that the ! schools were made fit to be taken over by the local I authority when they were ready to do so. (Applause). 1 Mr. LEIGH said it afforded him very considerable • pleasure to be present to show his sympathy with the ; undertaking, knowing as he did something about the i most interesting history oit their Church life at Cairo- ; Street—a history of which all connected withithe chapel and schools might well be proud. The noble traditions surrounding the place had been well maintained by the ministers who had successfully had charge of the work. Some of those men he had had the privilege of knowing, Amongst them he might mention his old friend tt.ss Rev. Richard Pilcher whom he knew intimately and for whom he had a very deep regard. Then there were the Rev. F. K. Freeston and the Rev. W. H. Drummond both of whom bore honoured names 1 in their denomination. He had not had a nrevious oppor- tunity of meeting the Rev. C. Flarvey Cook but he was sure their present minister would do his best to main- tain the noble traditions of Cairo-street. Although he (Mr. Leigh)had not'perhaps been as enthusiastic as some people!in regard to bazaars and sales of work,he felt there was .a good side to them as they afforded an opportunity for the members of the congregation to rally together and show their interest in the church and schools. Having paid a tribute to Mr. Holbrook Gaskell, who I previous to coming to Warrington lived at Menton, iMr. Leigh pointed out that their first minister at .Monton was the ejected Vicar of Eccles just as their first minister at Warrington was the ejected Rector of the Parish Church, so that there were common sympathies between them in that respect. He always had a special place in his heart for the congregations which had come down to them from an extremely interesting past. Theycould not do too much to keep that history before them and to try and make people understand the development which had taken place in religious opinion in connection with their church. That there was a future for the principles by which they stood he had not the slightest doubt. (Applause.) The Rev. C.HARVEY COOK acknowledged the services which had been so willingly rendered by the Ladies' Sewing Society and the Sunday school. With regard to the objections taken to bazaars, he thought the spirit of a bazaar was the great thingand not the bazaar itself. That bazaar showed that they at Cairo-street recognized the spirit of self-help and he was pleased to see that many friends outside their own denomination had come to support their efforts. (Applause.) 1 Mr. F. MONKS moved and Mr. J. HILT, seconded a vote of thanks to Mr. Long and Mr. Leigh which was very heartily accorded. , The business of the sale then commenced. Several very pleasing entertainments were given during the afternoon and evening. Miss Harding and Miss Poynting appeared in two capital sketches—one a scene from "Dombey and Son" and the other entitled " Geese." Miss Dowson and Miss Thornleigh contri- buted instrumental music and Miss Edith Birchall gave songs. The greatest enthusiasm was displayed in the pro- ceedings, and the proceeds of the Sale amounted to 41.10. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>SEAWEED IN KIDNEY DISEASES.</head>
        <p>SEAWEED IN KIDNEY DISEASES.</p>
        <p>Dr. JAMES WALKER,Nidney and Urinary Specialist, New York, says :—"I never treat a case where the kidneys, urinary organs or stomach are involved with- out seaweed. 1 regard it, as indispensable."</p>
        <p>The species of seaweed used by Dr. Walker is the same as that contained in VEso's SEAWEED TONIC, VEND'S SEAWEED TONIC is used by many prominent doctors in all forms of kidney and urinary diseases because of its great strengthening, healing and purify. in effect. It cures Nephritis of the kidneys, Brighi's disease, uric acid troubles, weakness, dropsy and back. ache ; especially succeseful in constipation. Pleasant to take. Cures permanently. No return. Ask for VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC, at Chemists every- where, is. 10. and 2s.</p>
