      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 26, 1906. EDUCATION COMMITTEE,</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 26, 1906. EDUCATION COMMITTEE,</p>
        <p>away because they had had the unfortunate ex perienc of two tenants allowing the business to go down. The Bench reserved their decision until after the hearing of the other cases.</p>
        <p>In the case of the Queen's Hotel, Mr. Longland appeared for the landlord, Mr. Richard Sanson and Mr. A. T. Hallaway (deputy town clerk), for the owners, the Warrington Corporation. The Chief Constable objected to the renewal of the licence on similar grounds to those in the case of the Rope and Anchor. No evidence was taken, as that given at the previous hearing was admitted by Mr. Longland. The Chief 'Constable also admitted the takings of the house as from £2O to £2l per week. Mr. Longland said it seemed• to him that a house doing that trade was not the class of house which should be dealt with by the Compensation Authority, and in adjoining towns the licences of houses doing a similar trade were renewed.</p>
        <p>being renewable for a further period of six months at the same rate.—The annual report of the Stores Clerk upon the keeping of the various stock books at the several schools during the past year was submitted, and it was noted that, with the exception of the Secondary school and Technical Institute, the books had in each case been satisfactorily kept. It was resolved that the attention of the head master of the Secondary school and Technical Institute be called to the unsatisfactory nature of the report so far as it affects that school, and that he be requested to furnish the committee with an explanation. The term-end report of the Headmaster of the Secondary school and Technical Institute was sub- mitted. 'ln reference to a suggestion made that a maintenance allowance should be given to free scholars atithe Secondary school, the committee decided that any necessitous cases that may be brought to their notice should be considered as they arise from time to time. Upon the recommendation of the Headmaster, it was resolved, (a) That a second master be appointed for the Secondary school, who shall be responsible for the conduct of the school in the absence of the Head- master; and (b) that Mr. Richard H. Albery, the physics master, be appointed to the position. in moving the adoption of the minutes Canon Stevenson asked for the support of the Committee in the request that was to be made to the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee for permission to the keeper of the baths to give instruction to the children in swimming. The committee were kind enough last year to enable them to use the baths and they thought it was of great importance that the children should receive instruction. The School Management Committee were not asking the Baths Committee to do it at their own expense but simply requested permission that the baths keeper should be allowed to teach the scholars. With regard to the resignation of Miss Bibby from the headmistress- ship of the Ladies' School of Industry, he would like to mention that she had held the post for nineteen years. The school was situated in a rather poor district and he thought during the whole of that time Miss Bibby had done a very good work for the children of the neighbourhood. It had always been the committee's custom—and he thought an excellent one—that they should not allow a teacher who had been many years in the service of the borough to go without some kindly word of recognition and it gave him great pleasure to call attention to the committee's resolution. Alderman SMETHURST seconded and said they all regretted losing the services of a lady who had served the town so faithfully. The CHAIRMAN concurred with the remarks of the previous speakers and also warmly endorsed the sugges- tion to the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee as to the teaching of swimming. It would, he believed, be of real service to the children and he hoped the com- mittee would accede to the request. Mr. PLINSTON said that at the last meeting permission was given for the appointment of an uncertiflcated teacher at the Sacred Heart School in place of Miss Miller, resigned. That had not been rescinded and yet the appointment of a certificated teacher was now to be sanctioned.</p>
        <p>Canon STEVENSON explained that they had been unable to get an =certificated teacher for the post and there- fore it was thought advisable ta appoint a certificated mistress. The minutes were approved. MR. HENRY ROBERTS AND THE CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>At a mceting of the Sit—es and Buildings Committee. A letter was read from Messrs. J. E. Wright, Garnett and Wright, intimating that they are willing to obtain a tender for the erection of the " Bolton ' Council' school at a cost of not more than £lO 10s, per head in accordance with the decision of the Committee at their last meeting, assuming that "a reasonable margin "to that amount will be allowed. The director was instructed to ask Messrs. Wright, Garnett and Wright their interpretation of the term "a reason- able margin." Councillor PEMBERTON asked what amount was specified. " Reasonable margin " was rather wide. Alderman BP,'XNETT explained that the interpretation from Messrs. Wright, Garnett and Wright had been received, but had not been placed before the Committee. Mr. IL ROBERTS : That is not an answer to Coun- cillor Pemberton's question. Alciartnan BENNFITT : You are out of order, Mr. Roberts. :Mr. ROBERTS : I think I have possession of the Com- mittee whiie I speak, Alderman BENNETT : You are out of order, and you must allow me to rule. Mr. H. ROBERTS : I say with all respect you have misunderstood Councillor Pernberton's question. I have a right to correct you if ' you are in error, and I do it with all possible respect. Con- tinuing, Mr. ROBERTS' said he thought Councillor Pembert:n had not read the whole of the resolution, or he would have seen that an explanation WAS asked as to what" reasonable margin " meant. Alderman Binisprrr : I pointed that out to him. M. ROBERTS : No, sir, you did not. The minutes were approved. GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE. At a meeting on May 21st the special sub-committee reported that the committee of the Warrington Infirmary were unable to extend the existing facilities for the treatment of school children at that institution. —The question of the consideration of the extension of the committee's tenancy of the Grappenhall-road and St. George's (Infants') Council Schools was referred to the Sites and Buildings Committee.—Mr. A. J. Raley was appointed an )additional representative to the Association of Education Committees for the year 190G-7.—lt was noted that the following managers had been re-appointed, viz. :—(a) Mr. H. N. Houghton, St. James's School, Latchford ; and (b) Mr. J. Rymer Young, Warrington British School. On the motion of the CHAU:MAN, seconded by the DEPUTY-CHAIRMAN, the minates were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>MR. H. ROBERTS AND THE CHAIRMAN</head>
        <p>MR. H. ROBERTS AND THE CHAIRMAN</p>
        <p>Lively Passage.</p>
        <p>Half an hour sufficed for the transaction of the busi- ness of the monthly meeting of the Warrington Educa- tion Committee held at the Town Hall on Monday evening. Alderman Bennett presided and there were also present :—Councillor Fulton Smith (vice-chairman), Aldermen Smethurst and Tinnion Counzillors Wilkin- son Sankey, Barton, Evans, Lord, Pemberton, Ellison and Ball, Miss Williams, Revs. Canon Morley Stevenson, J. Davies, J. Yonge and N. Wilson, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, H. Roberts, G. Walker, E. Plinston, H. Kingdon, C. S. Burgess, R.T.Pairclough, T.S. Steel, C. H. Unsworth and J. Moore Murray (director). NEW MEMBERS WELCMED O •' •</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said he would ilia— ii7—express the pleasure it gave the Committee to welcome a lady member in the person of Miss Williams. They hoped her connection with the Committee would be a long and 1 pleasant one. They also welcomed Mr. Steel, who had been appointed to represent the Friendly Societies, and he was sure all Mr. Jolley's old friends would be pleased to see him back again. (Hear, hear.) THE WAGES OF CARETAKERS. The Finance Committee recommended the appoint- ment of Mr. A. J. Jolley and Mr. C. Savikins Burgess as members of that 'committee. At its last meeting the Committee considered a resolution with reference to the caretaker of the Ladies' School of Industry, which was not approved and adopted by the Education Com- mittee at their last meeting, and it was rasolved that a Sub-Committee, consisting of Mr. A. J. Jolley and Mr. R. T. Fairclocigh, be appointed to take into considera- tion the whole question of the payment of school-keepers IA notification from the Board of Education of the pay- meat of an instalment of the Aid grant for the public elementary schools in the Borough, amounting to £2347 45., was submitted and noted. The Denuty Town Clerk having forwarded a resolution of the Water Com- mittee to the effect that it was proposed to place meters in all the schools in the Borough, it was resolved, (a) That Mr. A. J. Jolley and Mr. R. T. Fairclough be appointed to meet the Chairman of the Water Committee with a view to seeing if some arrangement can be made for all the schools to be treated as one consumer; and (b) that the managers be notified that they will be res- ponsible for the meter rents. The resignation of Mr. Ernest Haigh as Secondary Clerk was received and accepted, his engagement to terminate on the 31st May. It was resolved that thexproposals of the Director with reference to the arrangem'ents of the office staff be approved, and that he be given authority to engage an office boy. _</p>
        <p>Mr. FAIRCLOUGH moved the adoption of the minutes with the exception of that relating to the notification of school managers that they would be responsible for water meter rents. Until the matter was arranged with the Water Committee that part of the resolution should remain in abeyance. Councillor PEMBERTON raised a qu,sstion as to the proposed appointment of Messrs. Jolley and Burgess as members of the committee. He understood it was agreed with the Town Council that there should be is certain number of members of each conamiitee. The CHAIRMAN said that on that particular com- mittee ten was the number arranged and when Nfessrs. Jolley and Burgess were appointed there would still be one short.</p>
        <p>I Councillor PEMDERTON contended that if names were I to be added the selection should be made by the general committee, as at present it placed one in an awkward position if he desired to move an amendment. Mr. H. ItosEßTs said the Corporation had insisted on haring a majority of members on each sub-committee, and as long as that was observed—and he took it that it would be observed honourably by the Education Committee—there was nothing further to be said. If the proposition to elect these two gentlemen would put the Corporation in a minority there would be something in Councillor Pemberton's question but un- less that was the fact there was nothing in it. Mr. PrAssTos said he thought the Finance Committee had been a little previous in selecting Mr. Jolley as the member of a sub-committee at a time when he had not been elected to the general committee. The iN said Mr. Jolley had been recom- mended for election by that committee and if the recommendation had not been accepted the appoiytment to the sub-committee would have fallen to the ground. Councillor IWILKINSON If we appoint Messrs. Jolley and Bargess does it bring the committee up to ten?</p>
        <p>The DIAECTOR : Nine with two ex-ofiScio. Councillor IVILKINSON said Mr. Jolley's place had been kept open until the Council confirmed his election and Mr. Burgess was appointed in place of Mr. Bibby who was no longer a inember of the committee.</p>
        <p>Mr. PLINSTON asked for an explanation as to the amount which was being paid to the caretaker of the Ladies' School of Industry. Mr. FAIRCLOUGH said the committee passed a resolu- tion that for schools with 300 scholars caretakers should be paid £1 per week. In the school in question there were not 300 children, so it was open to dispute whether the caretaker came under the committee's recommenda- tion or not. He thought that now that the whole question was to be gone into it would be better to fix the wages according to the floor space in the schools. Mr. PLINSTON : What amount is being paid to this caretaker?</p>
        <p>The DIRECTOR : Twelve shillings and sixpence in accordance with the resolution that the cotutuitteo rescinded. . _</p>
        <p>Mr. Pussros : I say that is entirely wrong. We rescind a resolution under which she is paid a certain price and without any further action being taken we go on paying her on terms which we referred back. The CHAIRMAN said his impression was that the resolution was rescinded and that presumably the com- mittee ought to pay the 9ld figure until the new one came into force. The DIRECTOR said the appointment of a school keeper had not yet been made. Whatever salary Was fixed, payment would be made for past services. Mr. PLINSTON said the temporary ari-angements was 21 a week, and until the appointment of caretaker was made, the woman ought to receive that wage. The CHNIRMAN : I am afraid we can do nothing further in the matter at present. Mr. Pt's:Trost : What : And you are taking is. 60. a week from her ! I shall move an amendment Mr. Farncr,oocfr contended that as he had replied the matter was closed. The CHAIRMAN said Mr. PI inston could brinir forward a motion at the next meeting. The minutes were then approved. scaooL MANAGEMENT collmarrEE. THE ART ,MASTER'S POStTiON. THE TEACHING OF SWIMMING. At the meeting of the School :Ifanagement COM- mittee permission was given for the appointment of a certificated assistant mistress at the Sacred Heart school ' in place of Miss Miller,resigned. It was resolved that Hr. James E. Marshall be appointed Manual Instructor at the Secondary school ond Technical Institute, at a com- mencing salary at the rate of £llo per annum. It hav- ing been reported that the resolution of the committee I with reference to the method of staffing the public elementary schools in this Borough had been withdrawn from the proceedings as submitted to the Town Council it was resolved that this matter, together with the revision of the scale of salaries for certificated assistant teachers in view of the abolition by the Board of Education of the system classifying the results of the certificate examination, be referred to a sub-committee consisting of Hr Councillor Evans, Mr. Councillor Lord, I'sir. Councillor Wilkinson, Hr. John Upson, the Rev. Neville Wilson, and the chairman of the committee (the Rev. Canon Stevenson) for consideration and report. It was noted that the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee had decided to grant the use of the Swimming Baths for the school children on the same terms as last year. It was resolved that the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee be asked to grant the committee permission to make arrangements with the keeper of the baths to give instruction in swimming to the school children who attend the baths. A letter having been received from the headmaster of the School of Art, intimating his iniention to resign his position at some date not later than the 31st July, 1907, it was resolved, (a) that the resolution of the committee call- ing for the resignation of the Headmaster of the School of Art rescinded ; and (b) that, should he desire to resign at any date earlier than the 31st July, 1907, he be required to give the committee at least three months' notice of his intention to do so. The committee pro- ceeded to the deferred consideration of the communica- tion received from the Boird of Education in reference to the proposed leases of land at Sankey by the Governors of the Warrington Grammar school, and, after some discussion, it was resolved that the formal objection to the proposil recorded by the committee at their last meeting he withdrawn. It having beea reported that the resolution of the Committee at their last meeting appointing Hr. Coun- cillor Etans. Mr. Councillor Wilkinson, and the Chair- man of the Committee (the Rev. Canon Stevenson) to represent this Committee at the second Int9rnatioreil Congress on School Hygienic: to be held in London in August next, was not approved by the Ethic :don Corn- mittee at their last meeting, it was resolved that Mr. Councillor Wilkinson and the Director be appointed to represent the committee at that Congress. In connection with the resignation of Hiss Bibby as bead mistress of the Ladies' School of Industry, it was resolved that the Committee do place on record their high appreciation of the long and successful services rendered by her to the cause of education in the town. Authority was given for an advertisement to be issued inviting applications for the vacant position, and it was resolved that the Managers of the Ladies' School of Industry be requested in making the appointment, to give, if possible, preference to teachers who are or who have been employed as teachers in the Borough. The arrangements for the examination of teachins in physical exercie,s by r.Thomas Chesterton, of London, on the 19th May and 16th June, 1906, were approved, and authority was given for the payment of the travelling expenses of the examiner. It was resolved that the playing Reid at Padgate for the Secondary school scholars be rented by the com- mittee for six months from the Ist May to the 31st October, from Mr. James L. Fryer, of the Pear Tree Farm, Padgate, at a rental of £l2 103., the tenancy</p>
      </div>
