Qui inspiring addresses were given by the Mr. Brighouse : If there is no legal obligation Rey. S. L. M. Thomas, Openshaw ; Rev. J.F.Nlatthews surely there is some moral obligation. In these days and Mr. E. Wynne Jones, arid the meeting terminsted ! when they are going to eliminate religious teaching all by a few words of appreciation and thanks on the part ' round and rule out ef catechism and the prayer book of the pastor, Rev. A. J. N. Back, on to our neighbours should be stndled. Dr.odgkiuson : Personally I have always taken rdu aty</p>
        <p>ENTERPRISE•IN WARRINGTON. , the course which you suggest. THE BRIDGE STREET IMPROVEMENTS. ) Mr. Conner lam not going to say that Dr. Brown One of the most interesting events in Warrington took absolutely the best course. No doubt their was, recently is undoubtedly the opening of the Grand unfortunately, an error of judgment. Clothing Hall's new and handsome premises in Bridge- Mr. Brighouse : I should advise Dr. Brown to say street. The premises have been erected in one of our "I did this to the best of my judgment and no doubt most important and greatly improved thoroughfa„s, made an error. If I had had more experience I would which forms quite a welcome and new innovation in have sent for a police officer." I am certain the jury architecture for this tewmand entitle; it to rank as one and myself would accept it. of the finest and most up-to.date clothing and tailoring I Dr. Brown was called and stated that in his opinion establishments in Warrington. The shop is attractive, there was nothing wrong with the deceased except that light and airy, fitted up with every modern improve- he was suffering from a mild form of delirium tremens. ment, and we do not hesitate to say that their customer The man himself said he was taking too much alcohol. will thoroughly appreciate these improvemen ts,an d He promised nothing to Mrs. Gibbins except that he will find shopping under such conditions, and amidst such would take care of her husband and see him safely surroundings, delightfully easy and pleasant. home. In view of what had happened he thought ho For many years the Grand Clothing Hall have °con- committed a slight error of judgment. It would have pied s. very prominent position in the Clothing and been better bad be kept Gibbins until someone handed Tailoring trade of Warrington, and a special and coin- him over to the police. In future he would take that mendable feiture of their method of working lies in the course. fact that the tailors' sanitary and well-ventilated work. Mr. Brighouse: I don't wish to prejudice Dr. Brown rooms are situated on the premises,which affords special in any way but I know when I commenced in prac tit facilities for personal supervision over the work, and is what silly things I did. a sure guarantee that every garment will be finished Dr. Broom addtd that he handed Gibbins over to the and turned out in a satisfactory manner. The shop has porter whom he instructed to take the man carefully been opened with an entirely new stock of the smart- home and to leave him in charge of a trustworthy man, or, eat goods comprising all the latest novelties for the failing that to hand him over to a policeman. He season. The Grand Clothing Hall Co's., enterprise impressed the instructions very strongly upon the porter. deserves every success, which we feel sure will be Frederick Allison, norter,smd he received instructions accorded to them by the public, to take Gibbins home and find a strong man to take</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-26_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>CYCLIST CLAIMS DAMAGES. ---- Alleged Running Down Case.</head>
        <p>CYCLIST CLAIMS DAMAGES. ---- Alleged Running Down Case.</p>
        <p>care of him or else hand him over to the police. They left the Infirmary together. In Tanners-lane a woman shouted to Gibbins and he went back. Witness followed. The woman said to Gibbin's "You don't look very well " and he replied " I urn all right." Gibbins declined to have a drink with the woman and they went on. In Watkin-street Gibbins said he would go into the house by himself and witness left hint twenty or thirty yards from the door. During the whole time witness was with Gibbins he saw no thing to make him think anything was the matter with him. He had no reason to think the deceased was Out of his mind.</p>
        <p>In answer to the Coroner Allison stated that he had been in the Army and whilst there had had charge of lunatics.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse : If you had it all to go over again would you leave Gibbins at the corner of Watkin- street ?—I think I would.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse: Suppose Dr. Hodgkinson gave you similar instructions would you think you were justified in leaving a man at the end of the street ?—lf I got the same orders in the same situation again I am certain I would do the same thing. I had no idea there was any- thing wrong with the man or I would not have left him. Mr. Brighouse in summing up said he thought the jury would agree with him that they did quite right in adjourning the inquest and thrashing the matter out. No one would have been satisfied unless they had done so. There was no doubt that Gibbins was suffering from delusions on Monday,and on Tuesday he must have been worse, and that\he was left at the Infirmary pre- sumably safe. The aonduct of those connected with the Infirmary came tinder their consideration. He did not like the suggestion in that case that the halucina- tions of the deceased wore due to alcohol. Influenza waa sufficient to cause the delusions without alcohol or anything else. Apart from any legal obligation or any obligation cast upon him by the rules and regulations ef the Infirmary there was a moral obligation respecting the treatment of a man suffering from delusions and likely to be danger- ous to himself or to the community at large, and ths right course to have taken would have been to have telephoned for the police to take charge of him. He did not wish to say anything to damage Dr. Brown in his future career and he did not think anything had been said in the case which would prejudice him in any shape or form. He saw nothing against the doctor except that he had committed an error of judgment. With regard to the porter. He had proper and distinct instructions and yet he allowed the man to go. Any man with sense, with the vast experience of which Allison seemed to make a boast, would have known that one of the characteristics of a lunatic and a person suffering from delusions was a desire to deceive people. After what Dr. Brown told him the porter had no right to leave the man. He said he would do the same thing again, and he suggested that Mr. Forshaw should ask the Infirmary people whether the porter ought not tc. have different instructions. If he was going to remain the porter and carry out orders as he had done in that case, he was a dangerous man to have instructions given to him.</p>
        <p>The jury returned a verdict of " Suicide whilst of unsound mincl.."rhey desired to censure the porter, and exonerated Dr. Brown, whom they did not think could be blamed after the instructions he had given to the porter..</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse : I entirely agree with the verdict. Addressing Allison he said : .You had definite instruc- tions as to what to do with this man and failed to carry them out, and you aggravate matters by saying if you got those instructions again you are going to carry them out in a similar manner. If you had come to the conclusion it was an error of judgment on your part one would have looked over the matter. lam certain Mr. Forshaw and the Bbard of Management cannot do so with a man who says he is going to carry out instruc- tions again in a similar fashion notwithstanding the fatal calamity as the reSult of your failure to carry them out. I think your position ought to be recon- sidered.</p>
        <p>Allison : I answered your question truthfully. I did not mean—</p>
        <p>I‘lr. Brighouse : You have had your opportunity of arguing it out. lam not going to argue it any more.</p>
        <p>AN EARL FOR THE DEFENCE. A sequel to an accident at Wilderspool Crossings was heard at the Warrington County Court on Thursday before Mr. F. Cuthbert Smith, acting as deputy judge when Noah Ruben, picture framer, 78, Knutsford-road,. Warrington, brougir an action against the Midland Counties Garage Company to recover £lOO damages for injuries received. Hr. Acton (instructed by Mr. A. Browne) appearea for the plaintiff, defendants being represented by the Right Hon. Earl Russel (instructed by Messrs. Clifford and Clifford). Plaintiffs case was that abou three o'clock on the afternoon of the Bth November he was riding a bicycle along Wilderspool-road, in the direction of Warrington and when nearing the railway crossing he noticed that the gates were closed. He rode very slowly until about So yards away from the crossing when the gates were opened. There were two carts in front of him and he, was riding behind them when a motor car came up behind and he was knocked oft his bicycle The car gave no warning of its approach and plaintiff had no idea that one was in the vicinity. He spoke to the driver of the car who said he could not help it and handed plaintiff his card and said "it will be all right if you write to that address." Plaintiff walked home and at the time did not think his injuries were so serious as they turned out to be. He could not eat or sleep inconsequence of the accident and had been under the care of Dr. Smith since. His business had suffered as a result of his not being able to attend to it. His claim was made up as follows: £49 Os. paid in wages, £l9 for loss of business through his inability to personally attend to it, damage to bicycle £5, absence from home on the advice of the doctor for six weeks ,E2l, doctors bill and professional attendance £2 os. tid. and damages for pain and suffering £9 11. 6d. Cross-examined : Plaintiff had had to employ a man to do his work since the accident. The bicycle could not have been repaired for 21s. He went to Berlin for the benefit of his health and staved with his brother- in-law, to whom he paid £lO for hislodgings. When in Berlin he did not go (about soliciting orders. Before the accident he took £8 a week at his business and it had since dropped to £5. Earl Russell : £2l for a -visit to Germany. You did yourself well, didn't you ? Witness did not answer. His Honour: Is Germany a particula.ly health-giving place ?-0-I was seeing life. His Honour : But there were some unexplored spots in Wales or Scotland to whicts you could have gone rather than to Germany ?—I went to a friend' 'S Berlin because I thought it cheaper. Dr. Smith spoke as to tho injuries which the plainti ft sustained. He was suffering from shock and could not eat or sleep. He advised the plaintiff to go to Germany,. - his native country, as he did not improve at all. In his opinion that was quite a genuine case of injury. t Alfred Waring, labourer, spoke as to witnessing the. accident. Plaintiff was knocked down by the motor car.</p>
        <p>Albert Edward Cope gave similar evidence. The. distance between plaintiff and the cart behind which he was riding was about 20 yards. Earl Russell said the defendant's story was that as he approached the crossing in his motor car he noticed that the gates were closed and that several carts were stopped. Plaintiff was stationery and was supporting his bicycle by holding on to a light dray with one band. The gates opened and plaintiff retained hold of the dray whilst crossing the railway lines. Defendant drove slowly behind, and sounded his horn. Plaintiff turned round, appeared to lose control of the machine and fell off. The car did not strike the plaintiff's bicycle before he fell. Defendant also alleged that the damages were greatly exaggerated. James Ambrose Doran, partner in the defendant firm, who was driving the car at tho time of the accident, gave evidence in supnort of his counsel's statement. The accident was purely the fault of the plaintiff. Similar evidence was given by Mrs. ‘Vinstanley and Dr. Turner, who were driving in the car. Dr. Fox stated that he examined the plaintiff in December, and found him suffering from nervousness or neurasthenia. Ho was able to resume his employ- ment. Dr. Eangdale said that on the 15th January the plaintiff was suffering from neurasthenia resultant from the accident. He thought it would have been good for the plaintiff had he tried to work. Mr. F. R. Holding stated that the plaintiffs machine was worth 355. before the accident. It would have cost 255, to repair it. After addresses by counsel his Honour gave judg- ment for the defendants with costs. Plaintiff had failed to satisfy him that the accident was due to the defend- ants' negligence and ;therefore Ile was; bound to give them the benefit of the doubt.</p>
        <p>Joe Drake was fined at Hastings on Thursday .£l5O for using his private residence as a betting. house. The police, who raided the place, seized 7,000 telegrams relating to racing. It was stated that he took as much as ,C 2.00 a day in bets.</p>
        <p>Death. mcLARTy.—On the 24th inst. at 8, Egerton-street, Warrington, Donald MclArty, aged 77 years. Inter- , ment Warrington Cemetery to-day (Saturday) at 2.30 p.m. Glasgow papers please copy. - Warringtorc—Printed and published by JOHN WALKED , CO., at The Examiner General Printing Works, Bewsey-street, and published by them at le same place, and also at The Examiner Offices, Victoria-road , WIDNES : 32, George-street, ST. HELENS: 3, Mark Street, EARLICSToWN; Church-street RuNcOrtbi, 3137 2Gth, 1906. F</p>
