        <p>Some cricket teams have not had a single innings in match games this season. Wet Saturdays have played havoc with the summer pastime. There is no guarantee yet that we shall have the right sort of • summer at all. Earthquakes and volcanic eruptions are being blamed already. * * Miss Williams, the first and only lady member of the Education Committee, was specially welcomed by the Chairman at a meeting of that body on Monday. We hope she will find her new sphere of labour one full of possibilities for usefulness. The next thing the Committee should do, *hen it has a vacancy, is to find Miss Williams a 4, olle4gue of her own sex. The Swansea town clerk bas written to the town clerk of Cardiff (who sought information with respect to the legality of the expenditure of public moneys upon temperance placards)that the anticipated benefits were so great that no question had been raised at Swansea, and, further, in his opinion, no court of law woulequash an order for such a payment. * * * Thursday was Ascension Day and Empire Day all in one, and the Warrington scholars had a holiday , from school, In Manchester things were farcical in ! the extreme. The Education Committee refused to ; make the day a holiday, and in consequence all the ! Catholic schools were empty. Masters and teachers ' were compelled to put in an appearance, and after ! calling the roll and making the registers in front of empty benches, time hung heavily on their hands until dinner time,after which interval there was the relaxa- tion of marking the register again. The whole thing might be looked upon as a good joke—except for the teachers—if it did not involve a considerable waste , the ratepayers money and the loss of grants.</p>
