        <p>1 PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS. , . . Hs: "I have often noticed that women take ; personally any general remark that may be made i about them." She: "How can you say that? lam I quite sure I don't!" I NSYER allude to a dressmaker as Miss Sew and I Sew. -, A GRAMMARIAN would, no doubt, define Adam's original position as "first Orson singular." " UNEASY lies the head that wears a crown." Thia is a falsehood, crowned 'heads lie with the greatest ease. ; "LOOK here!" said an excited man to a druggist. ' "You gave me morphine for quinine this morning." "Is that so ?" said the druggist. "Then you owe me sitpence." I PLEADED long for the whispered "Yes;" . Which she long withheld; but, oh ! I sigh to think how her name I'd bless If she'd only stuck to "No." , jENNY : "Pray excuse my asking you, but why did you break your engagement with poor Toni?" Louisa; "Hush, dear—don't tell anyone—he was growing so disgustingly fat! When grief has pulled him down a bit I shall take him on again." His wife went away for a birthday visit to her mother, and he gave her exactly the cost of .her fare back and forth. 1" But' I shan't have anything to spend while I'm there," objected his wife. " Yes, ycn will," answered the wretch, "you'll have your birthday to spend." "I WANT to tell you one thing that your medicine did," said the caller: "one battle of it restored My son toll very fine position that he had lost." "How was that ?" Asked the quack. "He gave it to the man who had taken his place." WHEREIN are a policeman and ts rainbow alike ? Both are tokens of peace, and usually appear after a storm. "You must have perseverance," said the young physician's friend. "No," was the. reply, "what I want is patients." Two little girls were boasting about their respective mothers. One, whose mother was tall and thin said: "My mother is taller than yours." "Oh, well," said the other, whose mother was wee and dumpy, "mine would be as big if she was drawn out." " Witr is it that an engagement ring usually has a diamond, while a wedding ring is a plain gold affair?" asked the inexperienced miss. 'Because," replied the experienced matron, "if things are not up to the girl's expectations it is a simple matter to break off an engagement, whereas,. when the wedding ring is produced the groom knows that it will be tod late for her to back out. The engagement and wedding rings mark the difference between expecta- tion and realisatmn, promise and fulfilment." A MEDDLESOME old woman was sneering at a young mother's aWkwardness with her infant, and said : "I declare, a woman ought never to have a baby un- less she knows how to hold it." "Nor a tongue, either," responded the young mother. LOVE is its own recompense, and it always pays good wages. Miss A.: "That Mrs. Gadder seems to be at home almost everywhere!" Miss B.: " Yes ; except at I, home!" . FRIEND: "Did the doctor help you much?" In- 1 valid: " Well, he relieved me a little." "How much ?" "Two guineas." - " DOROTHY, I saw a beautiful gown in a shop win- dow to-day." "What .was it, 4idius 2" " Well—it was that zigzaggery kind of cloth—and it had those braided things across the front and down the back, and some awful stylish pointed things on the skirt- -1 wish you would get one just like it." , • "TIM good die young," remarked the funhy man, seriously, to the editor. "I don't know about that," contended the editor. "I notice that most of the good jukes Eire old ones." . DANKINS : " What means this coolness between Jonson and Joanes—is there a difference between them ?" Hankins: "Difference? I should say so. As ' ninch difference ns there is between a gentleman and a donkey." " H'in. But, which is the gentleman and which is the donkey?" " Veil, it is just there where they differ." -Iv is a sad reflection, yet it cannot be successfully denied, that' not infrequently the great enemy of friendship is compinionship. Bna : -.Every body Says you married 111 b for my money." He': "But I didn't, dear. I know you look it, dear, but I didn't." , BROWN : " What sort of a- man is Spriggins?" Smith: "Well, you can't get an idea into his head, and you can't get one MA of it." , Tim probability is that things are neither as black es they are painted nor as .white as they are white- washe'd. IF we all had equal opportunities—" We should not all he equal to them." " l'n like to find some business that isn't over- crowded." "If you do, you'll probably find that there isn't anything in the business to attract a crowd." MRS. BEASLEY : "I can't understand how the hialvin's manage to live so well on Mr. Malvin's in- come." Mr. Beasley: "I can. I went up to his °glee to see him yesterday, and twenty-seven men who were waiting in the mai cried out in chorus ask- ing what firm I was collecting for." . " He died of a complication of diseases, didn't he ?" No. Ile had ottly one doctor." ,Suste: " Papa, what makes a man always give a diamond 'engagement ring ?" Her Father: Vie woivan, ' M :climax'', : " What are your qualifications for this business ?" Applicant: "1 can't get anything else to Ma. B.: " How many da you expect to dinner?"' Mrs. B. " "Mr. St offer and four others." Mr. B.: " That makes eight." Ilk : " Do you love me well enough to become my - wife ?" She: " I think I do. But I must have all the money I want, and I 11111 St always have toy own way, and go when and where I want to, and conic Inane when I please, and stay away when I please. Yes, I think I do really love you well enough to marry you." " HE seems to prize his fiancée highly," said the first sweet thing, when conversation was well under way. 'Naturally," rt plied the other sweet thing. " She's hand-painted, You know." Of course there was no jealousy in the case; certainly not. Atelinsoic : "You say she only partially returned your affection ?" Clarence: "Yes ; and that's what I'm grumbling. about. She returned all the love letters, but retained all the jewellery." THE world doesn't give .near as much time and thought to frustrating our plans as the result would lead us to beheve. As bard I sing of castles brave, • Subservient slaves, and ladies gay; ' As man, a flat is hl I crave, With cash enot gh to pay my way. i 11-RS. lILINT : " From list I hear of your husband, I should infer that he is a man of iron will." Mrs. Blunt: " You're right, he is; and pig iron at that." Foor.s are the only people who have no doubt about, an-t fling. Smo the youth : "Er—all—l—is there any reason why you and I should not. wed ?" " What has reason to do with the case ?" asked the young woman. Then he knew that, love alone ruled her actions. IT takes a windy man to blow his own trumpet. Timm are two things which entitle a man to wear things and say things' that other people may not— . half a millions pounds' an 1 white hair. ' Tom: "You say the bride and bridegroom looked nice : wh it about the guests?" Will: " Oh! they took the cake !" LADY Al' THE Door:: "I believe, in my heart, you are the saute tramp I gave a large piece of Christmas pudding to a few days ago!" Tramp: "No, ma'am ; you're mistaken. lie's dead." . , Mmicnaser : " I'm satisfied with yom\hand-writing. But can you write shorthand ?" Clerk': " Yes, but it takes longer !' " W HAT is the worst thing about richee ?" asked a school teacher of a boy. " Their scarcity," he re- plied, and was immediately rewarded with a prizet . Tuts would be a very happy world if people would always wear the expression they do when they are having their picture taken. Witten is the best—to be lucky or rich ?" , "To rm., don't. need itqle.lnekv."</p>
        <p>GIEZATHEART (isoleninly): "If I give'you this penny, what will you do with it ?" Tatters (sarcastically): I "I'll be honest with you, guy'nor ; "I'll spend it all I in riotous living. I Ha found his hair was leaving him at the top_ of his head, and took his barber to task about it. "You I sold tne two bottles of stuff to make the hair grow—" "It is very strange it won't grow again,' said the barber; "I can't understand it." "Look here," said the man, "I don't mind drinking another bottle, but this must be the last." MAUD: "So you have accepted Tem ?" Mabel: " Yee ; he said he wouldn't marry me if I didn't." THIS is the glorious season of the year, when you wear a t.reat-eoat one day, a thin coat the second, a porous-144n- the third, and a doctor's bill the fourth. " GoonsKaatt Ives had his salary raised; was it for extra work r' "es; li. always listens when the proprietor tells his baby's smart sayings.", FATHER: "SOW is it that you're such a dunce al your lessons, Tommy?" Tommy: "I expect iti hereditary." FIRST BOARDER: "Don't you always have a stuffy feeling in this house?" Second Boarder: "Not when we are in the dining-room." t, Doses: "There's a man who shaves several timee a day." Wiggin : "You don't mean it? I should think there's nothing left of his face." Dobbs: "11 doesn't hurt his face at all. He's a barber." TAXATION OF LAND VALUES. _ Arguments for a Much-Needed Reform. MEETING AT MESSRS. CROSFIELD'S. On Tuesday evening a meeting was held in the Assembly Rooms at Messrs. Crosfield's works to pro- mote the movement for the taxation of land values. Captain Crosfield, who presided, made some interesting observations upon systems of land tenure in Italy and their results which be had noticed in his recent tour. In particular he explained the method known as the Metayer system which prevails in Lombarday. The taxation of land values, he concluded, was the only means of permanently solving the great housing ques- tion. He was convinced that in this manner the rates and rents would be lowered and a great and undeserved burden would be taken from the shoulders of industry and the way opened for all kinds of new activities. In the absence of Mr. W. R. Lester, M.A., who had been announced to speak, Mr. Okell, the local secretary of the League for the taxation of Land Values, addressed the meeting upon the general objects of the League. He showed how the taxation of land values would effect wages. At the present time our English soil was only productive to half of its capacity. The reason was that the great body of the people wer excluded from the use of land in any form, and conse- quently unable to find employment, they com- peted at the factory gates, and 'compelled as they were by hunger, to work for any wages whatever, they depressed the labour market. The result was that,the purchasing power of the whole nation was diminished and every industry in the land was unfavourably affected. If taxes, however small, were imposed upon unearned increments in the value of land, it would compel landlords to put their land to use and the increased production would enable them to pay the annual charge upon it. Thus there would be a tendency for all land to be made productive, and not only would agriculture be stimulated but every forni of human activity would feel the benefit. Mr. Okell referred in an interesting way to the lines on which the Germans were developing their colony of Kiaow Chin where the principle of betterment or taxation of land had been adopted. A tax of 0 per cent. was placed upon the capital value of the land and this was arrived at in a curious way. The owner himself stated the value and upon that value he was assessed. If be stated it at too low a value the Government might step in and purchase, it being a condition that the Government could always purchase at the price at which the property was assessed. As a further check, every time the property came into the market a tax of 33 per cent, was payable upon any increase of value that the property was found to have acquired since its last transfer. This (Friday) evening Mr. W. R. Lester, M.A. will deliver a lecture at the Co-operative Hall on "The taxtation of land valueslnd the cause of unemploy- ment." Full particulars are published in our advertis- ing columns. 411. NOVEL LONDON FIRE STATION. Anew fire station, opened on Tuesday, in Grey-coat place! Westminster,, contains features which are not seen in the most complete of previous stations. The appliance room is the special novelty, and should be found to expedite considerably the answering of fire calls. The doors of the stalls for the horses are arranged to open automatically, so that immediately the fire bell rings, and the men slide down the poles, they can, as they descend, pull the cord which opens the door of the stalls. The horses will then run up into position below the automatic swinging harness, and in a second or two will be ready to leave. Messrs. Battersby and Co's hat factory at Stockport, which caught fire on Tuesday evening, was burning all night, and the whole of the warehouse, containing enormous stocks of felt and straw hats, and part of the working rooms were destroyed. The total loss is estimated at £50,000.</p>
